        "text": "<|endoftext|> president.\n\nAdvertisement Continue reading the main story\n\n“I would make that sound like saying yes, I will no longer work for you,” said Mr. Hannity. I responded that I would tell him the story.\n\nHe asked me to promise not to: “As soon as there’s an inquiry under way, come on, come on because I’m about to start making further threats.”\n\nAnd all I did was give him the phone number and told him to call the Washington Post.\n\nNewsletter Sign Up Continue reading the main story Please verify you're not a robot by clicking the box. Invalid email address. Please re-enter. You must select a newsletter to subscribe to. Sign Up You will receive emails containing news content, updates and promotions from The New York Times. You may opt-out at any time. You agree to receive occasional updates and special offers for The New York Times's products and services. Thank you for subscribing. An error has occurred. Please try again later. View all New York Times newsletters.\n\nHe could have stopped him immediately. All I would do — wordlessly — is say “Inside out,” then demand I get on the phone with the White House press secretary and tell him Brady brought it up with his staff.\n\nThe mission, described by one television traveling buddy, is to get news conferences with members of Congress, and they need to be resolved quickly. It’s a little scary what they’ll do.\n\nIf Brady brings it up, I am prepared to say this in private: I won’t say the names of the officials who handled the investigation.\n\nAs for President Obama, he was a failure during his first term, grandstanding too much. He focused on controlling the economy, not manufacturing in his first term.\n\n“It is just a small detail you can point to now, if you want,” he told a group of financiers at the Council of Economic Advisers. I told him I would tell the Wall Street Journal that deficit spending had no bearing on the economy. Obama did not sound uncommon, by then, at districts where deficit reduction was discussed.\n\nPhoto\n\nAs governor, Christine Todd Whitman became the dominant figure of New Jersey politics. She told Iowans who were interested in listening that if you ran for governor, you talked about deficit spending. She even mentioned the Christian Science Monitor during the campaign.\n\nDemocrats around her despised that. They’d always told conservatives, in order to pass Act II of the stimulus package, that the Republican Party could only win a caucus of deficit hawks.\n\nHarvard professor Lawrence Summers, considered as the consensus candidate for the post of Treasury Secretary, belatedly embraced deficit reduction, in an era when candidates were lashing out like bees to the top of the hive.\n\nAdvertisement Continue reading the main story\n\nIn 2008, as loudly as she’d demanded deficit spending, President Bush admitted that before he made the change that he had to see the Journal’s investigation. That’s the same truth that she once tried to capture in a clever 2007 ad by the Christian Science Monitor. It doesn’t matter how solid her current belief is. She doesn’t have a secret motive other than her priorities. It is the same reason she does not look past herself as an only child who was constantly overwhelmed by the way life was.\n\nPhoto\n\nIn Republican circles, Republicans agree with the Journal, that easy spending has a negative effect on the economy and tends to produce deficits. They credit Republicans for imposing strict natural gas production standards.\n\nA lot of Republicans are mad that Obama failed to act against Perry’s impeachment and encourages the investigation. Santorum says GOP officeholders deserved better than that. Republicans seize attention now that we are a week from New Hampshire, and some criticize Romney for playing by the rules of the game.\n\nPaul Ryan is a sellout, but I would wager that if Romney were Palin, Friday’s Boston Globe or New York Times would shoot him down.\n\nSome reporters are still circulating a story that Romney advisers are worried he’ll not admit that the regulator created an illegal financial system. I tell them this is a legal cover. I say what Republicans complain about the media isn’t their only problem. But you have to get the Obama presidential campaign far more certain if they think it is.\n\nOthers who will remember the questioning of Obama by CBS correspondent Dan Rather deliver lines about the president’s sex life, courting controversy.\n\n“And it wasn’t a problem created by the federal government,” Rather complains. “It had been set up. It never existed. You guys got people’s sex lives for free in meetings.”\n\n“Tell me more than that,” After 30"
