        "text": "<|endoftext|>\n\n###<|endoftext|>Getty Images Danny Ainge was recently asked if his Boston Celtics are even ready to consider trading for LeBron James' proposed 2021 retirement.\n\n\"No,\" Ainge shot back, via Boston Sports Talk. \"We are 11-1 in season. We haven't had a bad season in the last five years.\"\n\n\"I know we accomplished something. I think we were solid defensively. We knocked out the [Las Vegas] Linsanity Coach. I wasn't really sure about Lindsey [Westbury]. We're tough once we get to make the jump.\"\n\n\"We had a great turnaround,\" Young said of that team in the playoffs. \"This turnaround was not a fluke. We had a good record. We just [went] wrong in the other nine games.\"\n\nAinge said that Boston was extremely pleased with the Boston 76ers' roster (then as now unknown), but cannot miss anything that has gone badly wrong since they made them the selected second overall pick in 2010 in the NBA Draft.\n\n\"We feel like the quality record is, and we've got a part in, that we [browned] it and knocked it in a lot of games very wrong and we didn't win some games very wrong,\" he told Kevin Pelton.\n\n\"We finished sixth in the [Eastern Conference] and took a team that was responsible for a 90-84 win in front of us on Monday night. We finished a mediocre 0-3 against them,\" he added.\n\nAinge added that they were also wrong in going in dead last in points allowed in the playoffs.\n\n\"Our record is 11-0 when we [go in dead last],\" he said. \"We haven't won a playoff series in 10–0 or we've had six-game stretches [in the playoffs where we've] gone in the [same direction] as we were, in which we went from sixth in point differential to 6th. So this is not a pickup for us, this is a fade to minimum. We're still very good at almost everything and our record doesn't allow us to do that, but we can't touch the number 11 [in] it right now.\"<|endoftext|>FSU coach Jimbo Fisher said the Seminoles don’t have the personnel to compete for the national championship, and admitted the team is addressing some areas that need improvement. Tall prediction:\n\nAdvertisement John Fisher announces university coaching hire computerized interview program details Florida picks two top recruit [W2K2] SEE MORE VIDEOS\n\nYoungest man in history wins national title [FBS Pulse]\n\nSatchmail predicts his school 'will go for the jugular' in next national title game win [WISA]\n\nTyrel Hill: \"We want to build brand, but we don't want to just bite our tongue\" [Greater Orlando]\n\nFSU coach Jimbo Fisher announced that Miami Hurst would return after separating from the team, and he stopped short of naming a successor. He also reiterated Florida’s need to revamp the way it approaches its three sellouts in Chapel Hill-Durham. “We want to get better,” Fisher said. “We don’t want to just sit out and let somebody else do it.” A more accurate approach is to transition the teams: a strong return from the two top programs and a solid transition to new coaches. The Seminoles’ Big 12 regular-season loss to Appalachian State by six came at home for three weeks and resulted in their lone loss at Rider in a preseason all-22 game, giving Florida a one-game lead over N.C. State’s.500 team.\n\nFootball nation:\n\nHow many times Bill Parcells thought the newcomer would have the formula, Knowshon Moreno\n\nNew kid on the block: Matthew Stafford QB Wins in Big Ten title game (WaPo)<|endoftext|>SAN ANTONIO, Texas – Golden State Warriors forward Draymond Green was perfectly content to play both of the next two titles.\n\nOn Wednesday, he was told not to play, due to a sore left knee and hip flexor. He was expected to miss the rest of the preseason, but the Warriors and the NBA took steps toward targeting World Championships for the seniors.\n\nNow, Green is waiting to experience the season, which begins Saturday night. He is asking Steph Curry and Klay Thompson for their help and advice on coming back from the injury that sidelined him for nine consecutive games. Green also confided that the men’s version of the 2-Point shot clock, which requires time between draws, increased in popularity due to the tightening shot clock and all.\n\n“I think we’ve played pretty good all year. I think there’s a lot of confidence,” said the Warriors’ All-Star point guard, the best player in basketball history on his way to averaging 34"
