        "text": "<|endoftext|> East Beijing\n\nfigriment as Georgia 2015 global best and worst travel\n\nvisitors are entitled to have fun\n\nDavid Cameron established the first UK-China grouping of our great three neighbours as momentum for negotiations on a free-trade deal did not die with the £1.8 billion China investment deal announced in late last year.\n\nUK secretary of state for international trade Philip Hammond criticised Beijing after it exceeded expectations for arrivals of 160,000 people last holiday, with the ONS forecasting up to 350,000 more arrivals from Asia-Pacific this year than in past years.\n\nCameron, officially to leave for Japan in October, reassured Chinese premier Wen Jiabao that the UK’s trade agenda would include more business investment in China. China rejoined the World Trade Organisation in a ceremony at the British embassy in the Chinese capital, Beijing, last week.\n\nBut in a Paris summit last month he said: “No single country in the region will shrink from inclusiveness and we will work hard to ensure that the world is led by one government.” German Chancellor Angela Merkel criticised the UK’s use of “pre-conditions” so curious for entirely unrelated reasons.\n\nHe complained that countries complained of “less than optimum treatment” of vulnerable states such as Vietnam, members of the North Atlantic Treaty Organisation (NATO) and Syria.\n\nBruce Johnson, the director of the British Chamber of Commerce’s China and the UK business advisory body, stood down as China minister after Cameron became prime minister without achieving his key long-term pledge: to take in 60,000 migrants each year across the eastern half of Britain.\n\nJohnson's profile had a setback after he was accused of \"slavishly following an explicit US mantra\", making promises that didn't come true.\n\nFor other Chinese business leaders, living in Britain is a strain having been offered half a million pounds each from the government's youth service to switch from China to the UK. China’s General Nationalisation Law (GNU) gives the Ministry of Young People’s Service the authority to take action against foreigners that show “profound cultural, religious or linguistic differences”.\n\nLikening the situation last year to the time he was given that free country handout, Johnson said the government had no choice but to do everything possible to stop illegal migrants arriving illegally from eastern China, his home country.\n\n\"I pay tax on all Chinese arriving anywhere in the world, in any circumstance. There have been terrorist attacks in the UK in particular and very recently maybe the Chinese government has changed its policy. I would certainly respond with what it has done in the past,\" he told the Daily Telegraph. \"I've made it clear to the prime minister that even in the absence of that change of policy the business lobby will complain that I don't want them to invest in and repatriate their Chinese nationals.\"\n\nJohnson said Chinese entrepreneurs had started using crowdfunding to argue that UK companies had done an abysmal job of bringing capital investment back into the UK. Chinese investment was down 37% over five years and was 2% below what it had been 45 years ago, said Chris Patten, a London-based consultant.\n\nJohnson said the focus should be on building Chinese-owned businesses.\n\n\"Now you have commentaries from officials in China. But what we see is Chinese people living side-by-side with, from a mindset of 'how do I get more Britons living here' in London, out there? Really unfair.\"\n\nThe Ofcom panel of immigration experts is due to announce guidelines for dealing with asylum seekers in November.\n\nJohnson said: \"I don't think it is possible for the UK to accept 60,000 British people who are coming voluntarily, bringing with them property values that have been poorly set up. That cannot be hoped to turn Britain into a destination for 90,000 people at Christmas of all people being allowed in, considering all the hardship that there is in these rough seas who come from all over the world to take advantage of the British system.\"\n\nAdam Stokan, the director of the London School of Economics’ China Sustainability Institute, said: \"The Chinese ability to work the biggest – and most affluent – parts of up to 60,000 people out of the country hasn't posed a problem since they were introduced and now more than 1.5 million are coming every year in population growth and migration that continues to increase.\n\n“The only problem is that many are not improving their behaviour and have displayed poor work-life connections, which has made these petty hostiles less interested in coming and more inclined to employ them as workers but just as malicious as when fleeing.\n\n“The first question that needs to be asked is what recovery in social mobility is going to look like for the Chinese. The overwhelming consensus has been that the rate of migration has slowed considerably for them, the"
