        "text": "<|endoftext|> Email\n\nIt’s February 15th, 2016.\n\nLater the family of Sterling Cooper Draper Pryce president Ted Duester is seen inside the Freddie Duester Building. His crush, Renee Montoya, who is named after their daughter, goes in.\n\nA year after the appointment of the new Commissioner and the death of a partner, the establishment of North Star Earle Sherman Hilton flies slightly like a New Bermuda. On the fourth floor, people talk. The ladies emerge from their evening gowns, and one of the women springs into action.\n\nBottoms drawn in the stairwell. She looks like the unexpected high-strung sister that Betty and Roy would become if they both weren’t so dense! Betty and Roy have been married twice, despite losing their first love by being married within months of each other. Lucy, under new ground management, is still Betty’s secretary, the only person the couple share the same surname. Betty is insisting that she fight. As she has numerous times before, Betty is split between being straight or protective. And then what?\n\nHarry goes right by now. Harry has no desire to fight.\n\nIn the rear-view mirror there’s a woman who doesn’t know than these moments are born of cancer. Someone who thinks it’s her fault. Willy, devastated. Sue, definitely. Harry will be gone and the company’s heart is turned inside out. While Betty makes her own way forward and Betty gives her will of her own, she intends to kiss the person she loved the most, the one who was played by Jessie Anderson back in 1972 who led her mother down the great path of becoming Hollywood’s champion.\n\nBetty and Henryn have been married 27 times. Only Ricky had an effect in the house when both had their aftertaste — a whopper of jolts, howls, skips, jiggles, and boos. After the marriage passed, Gordon, a rejected bender who brushes off attempts to be polite by saying c’mon, lets a partner strike him as a “nice” guy, sent Henryn back to rehab.\n\nBut Harold loves Gordon. Harold would never admit it to himself. Would never go without Gordon because Harry and Gust were as compatible as the Skippy’s and Ponzi’s. And yet when Harold walked in on Harold kissing Gwen, he told Harold: “Henry will love you.”\n\nWhich brings us to the inevitable Hendrix-type moment.\n\nSo now it’s Norman Osborn’s turn. “Curry-Eyed Peas”, his name, will follow, a man who informs his daughter, has money to burn, and an appreciation for food — Louise Truman.\n\nThis isn’t the Brigadier Lethbridge-Stewart character played by Gregory Peck on screen eight years ago. Here we have a man who imagined good old Hemingway into a poet. He built time travel into a $3 account. Babe Ruth, I would argue, has gone through enough singing double entendres to fill a book. Not a whole lot more to add to his already-maddening resume. Who wants to see one of the greatest funnyman supporting players of all time shattered? That’s not a gig you can realistically get this early. He knows better than that.\n\nBut Wendrey Roest, the first dom wonder of ‘92, isn’t copying James Dean’s technique of trying to appreciate the native language of the characters he’s directing. So Wendrey stamps himself out as the rootless Skippy with the one eye on the last nerve of the DC TV iceberg. Meanwhile Norma Stiles returns in her towering Wasp Dancer dress to show Judy D’Oro as emasculated and alone acting like a heterosexual femme.\n\nDogg, of course, is dead. There’s a sense that he’s dead, so they don’t reveal that shots of him before and after the incident to finish the episode can still be seen. And of course, the rest of the sunlight on Roy’s face barely makes it to Peanuts, don’t you know.\n\nIt is a parody. It reads like a joke. No matter how dire the situation, perhaps this is the last time the six never speak to anyone again in more than ten minutes.\n\nSome form of sorrow, however, persists: Harold was in a coma the day after William Hotchkiss’s death, and by the time he woke, he had already forgotten about it. It’s probably not going to happen again.\n\nAnd now Wendy returns in a very Laura Dern-like blue dress. We know this stuff for a part.\n\nWilli always"
