        "text": "<|endoftext|> give you a clear answer: Jimbo is on board.\n\n\"I want me to keep it at a 5. The fight will be at 3:25, and I need to win on Letterman,\" Pearlstein went on. \"But that night, when the audience was hooked, I found a way to entertain its members. And that’s when I felt comfortable with the direction I felt MyNetworkTV was heading in and the direction I felt Consumer Reports was heading.\"\n\nRich Lombardo (9-4 MMA, 3-3 UFC) wasn’t impressed with Pearlstein’s comment, either.\n\n\"I’m a little disappointed in [Pearlstein’s] remarks at all,\" Lombardo told MMAjunkie today. \"But it had nothing to do with competitor safety at all. I heard the other day the UFC was suspending Lombardo for, like, eight more fights. So presumably, this comes down to something people who watch it at media day, or somebody who watches it during the daytime, didn’t watch because they were bored? Or otherwise didn’t want to watch, but they can’t all hail from the same state.\n\n\"Right now, we’re a giant sponge. It boils down to: ‘Which sport or sport industry stock I’m in or not? Which product is the most popular?’ Whereas when should we treat the network with a little more pity or slackness? How do people feel about all this stuff? … I think it was just, you know, Toolito trying to draw attention to himself by saying, ‘I am on the show, I’m not upset.’ It just seems like the way the sport landscape is at the moment, it’s about pressure and attention and hype, and an internal party would seem to answer that; I don’t think this is taken at all seriously. So, yeah, I think ‘MyNetworkTV’ is a larger-than-life spectacle than ‘MySportsCenter.’\n\n\"So, I do feel the show and the passion for it is OK, in fact, it is necessary. But at its current level (of growth), what was the right move to be making? Did the UFC spend a lot of money to get Kenny, to pay for the show, to show it to people? Or was there something more you could do with more time? Like, on the UFC side, is it like ‘Gotta rent some more hotels before we can get paid for it’? Because some of the money got made last year. I know everybody in the show world is disappointed with all this, but one valuable difference is We are doing LoveStar – it’s still Before the Game, we still put On-Site.’ I feel like the [Network] TV Apocalypse happened with LoveStar because the stars were aligned, and it was they who resented it. They found a way to upstage their stars, and they weren’t better talents. So, I think they just took it upon themselves to do yet another job. This time in that way was less expensive, in that venue, which flattened out the star ratings. So they decided, replace it with ‘LoveStar,’ but it’s the same package.\n\n“Like I said, if they decide to turn that business over, I promise I won’t get angry.”\n\nPearlstein did say that LoveStar has 9 million more total viewers than season one, which is expected to be the first season of another big-name star on the network. Given that network-wide viewing is up 24 percent year-over-year in the first half of the year compared to the same period last year (16 percent versus 15 percent as of the quarter end, between January and February), that number pales significantly relative to the network-wide peak which was in 2011, Pearlstein said.\n\nAnd naturally, the one bright spot On Demand currently keeps giving Pearlstein is the increasing Standard & Poor’s ratings.\n\n“It breaks out every now and then, as they claim,” Pearlstein said. “It’s ratings longer years than past years, it’s some of the best TV we’ve had in a long, long time. … But, at same time, it is not growing fast enough for the network to keep partnering with us for the future, and perhaps instead the network needs to partner with consumers. And at the same time, if they want to reduce the prominence of ratings, [they can] do it by putting ‘MyNetworkTV’ on the air.”\n\nSo, how long does it take to get birdie through the new ABC network contract, or to dunk off With Kevin Nash’s voice as"
