        "text": "<|endoftext|> British Library.\n\nA RoboRoach delivery turned major.\n\nAdvertisement\n\nA spokeswoman for the Swedish embassy in London said the timing of the incident – the first of its kind – “offered Turkey a golden opportunity to deliver its undivided attention to what it sees as a man-made conflict.”\n\nTurkish Foreign Minister Mevlut Cavusoglu spoke about the incident on Saturday, stressing the importance of creating an atmosphere of ‘impartiality, kindness, compassion and understanding’.\n\n“When people are faced with facts, they are ready to accept facts. Prevention of terrorism does not mean preventing travel,” Cavusoglu stated.<|endoftext|>Former Michigan under-23 goalkeeper Burke Stouffer has been signed by the Browns as head coach.\n\nThe 6-foot-4, 215-pound native of Clevesville, Md. was at practice Tuesday but didn't get a chance to see the field and said he is still awaiting word on his future. The team said it plans to make a decision Wednesday.\n\nStouffer wasn't all that involved Tuesday, appearing in just two games for the Browns. He started one game, going 2-1 overall with a team-high six saves in relief of starter Shimon Jacobs. Officials said he had splint issues throughout the year and ended up signing with the NHL's Dallas Stars on Sunday. The Stars said in a statement last week he was done visiting St. Louis.\n\nUnless asked to return to Michigan, Stouffer will likely be looking to figure out his next level of play. He started all 14 games last season with Chicago, compiling an 8.35 goals-against average with a.924 save percentage and 3-2-1 record. He also went a long way in helping the Bears win the NFL national championship.\n\n\"Probably it was not the best choice, right?\" Stouffer said when asked about his decision Tuesday. \"But I'm pretty sure I would do well out there.\"\n\nHe's only the fourth person in the history of the game from Michigan to become head coach at that school, joining Dale Dooley and Fulton Pierce Haley, who were both on Michigan's active roster in 1959.\n\nThough Stouffer got into the game at Game 3 against the Diamondbacks in the 2006 World Series, there hasn't been a convincing NFL case as to whether he can give the Wolverines any type of ability outside of the net. Michigan ranked last in the league in goals against last year at 3.29 per game, a number that's not sustainable right now.<|endoftext|>The UK Prime Minister has announced Britain will use the WTO to obtain an agreement about a future regime for trade between rich and poor, such as existing one in South-East Asia. The agreement will be in the coming weeks in “massive bilateral conferences” with value-added representatives of 22 countries, conducted by a panel comprising the Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) chief economist.\n\nPrime Minister David Cameron greeted the surprise announcement during a visit to Luxembourg on Tuesday, and later added his voice to the call for increased global trade. “To put it quite simply, this is a return to the old transatlantic partnership that Jean-Claude Juncker put on the table 15 years ago. We're going to make it work – bring substance and rigour back.”\n\nCameron said the talks were to be held during the week of the United Nations General Assembly, in the United Nations Economic and Social Council in New York (US).\n\nBut his move to press ahead with a free-trade agreement comes as the 45-nation EU is facing budget cuts after a new Conservative-led government came to power earlier this year.\n\nCameron’s announcement followed more than a week of criticism from the UN, spearheaded by the International Trade Union Confederation — the U.K.'s main trade union union group.\n\nCameron, however, is highly unlikely to persuade the UK to lead another major trade war with China or European Union countries despite public opposition.\n\n“This is a technical, technical agreement,” Cameron said. “I'm not going to make the argument for it because it wouldn't work out either way. Let's study the question and decide if we want to take an instrument that may turn out to be some good, positive agreement for both us and China. Do we take it?”\n\nHe also rejected calls for Britain to compensate the EU for the fall in sterling after Europe's sovereign debt crisis.\n\n“That would be asking us to put the penny back a little bit so that the rebate could be paid to the nations that both the UK and the eurozone should pay,” he said in an interview.\n\n“No, as the Prime Minister stressed, the question for the debate in Europe is whether we should raise it. That is my view.”\n\nCameron"
