        "text": "<|endoftext|> up with Gardiner, a tierred defenseman in the seventh round in 2011, taken in the fourth round, 61st overall by the Sabres in the 2013 draft.\n\nBrad Boyes completed the cut.\n\nDefensemen: Robin Lehner, Nic Rielly, Rene Bourque, T.J. Brennan, Jourdan Reuchel, Jason Pominville, Ryan Ellis, Anders Lee\n\nGoaltenders: Curtis McElhinney, Brooks Laich\n\nBills open with debutant Connor Brown and now the team moves in with Brian Flynn and his brother, Brett. Flynn played for the Jets last year.\n\nUp front the Sabres commit two players who are forwards. In Leon Draisaitl, they hope return from leg injuries for Jason Pominville. Taku Tarnowski, who may be their second-leading scorer in 2016-17, arrives late in the draft.\n\nRugman and Raanta add draft picks.\n\nThe Sabres will play at the renovated Pepsi Center in South Florida outside of Buffalo, N.Y., in the second of two preseason training camp periods.<|endoftext|>Former Florida Senator Rick Santorum accepted a $28,000 award from the Blue Nation Foundation last fall that he owned that year. Perhaps wisely, he is secretive about his benefactors and what they do for their own bottom lines.\n\nSantorum’s charitable giving has gone up 67 percent since at least 2002, to nearly $8.5 million in 2015. He was paid more than $1 million to help promote his successor, Minnesota Rep. Michele Bachmann, at a conference in Bing Crosby’s golfers club in Pennsylvania over the summer.The speaker’s list of guests the day before the charity’s announcement was no more than 1,500 donors.\n\nSantorum, a former neurosurgeon, has never received an after-school baseball or football scholarship, according to a Center for Public Integrity analysis of research from the Campaign Legal Center, a nonprofit nonpartisan watchdog group. He’s also neither a teacher nor a physician, and he’s made poor judgment decisions over his varied career.\n\nSantorum was one of his brother’s chums for almost a decade. For years he confided in a friend that one day he was looking to disassociate himself personally from his relationship with Mark Tabata, a Pittsburgh professor who was also a consultant to Michele Bachmann’s campaign. Santorum had allegedly tried unsuccessfully to get Tabata to end the anonymous relationship.\n\nPlenty of people who know Santorum in Washington find it hard to believe that his salary on a recent $2 million salary conference call is peanuts. But speaking with CNNMoney, Santorum suggested the difference was only a theoretical one. He said he’s met with Tabata’s former wife three times over the years. His money went while his wife was out of town on fundraising trips with Bachmann on another trip.\n\nSantorum and Tabata remain close, but not at a state level. They work almost independently in their spare time and have never talked about claiming brotherhood benefits.\n\nSantorum and Bachmann each announced last month that they’d donate what they found for Dreamworker, a worker’s compensation program activated earlier this year to help consumers work two jobs both at home and on the road. Bachmann and Santorum are members of the board that oversees the company.\n\nSantorum was the 2008 Republican vice presidential nominee. His robocall, in which he falsely suggested he’d changed his mind on net neutrality and headlines about illegal immigration lyrics; performed at an anti-immigration rally, ended in a pep talk for dark-skinned protesters and a shout “POLITICISM IS, ON OUR WALL – WE CATER MORE THAN EACH OTHER!”; and called America “the world’s most dangerous nation,” all tour stops since becoming a candidate in 2012, contributed to thousands of dollars to both candidates’ 2013 and 2014 re-election campaigns. Before becoming speaker of the House, the longtime speaker helped draft Bachmann’s husband’s book, Targeted Victory.\n\nThough Santorum was never officially indicted for any wrongdoing in either cases, the revelation that he was at one point awakened to possible criminal activity by an informant raises further questions about appearances to the Capitol Police and what security duties might have been created by his entanglements.\n\nSantorum may still be exempt from FBI and federal bribery reporting, according to a watchdog group called Open Secrets. But the Trump Organization now may be included on the FBI’s list of Russian targets for asset freezes and asset freezes, barring new revelations. The Trump Organization has been under federal scrutiny for spending more than $6 million raising money for President Trump’s presidential campaign.\n\nThe Boston Globe’s investigation of Canada’s<|endoftext|>"
