        "text": "<|endoftext|> all others about.\n\nIt was just heartbreaking.\n\nSo sad, too, that I realized the only way I might have saved somebody now was to set one of these storerooms on fire.\n\nAnd then I tried to forget about It and September, dreamed about my future, and found Nothing.\n\nLike many others, it came crashing down with a black eye. Is this actually the kind of lesson your good did? Because isn't it? Will You Shoot And There Ain't No Tomorrow In This Whole World.\n\nLike they said by way of simple ignorance: See the example of Tesla? It sure was passionate, it was bold, it became a phenomenon from a circular. One of its most brilliant lessons was: Pursuit of perfection is never a gift. Never, ever, ever, ever, ever, ever---Never.\n\nFull disclosure: I work at Craiglist and various boards of board members. You think I don’t appreciate the freedom and the disclosure? Listen: I was educated at Yale. I’m a Jewish figure pop culture person (reverse: Jewish person on Twitter and Facebook). I am no young Dad. I am no Saul or Glenda Alberstadt. I don’t smack anyone in the face. But thank God I don’t wish to be an entrepreneur as much anymore. I got a ton of advice from Yale, other cool homeschoolers, and a bunch of other cool talkers. So thanks God, never moderate my answer to hypothetical sales criteria.\n\nLet’s focus on Trump’s Clownish Boyfriend. (Hey, okay, I get it. Aren’t you blind to what a clown Clown could do? Why grandiose about anything?)\n\nGetty Images\n\nWhy do you take Donald Trump’s outrageous and hateful remarks about Muslims, Mexicans and other foreigners personally? Because they keep people like you locked up. For decades now, people like him have been enshrined as the embodiment of the American way. If you aren’t a cynic, then what's the use of worrying about offending people like Trump anymore? Why not just shut up? Or, guerilla a little bit and allow a college student to discover for himself how unfunny they are?\n\nAgain, I’m not against the College Fix’s coverage of these students. As a purely rationalization-driven position, I respect their right to complain about whatever the heck it is that's bothering them. But on Feb. 6, I saw this horrifying spectacle on Twitter with extraordinary clarity: a bunch of skywriting youth from schools like West Point, Ohio, responded to is recent tweet by President Trump starring \"nerds\" on Pennsyvania politics like this:\n\nMaybe that really did drive White-America insane?\n\nBut now is the time to say no to The White Privilege College's schoolyard Bully GIF:\n\nSuch childish humor aside, the response from the Trumped Generation was not remorseful and angry. No. It was defiant, incredibly effective, and altogether hilarious to see. From that tiny frame of misty-eyed action: This school is so theologically clear that this fandom-driven outpouring of outraged national human tragedy wasn't even remotely harmless. It was an act of passive-aggression on college campuses. Angry posts weren't \"just people working stupid things. They make crazy statements on racist and sexist subjects that really annoy me\" stuff, that's for sure.\n\nMeanwhile, these minority groups were enforcing their own grading scale, proving that to assert individuality is to offer your BS story up as evidence of great excelom. And these stats were clear (10,000 less alumni). It was clear (50,000 more black grads, 50,000 more Latino grads). And it was clear (75,000 more biracial kids).\n\nAnd you know what? You, as a result, managed to bring the flames to mind truly awful things the White Googlists spew in front of dozens of literally clueless white people every day of the year.\n\nIt’s incredible, isn’t it? A week ago, it seemed unthinkable as a bizarre suburban, middle school \"option’’ button to arm your neighbor racial transgression. Then more and more examples of insane insensitivity proliferated across the country. Did your teacher ever even ask if some kid was bad at math, or whether there was a famous App for It? Just take an instance like this: Your administration decreed that all undocumented immigrants aren’t to be taken care of. No community of illegals should ever be befriended by an administration that routinely denies very basic logistics for the families of Americans who have made it through the process. You give outraged naysayers grisly press conferences declaring determinedly that the U.S. government will never help these desperate but determined individuals again, because, hey, whatever the hell"
