        "text": "<|endoftext|> no fruitful and short-term projects.\n\nAll this adds up to one thing: Trump is a bad man and having his thoughts and opinions drilled into the minds of millions of Americans is bad business.\n\nTrump has become the advert of a man who never really made it a Cabinet, Senate, or Supreme Court appointment, either. He hasn’t even dared advance a specific agenda for the presidency. He has said everything from a reset embargo to dismantle the Trans-Pacific Partnership to push for a religious test for our immigration system. He hasn’t actually done anything to return the United States to, or at least be a U.S. ally.\n\nAnd that matters less if we, as a nation, are fundamentally divided by class prejudice. Persuading all of us to fight to remain in a failed status quo is practically impossible. Today America is an open and honest country, and the notion of “the people” acting against their “corporate stranglehold” is laughable.\n\nPhoto credit: Flickr/Adelenei.ir<|endoftext|>One of his top advisers has been indicted on Wednesday over allegations of trying to obstruct justice and taint the Democratic National Committee with politically damaging emails.\n\nRobert Creamer, who helps run Democracy Partners, has been charged with three counts of unauthorized removal and retention of records from the DNC, Department of Justice officials confirmed to Politico. He submitted evidence of his own to the FBI and the Justice Department already, and the agency is investigating his credibility, they said.\n\nCreamer has operated under the alias “Team Decider” in a series of online chats, which trace over a period before Nov. 19, 2014, when Democratic National Committee Chairman Tom Perez sent an e-mail to Sanders supporters urging them to follow the lead of Perez in backing Clinton.\n\nAfter the e-mails were leaked, a few people behind Clinton’s digital operation were fired.\n\nTeam Decider had long pushed for changes to the DNC rules, including elimination of superdelegates, giving them only one-third of the remaining delegates pledged and helping to make the party-building process more competitive.\n\nNow prosecutors are preparing to indict Creamer, according to The Chicago Tribune. Creamer isn’t been charged but his case was initially reported, but prosecutors on Wednesday transferred the case to the same bureau that investigated Trump’s transition team for hush money in connection to the Russian government.\n\nA corruption statute hasn’t been filed in national politics, but Crook, the law professor, questioned why federal prosecutors had taken this action after previously acting as chairman of the secretive Congressional Black Caucus Foundation Political Action Committee.\n\n“Despite all this evidence and all the best inflammatory rhetoric of Trump, Sessions, etc., no one had brought allegations for the DNC to do anything on its part to damage Clinton or the DNC in this vote, and right now, with all the Bill, all the Harry Reids and Waxman’s we’re not talking about that much,” Crook said.\n\nThe court had not stated its intention to announce the charges but multiple law enforcement, congressional and news reports said the charges could limit Creamer’s ability to work as an independent counsel in addition to stopping him from two of his top contacts from working in government.\n\n–ABC News\n\nImage credit: Reuters<|endoftext|>In addition to the reform of expunging records of unconstitutional spying, Senator Ron Wyden recently led a group of close to 40 U.S. lawmakers to demand that data agencies and the NSA produce records of any call records or electronic correspondence within 20 days.\n\nCurrently, Americans are mandated to hand over all phone records within a tracking time-frame of 20 days if they believe calls or data are related to a law violation, but Wyden’s legislation would require the data released to U.S. intelligence agencies to be queried 8 to 10 days in advance if the callers were suspected of a terrorism related crime.\n\n“Senator Wyden’s legislation ultimately would require that the PRISM investigation is conducted with oversight from the House Oversight Committee,” Wyden said in an on-the-record statement on Tuesday. “Unfortunately, these encryption practices have contributed to this to further entrench the type of mass surveillance programs that have been alleged.”\n\nWhen paragraph 2 of Wyden’s bill was introduced, he requested details from the NSA, including where the PRISM investigation was taking place.\n\nIn a letter he sent to the NSA that is currently being reviewed by Senator Dianne Feinstein, Wyden and Senator Ron Wyden of Utah asked for all ongoing USA Freedom Act efforts to be examined. NSA documents provided to the congresswoman by whistleblower Edward Snowden revealed that the NSA was collecting “even as many as 215, records of every one to six unauthorised telephone calls made outside the United States.”\n\nIn that"
