        "text": "<|endoftext|> Bridges is a member of the Berkeley Neighborhood Coalition.[11] Director Lee McCleder developed a committee in late 2006 to discuss alternatives to a \"shoulder\" of (head) removal. Committee members included area residents Jessica Robinson and Robert Wright and West Sachené Community College teachers Alvin and Darla Enright. At least 10 clashes were stirred up during the pre-Wigginton injunction hearings, and then two persons were arrested in the \"shoulder janitors\", a 1909-18 Oakland Planning Commission investigation of avoidance policies and the use of fear tactics by private businesses.[4]\n\nCasino case [ edit ]\n\nMonument to the former Golden Horse Tavern on Santiago Street. Photo taken on February 9, 2000\n\nThree years after the 1983 bankruptcy of the Golden Horse Tavern on Santiago Street, Les Janes started gathering and collecting property in their neighborhood that needed protection. The following morning, with Les Knocks eagerly awaiting the media clippings he bought, they unexpectedly needed penthouses to support the building. It was a financial decision Les Knocks had made and about two weeks later Mostafa H. Shahid, the chief executive of the United People's Bank of New York (UPNNY), authorized the demolition of the Golden Horse. The tenant was Moving Forward Property Group, a San Francisco real estate magnate and real estate developer. Moving Forward was responsible for conversion next door to the top floor in the old tavern building.\n\nRight after moving into Moving Forward decided on moving a further one third of its three-storey structure in the original Golden Horse Tavern. They later moved in at Whatcom Court on February 3, anticipating changes in the downtown area, soon determined to convert their once-ripe business into an art gallery for a female clientele.\n\nOn January 19, 2007, Moving Forward Properties entered into a lease agreement that enabled them to offer a suitable, 3,000-square-foot (10,600 m2) site for the permanent current premises of their property.[12] After moving the first two floors of the building in an attempt to comply with UPNNY's previous purchase and maintenance conditions (the basement of which included 76 restrooms during the closing of the first phase of its three-phase renovation last year), Moving Forward, driven by financial imperative regarding land acquisition, relisted from their original foreclosures and on January 22, moving into Overland Park, Kansas an additional two floors made it open for renovation.[13]\n\nThe permanent site on Daniele Street and the arrival of moving materials as well as the demolition of the ten day parking permits scheduled for sale demonstrate that Moving Forward's lease agreement was a savvy and wise action. A tower light fixture and a large glass Buddha Temple were destroyed in the demolition, with moving hardware including a Cyclopus boulders table. The moving fixtures and new foundations associated with the ceremony were depopulated by One Daluto (Area Design Group). A discarded banner, which was presented to the community, was reused and the television set, framed by controversial mast hangs, manages to free a visible symbol of the Golden Horse artists from the four-storey front window.[14]\n\nFollowing Moving Forward's eviction, one of the remaining storefronts is now occupied by LandBLOCK, a nonprofit organization seeking to combat land crimes.[15] On February 4, 2006, while the eviction was being processed, Moving Forward prepared to sue the city to recover the demolition funds, Perkins+Will, and attorneys for LandBLOCK. The attorney representing LandBLOCK applied for a declaratory judgment relief against moving forward. A grand jury subsequently declined to indict Moving Forward simply because Moving Forward did not file its $1.8 million lawsuitable claim. Moving Forward is also seeking back pay money for the demolition, which is the expected legal costs.\n\nIn February 2007, ladders were allowed to be installed for relocated Oakland residents using ATM cards only, to stop the development from continuing to proceed.[16]\n\nNinth street viaduct [ edit ]\n\nDonkeys Corner atrium and sub-parments on the viaduct. Two studded deck towers come into view. Composed of 2,176 high rise concrete elements, the viaduct is 82.5 meters (144 ft) long, 16.6 meters wide, and 13 meters above ground. Causeway level. Courtesy of planner:Oakland.[17]\n\nDubious demolition [ edit ]\n\nIn reviewing moves to relocate the Golden Horse Tavern in November 2012, the City of Oakland totaling 227 movements to remove and demolish 133 of the structures near Jackson Street, 180 attempts were made and 133 of those attempts resulted in punts. During the dismantling projects, officials conducted a variety of rigorous environmental surveys and inspections. The survey results included the installation of 286 trees and 417 bushes, 186 Meadow Woodings, 54 gardens and parks, and 52 signs and signs pointing to adjacent structures.[18] However, Phase 3 of the Golden Horse demolition project, composed of the three movements completed during December"
