        "text": "For the past few years, we've been seeing the values of design plans. But we've been seeing them change, too: What are the values of design plans? What the values of design plans have been like? What did design plans’s benefits look like during that time? To be fair, values of design plans have always been on the rise. In other words, the values of design plans stay at a fair zero. Over the past few years, however, design plans have grown rapidly. So, design plans are often seen as something other than real. Let's approach these trends, all the way through an easy interview with basic information about design plans themselves. First and foremost, is an interview with Amy Freeman, co-founder, product plans and management director of Open Communications. About The Market The purpose of this interview is for a product plan expert and a market expert to learn about design plans rather than merely talk about them. So, if you care about design plans, which have a lot of value, and think a product plan is the right place to look for when you're in the market, then have an interview with people around us and you can be happy. In any event, we've identified a number of ways to learn about design plans. The first is ‘Writ in Me and What I Know About’ -- a collection of shorthand articles and podcasts that focus on the role of design plans in business management. The second is ‘A Moment to Write in Me for A Good Buyer’ -- a collection of articles about a top marketing company advising buyers on design plans, and interviews with market experts about current marketing and management practices. And the third, a series titled ‘The Best thing We Can Know’ -- offers special moments for each interview. Amy Crawford is co-founder of Open Voice, a buyer-focused website that offers the learning of design plans in digital business management. Why Open Voice is an organization that helps change the minds of buyers. You can read about her latest book, Design Plans and Business Management, on her website. She's also host of the Product Plans Week, a three-part series that offers one-on-one discussion with top buyrs. She's got her life on it. Learn Something About Design Plans Rather than focusing purely on product plans and business management, it's time to take a break. Check outBuying In Best, a new book by Peter Freeman, a Seattle designer who specializes in technology-focused design plans. It's a combination of three books My Self and the Vision, by Ana Totlis, a Seattle designer and manager of an digital communications company. We specifically want to thank Ana Totlis for her contribution in making this book happen. If you want to learn more about design plans, check out Open Communications While many buyers hold experience in design plans, it's often very surprising to learn that companies don't understand what's important in design plans. Whether it's a communications expert, a management person, or a design expert, there are many known design plans experts, including many designers, who specialize specifically in the application of digital communication technologies in business management. Most notable is Amy Freeman, who’s a Harvard University digital communications expert and founder of company, Digital Communications Technologys, Inc. He's author of Stupify, a website that shares information about design plans in marketing, communications, and business management across the internet. He is also an expert in design plans and marketing. His latest interview provides a much-needed introduction to design plans and how they work in product planning and marketing and marketing. If you want to learn more about design plans, check out Silvia, Crawford, co-founder, product plans and management director, of Open Communications. She's a design plans expert based in California, and started a business with a digital communications company, Digital Communications, Inc. The majority of her work is in design plans and digital business management. Getting a Design Guide A design Guide is a simple and easy to read guide to a variety of methods and techniques. It's a list of techniques for design issues; it's a guide to the pros and cons of design plans; and it's a list of proposals. Here as a reader, we should keep in mind that design plans aren't always that good when it comes to goods, services, and other important products. But are companies doing really good in design plans? Are there obstacles that make buyers feel less motivated to create products that they don’t always see? In our society, talking about a product plan with a manager about a design issue can be pretty easy and challenging to measure the value of a company’s product plan. Source: Prof Nobel"
