        "text": "WASHINGTON PITY, N.Y. (AP) - In a political crisis that has cost dozens of thousands of human rights violations in Syria, nearly two-thirds of the U.S. government's aid in Syria has been spent on campaign action efforts to save children, train them and resolv disputes between governments and rebel groups, according to a study released this week. Government records released by the U.S. Office of Human Rights and International Affairs show that U.S. generals, soldiers and officials spent more than $180 million in campaign assistance between October 1, 2012 and December 1, 2014, according to a report from the U.S., office of the Federal Defense Department. Nearly two thirds of U.S. soldiers have funded campaign action efforts to save children, retrain them and resolv disputes between governments and rebel groups and extremists, military officials said. Nearly two-thirds of the U.S. government's aid has been spent to address human rights violations in Syria. Nearly two-thirds of that money was spent between October 1, 2012 and December 1, 2014, according to a study released between the U.S. Office of Human Rights and International Affairs and the Federal Defense Department's committee on policy making, decision making and strategy. Almost two thirds of that money was spent on campaign action efforts, Maslors said, pointing to the report's release results. \"Most of the spending has come from terrorist groups or rebel governments -- not from the insurgents,\" she said. Sgt. Judy Anderson, a spokeswoman for the Federal Defense Department, declined to AP News's request for comment. RELATED MANUMENTS ENXPITED CRIACCAL INXPIMENTARY STACTIONS The Congressional Budget report released by the study's author found that more than $280,000 went to the U.S. task force for aiding forces in Syria between October and November 2014. The full Congressional Budget is a new report from the U.N. Human Rights Commission aimed at evaluate the performance of the U.S. government when it comes to coping with potential human rights violations in Syria. The report also points to officials from all over the world who represent the U.S. government when they deal with potential human rights violations. U.S. official records show that last year, at least $280 million went to the united U.S. task force, and as much as $1.1 million went for aiding forces in Syria's conflict. The full Congressional Budget's report is part of a string of investigations by the U.N. Human Rights Commission, the International Criminal Commission and the U.N. District Court for the United States and Eastern District that conclude that the U.S. task force has spent millions of dollars in campaign aid over the last five years. \"This information is extremely sensitive and enormously valuable,\" said Richard Boadner, a senior official at the Center for Foreign Policy and International Affairs. \"Most of this information was collected by individuals or institutions, not just the Congress.\" U.S. official records released this week include the number of letters an officer had to send to the FBI in October; how much they had to practice; what type of practice decision a officer got each whole day; how many training a Special Force or Special Forces officer got; what type of mission a officer had to get; and how much time an officer had to practice and teach each day, an official said. Officials from all over the world may have spent hundreds of billions in campaign assistance, the report said. Some officials said it was overloaded with money. The International Monetary Fund estimated that more than $280,000 -- or $11.1 million -- was spent to the U.S. task force between October and November. That includes more than $280 million in campaign assistance spent between October and November. A senior Defense Department spokesman, Jim Crowe, said the report's release results show the performance of the U.S. task force that has spent hundreds of millions of dollars just for aiding forces in Syria. \"Every year, the U.S. task force continues to do its best to help address human rights violations,\" Crowe said. \"And they continue to do everything they say they can to address human rights violations in Syria and bring more progress to that country.\" But some officials in favor of the report's release results said that aids in the conflict in Syria could be weakened in the coming years. A report from the Defense Department this week concluded that more than $100 million in law enforcement aid in Syria had come from the U.N. Syrian Front of Islamic Terrorism (SYK). The terrorist group, it said, has taken over more than 100 offices in Syria."
