        "text": "Trump later targeted at the two leaders and went on a London-based trip noting that Russia “would expand its relations with the world” and that China “would expand its relations between 2012 and 2020,” according to NBC News’ Matt Lewcey also noting that Russia “would expand its relations with the world (between 2012 and 2020). Following his speech, Trump offered a sign that he planned to meet in London with U.S. Secretary of State John Pancei. The two discussed as a sign of a potential trade agreement, while the pair also discussed a host of issues with China and countries such as Ukraine, Ukraine and Syria. Pancei-Pikersen did not immediately return a message seeking comment. But when the former administration official asked about the prospect of improvements in relations between the U.S. and the UK, Trump didn’t answer the question, offering it as a sign of a potential trade agreement. Trump’s comments were a significant addition to his earlier speech at the White House in which he mentioned a controversial 2021 treaty that allows US tech companies to sell products around the world using the basis of technology in China. In addition, the 2021 treaty reclaims strict trade protections that mean the U.S. sells all products and services to China, producing $1.8 trillion worth of revenue each year. Speaking about potential improvements in relations between the U.S., the UK and China, Trump also called for the signing of an entirely new trade treaty to deepen commercial relations. “The U.S. government continues to play a leading role in bringing tech companies together around the world,” said Gorvin Richards Jr., senior policy officer at AT&T’s Greenberg Institute for Tech Policy, Communications and Innovation. “It plays an important role in keeping tech companies safe and stable.” Include to Trump’s comments is a new climate cap, a cross-border energy benefit that would allow the United States to increase its electricity capacity and financially support the countries that build, export and use tech technologies globally. The climate cap allows for short-term investment in renewable energy and resources such as clean air, as well as global investment in innovation technologies such as robots and artificial intelligence. Trump’s comments also marks the beginning of a deepening fist at various levels of the administration. The White House marked a step in the process toward formally signing a climate change trade treaty, with the most important being a fully signing new trade treaty that would allow the United States to continue to expand commercial relations. Pophonically, the president also said that he expects the treaty will help to deepen his administration’s agenda. While the comments do indicate that Trump is favoring a “long-term, stable trade agreement,” the comments implyt a much more broad-term agenda that he hasn’t been happy with. Image copyright 2014 Getty Images Image caption The United Nations has launched an air strike campaign in Syria against ISIL 2:45 AM The International Office of the Human Rights Watch (HHS) says the United Nations launched an air strike campaign against ISIL in Syria to honor Syrian President Afmar al-Salfi for organising and preparing to support a suspected terrorist organization in the Syrian government. The United Nations has said the United Nations has taken a \"full step\" against ISIL. The honor was paid during an emergency service by the Human Rights of the U.N. Human Rights Watch (HHS). They're a tribute to Syrian President President Bashar al-Assad, who has been heading the ongoing conflict between his government and suspected Islamist groups, which have demolished a U.N.-led base in Syria since the start of last year. \"It's that (the United Nations) has taken a ‘full step' to overthrow some of Assad's rise,\" senior UN official Lt. Col. Kevin Schuler told ABC's Matt Alive. \"What we are doing is we are working with ISIL and Assad to overturn the route that this Washington-backed terrorist organization is able to take.\" The honors come as the U.N.-led coalition has launched an air strike against ISIL, a suspected jihadist group that has been fighting to escalt violence in Syria for months. The United Nations has repeatedly called for further military action against ISIL. On Wednesday, Germany's German Prime Minister Angela Merkel hailed Afmar al-Salfi for organising and supporting a terrorist organization in the Syrian government, adding that he was working with the U.N. coalition to strike a deal to demolish a base in Syria to support ISIL's rise."
