        "sourceId": 638,
        "text": "Jean-Marie McHillary, executive director of the International Federation for Childrens, the international women’s rights organization, said that Britain had some level of concern about Russia and the Syrian government, adding that there were significant differences in concern between British countries. UK Foreign Minister Julia Butler, executive director of the International Federation for Childrens, said Britain may not be aware of Russia’ involvement in Syria. “Right now, Britain has little concern about Russia’ involvement, but there is not a diplomatic agreement between British countries in the situation in Russia and Syria,” she said, adding that they were not trying to expand their relations with Russia and the Syrian government. The foreign minister noted that “the potential for a solution between the Russian government and Syrian government has yet to be determined. The diplomatic agreement between Russia and Britain is ongoing,” she said. Russia and Syria have exploded into controversy in recent weeks as the state-held Kremlin authority put forward a travel ban on foreign detainees to the country. UK Foreign Minister John Wheeler said Britain may not be aware of Russia’s involvement in Syria. Russian Foreign Minister Nidi Movasky said the situation in Syria is the latest in a series of conflicts between the government and the Kurdish leader Muharoud Assad in Syria. “It is urgent that Britain immediately address the Syrian conflict and immediately recognize Russia’s involvement in the situation in Syria,” Movasky said. Britain is scheduled to participate in talks with the Syrian government and the Russian government and to participate in development efforts in Syria, he said. The talks are scheduled to start next Tuesday before Britain can make a decision on how they will be handled. International Anti-Terror The events of a potential conflict between Syria and Russia continue to affect Britain in many ways, and the situation could be worsening if limits are met, said Sergeo Weaver, general director of the International Anti-Terror Organization. “When you discuss terrorist groups in Syria, it’s a combination of racism, terror, violence and fear when you discuss Syrian leader Muharoud Assad,” he said. SAN VIDEO (CBS) — The mother of four did apply for a visa to move to the Ontario Monday morning but said she didn’t think it would do her any better. After getting her child’s visa, Mrs. Hellford went to her financial store and picked up all of her child’s money. “I talked to a friend of mine who was very scared and told him I should follow him because he was very scared,” said Mr’s mother, Willwin Hellford. Mr. Willford said she went back to her financial store and found out her husband had stolen her child’s money. Her husband then went back to a financial store and told her to follow him because all of her money had been stolen. Mrs. Hellford explained that she had to be able to provide her child with a visa from a for-profit nonprofit service she had sold for $250 since the beginning of the year. She said her husband never got a visa, but he decided she should take it because he was scared. “He didn’t feel good enough because he didn’t feel good enough to change his mind,” said Mrs. Hellford. “He didn’t feel good.” Mrs. Hellford said she worried that her husband came to the financial store and picked up all of her child’s money. “She’s actually a mom with her money and she wanted the mom’s to follow me. I’m scared about that because I got my visa and she wanted the mom’s visa to return all of her money.” Mr. Hellford said he was “misused” at her’s decision to apply to move in Ontario. “I don’t want anyone else to get my visa back so I can move there,” he said. “She’s a mom with her money and she wants the mom’s visa to follow me. When I get my visa I’m not going to follow her because I’m scared about that.” With summer swamped with snow and rain, many Syrian refugee candidates are heading for Europe this spring. European officials have started to increase the number of Syrian refugee candidates who won't be heading to Europe starting next spring. Latest research from the European Council estimates that 66 percent of 2,031 Syrian refugee candidates will head to Europe this spring simply because as a result of warmer weather. The agency estimates that about 60 percent of candidates will be heading for Europe starting next spring simply because of the increasing demand for Syrian settlers. The research is in partnership with the European Council of Refugee Candidates, a non-profit organization that helps Syrian refugee candidates find the best possible way to stay in Europe."
