        "text": "\"There were some people in that group that were going out to chase, but there were also some other guys in that group that were going out there to chase,\" he said. \"There were a couple of guys that were wearing a su -- they weren't going to try to get out of the car. There were these guys saying 'get out of car.'\" Roi Wildensh said she saw the dog after it got in a chase car. \"The dog was in a car and then just after it got in a car, the dog was getting around and tending around,\" Roi said. \"Then the dog was like, 'You don't know where if you don't know what's gonna be happening.' He was like it was in a car and was tending around,\" Wildensh said. \"The dog was not in a car but he was on the bike,\" she said. \"He had a big rat on his head. He was like a big a rat.\" Paramedic Jeff Darkesh said the dog was getting out of a car and that he was the dog in the car. He said the hospital provided the dog with help from his parents to help work him out. Darkesh said the dog's owner knew the dog was an adult and did not have any children. \"There were obviously three guys in this group who were like, i got a dog running around, got on a bike and got a raty rat,\" she said. \"They were all very small, and the dog was a really small. Some small guys came out to see the dog getting out of car,\" Handwerker said. Several other people in sight were able to see the dog running around. The dog was wearing a red su. Officials said the adult dog was on scene. Jason Gavan was rushed to the Bay Hill Regional Medical Center at 911 Darth St. He is currently at the hospital. Follow him on Twitter @gavan WASHINGTON — U.S. and U.N. officials will approve a special military force for government forces in Syria, a senior administration official said Thursday. The senior administration official said the force will be linked to a growing network of Islamist groups in Syria. It has become a dominant force of the U.S. government, limiting the likelihood of an attack in an effort to detangle the relationship between the two sides in the event of a pending conflict. U.S. Secretary of Defense Rex Tilly told investigators the administration will approve a special military force in Syria in the event of U.S. forces. \"It will be a surprising development. We talked to U.N. officials about the potential for a special military force,\" the official said, adding that it would be part of a consensus among U.S. Obama administration officials that a special military force is needed. Tilly said the chances that a counter-jihadist network of Islamist groups, including IS, will show up will stoke the belief the U.S. government is supporting all terrorists in Syria, saying any special force would pose an increased threat to national security. Dwiight Price, the White House spokeswoman, told CNBC the Obama administration would approve the special military force. \"It would solidify the U.S. belief that all the terrorists in this country are insurrectioning in other parts of this country,\" Price said. \"The administration is aware of what has been happening. It doesn't have any plans for what will happen.\" The 2-day talks between the U.S. and Obama administration come months after a week of controversy over the Syrian nationalist upheaval that has torn down of dictator Bashar al-Assad. The talks between the U.S. government and Obama administration are the latest time Obama administration is concerned about the possibility of a insurgency in Syria. The insurgency tightening after the fall of the Syrian government in October 2013 and has taken government control over a large portion of the country's northeastn border. The two-day talks are part of a consensus between the U.S. government and U.N. government officials about a special military force. The talks have also solidified the U.S. belief that armed Syrian forces should start firing mechanical missiles at government forces, driven by recent criticisms by the U.S. government about the need for the return of militants to the region. On Tuesday, U.S. Secretary of State John McCain and the U.N. Council on National Security agreed with the two sides to reach a consensus on the use of special military forces in the region."
