        "id": "s-0fb6cefbbb3e76bf92eec162",
        "sourceId": 844,
        "text": "Tulkshau furthered this news by announcing the release of Unmatch-the-Lisse, a type of role-playing game that allows players to enter a new virtual reality universe, and live their lives in real time. Unmatch-the-Lisse will be joined by many new titles from the game universe, including Superman, Supersman, The King of Stone, New Guan, and Nintendo’s “Sweels” Skycraters. The game exploits the new virtual reality to allow players to play with their rivals in real time. The game has the same basic features as Unmatch-the-Lisse: players are able to find units in a target zone, stand by their opponent’s forces, and send weapons to the target zone. Unfortunately, it’s not clear how long the game will last or how long the game will take before more units become available, including more weapons. U.S. Secretary of State Hillary Clinton made comments Tuesday at a Democratic campaign convention in Washington, D.C., a late morning speech added to a president-dominated conversation about relations with each other, including Paris and tightening the U.S. sanctions. \"Those are the things I'm excited to do. As president, what I'm looking forward is expand relations with the world leaders,\" Clinton said. \"As president, this is what I'm doing. I really want to do it because what I want to do is stand with the world leaders and talk with each other about how they're governing the region.\" Her comments came at the end of the cycle of conversations between Democratic candidates, supporters of the new president-elect, and his administration that included tightening the Paris sanctions, expanding U.S. imports to Europe and appointing ambassadors to the South Asia region. \"As president, I want to say that these are things I'm really excited to do. I'm looking forward to doing,\" she said, before U.S. Secretary of State John John said that it was important to have a strong relationship with Russia to protect its security and keep it safe in the region. \" As president, I'm looking forward to what the two parties are doing, how they're working together. I am looking forward to what they're really doing. I'm looking forward to communicating with both of them and working together in a way that is very important to both parties.\" Democratic Secretary of State Hillary Clinton as she met Russian President Tayyip Erdogan. (AP Photo/Kenry Martin/Getty) The opening conversations were part of a two-year partnership between the president and Clinton that would expand relations between both parties. \"It's just a great thing to do,\" Mr. Clinton said during a late morning speech at a Democratic campaign convention in Washington, D.C., adding that he wants to have a more strong relationship with Russia. \"Those are the things I'm really excited to do,\" he said. \"There is support from both parties, the government, from both parties. This is something I think very important. And I think the important thing is that we will work together with Russia on more security issues that I think are very important to the region.\" The conversations are part of a wider national security agreement, which is to be signed by Secretary of State John Manafor, the chairman of the White House's security team. Clinton is also expected to speak more closely with U.S. Secretary of State John Pence, the chairman of the White House security team, and said she hopes to continue to work with the two parties on renegotiations. \"We have a detailed process, relating to renegotiations, and we'll work together as much as possible to help achieve such a agreement,\" Mr. Clinton said. \"If the conversations come to an end, I think we'll do everything we can to reach that end.\" The Democratic nominee signaled that Russia's actions are very important to the security of the eurozone. \"We will need to work together to find ways to stop this continuing abuske, and to bring away the weaknesses of our powerful eurozone,\" he said as she opened his first ever conversation about Russia's U.S. sanctions and hacking activities. EL GERIA — Europe's euro zone has come to a close as Russian President Jacques Macroni applaudd U.S. ambassador John John to visit Moscow on security issues. Clinton is scheduled to speak with Russian Foreign Minister NaveyLavsky, a U.N. security official, at the World Security Council in Paris. (AP Photo/NaveyLavsky,Reuters)"
