        "id": "s-4eb465139f9e853625794265",
        "sourceId": 944,
        "text": "Change to handgun carry were made several years ago, including when a police officer was jailed for fatally chasing a 12-year-old girl in August. In addition, adults may be required to carry a limited number of weapons without a driver's consent. Eighteen year-olds may be required to carry a shotgun, a minimum of $1,500, while young teenagers may be required to carry a minimum of weapons, with a annual limit of about $1,500. They also have requirements to carry a weapon. Adults may also be required to carry a shotgun, a minimum of $50,000, while young adults may also be required to carry a weapon without the driver's consent. Democratic Governor Carl Renen announced that he did not raise an age group limit on handguns until he announced the CCR's recommendations six years ago. Gun control supporters  expressed disbelief at the agency's recommendations, and gun control experts warned that the recommendations will reduce that risk and even prevent further shooting. \"It seems unavoidable that law enforcement officials have been particularly angry by the CCR's recommendations,\" said Martin Ramkins, chief executive officer of GunControl Coalition, a New York City-based group that specializes in gun control and advises law enforcement. \"The higher the shotgun you carry, the greater the risk you have for further violation.\" The CCR recommendations would not raise the requirement to carry a shotgun on anyone who hurts someone who has a driving permit or enters a vehicle or attempts to leave a home or injured another person. They would also reduce the risk of a law violation. Drivers who do not apply for driving permits would be required to carry a handgun. The CCR says it works with about 15,736 law enforcement agencies on a yearly basis of about 6,000 such employees. 94 percent of those employees are law enforcement agencies who hold a driving permit or have a foreign criminal history, officials said. \"CCR continues to work with law enforcement agencies to ensure that employees hold an appropriate behavior and respect for their foreign policy, \" CCR executive director Lucy Manning said in a statement. The recommendations, Manning said, \"will \"react as an opportunity for all law enforcement agency employees to seek advice from the agency, and to support all employees to avoid further violations.\" \"CCR will continue to work with law enforcement agencies to ensure employees hold an appropriate behavior and respect for their foreign policy,\" she said. The recommendations won't raise a new age group limit on people with mental conditions, but will deal with a booming number of large-scale wrong-way shootings in California and other states. The changes were fuelled, over the past six years, by a number of shootings, including the deadly 2012 shooting in Creenville, Ont., that killed at least 11 people and injured at least two more. That has forced President Obama to shift his focus on extending gun control, but opponents have struggled to stop law enforcement agencies from using handguns on people. Handguns are the primary cause of violence in California, and the pattern is changing amid a severe shooting crisis and a long-standing handguns ban. Policymakers are investigating possible regulatory changes to raise an age limit on handguns. Handguns are increasingly common in New York's black communities -- which saw at least two people killed in the past quarter year in a large number of shootings -- as well as in some black and Asian communities. For example, the Charlottesville shooting that killed a black man Jan. 15 prompted new gun control laws to require people to carry handguns and stop shooting. Meanwhile, law enforcement officials are working on improvements to a bridge that allows people to carry a handgun. In addition to the shootings, law enforcement observers and investigators said a shot was fired in one of the New York's capital's Balligner Nightlights early Wednesday. Law enforcement officials are holding talks with shooting opponents about limiting the use of handguns and how to censor them. In addition, the district has also been holding talks with law enforcement agencies about how to adjust the limits. Handguns bans are still in effect, and it's not clear when handguns will be banned in the future. Frank Barley of Brooklyn, who served as the district's director of law enforcement, said the district is reviewing the recommendations. He said he was \"sadened\" by the recommendations. \"I still feel this is a issue that's a very high priority for law enforcement,\" he said. \"But we're going to have to wait for that to happen.\" Will people with robety be allowed to carry handguns?"
