        "id": "s-78ec7e70567930182d90aafb",
        "sourceId": 33,
        "text": "Based on an earlier study by the University of California-San Diego professor of computer science, Octavio Cousins, Mark Dwuch Wolfberger and Daniel Mkueter, we used a systematic comparison of the metric impact of software experience on cognitive function. In both of the published surveys, a team of three primary scholars took a white-collar sample of software experience enhancements and developed a multi-scorer for evaluating the software impact on cognitive function with a set of preliminary measurements. A multi-scorer provided a probabilistic measure for the long-term effect of software experience on intelligence and ended with a measure for its impact on cognitive function. A data aggregation analysis was also significantly beneficial, as a result of the Amazon Consumer Health Survey, compared with the results of the published survey rather than with conventional methods. Due to the present findings, alternative methods provided for estimating the perceived impact of software experience on cognitive function were reported difficult to use or were significantly isolated from the results of the published survey. In addition, we compared the impact of software experience on cognitive function using a data aggregation analysis which occurred as part of the Amazon software experience enhancement survey (Jan 2015). Using the American Psychological Association (AAMA) method, we subjectively evaluated the accuracy of software experience as a powerful predictor for estimating its impact on cognitive function, was significantly increased for a sample of 40-year-olds or older. Specifically, we found a difference in the association of software experience improvements to preliminary measurements and their effects on cognitive function – the accuracy of a sub-division measurement strategy was statistically reduced for both tasks compared to the 1 percent found in conventional methods. In evaluating the perceived impact of software experience on cognitive function, we compared the results of a mid-year Amazon Consumer Health Survey (Jan 2015). Combined with a published survey, we found a difference in the accuracy of a multi-scorer for estimating the improvements implicated in the preliminary measurements by a sample of 45-year olds or older. As part of the mid-year Amazon Consumer Health Survey, we found that the association of software experience experience improvements to preliminary measurements by a sample of 45-year olds or older was significantly increased with the results of the published survey, when compared with conventional methods. Similarly, the perceived impact of software updates on cognitive function with the use of preliminary measurements was statistically reduced with the results of conventional methods. Furthermore, the improvements increased statistically after taking a set of preliminary measurements, while the accuracy of a software update strategy declined. However, it should be mentioned that our study did not demonstrate a significant effect of software experience on cognitive function. As a result of the published findings, we found that the generally perceived influence of software experience on cognitive function was similar to conventional methods and that the results of the sub-division measurements were very different from conventional methods. Moreover, our comparison between the influence of game experience and software updates on cognitive function and results of the published survey did not demonstrate a significant effect of software experience on cognitive function. Although there was no systematic correlation, our study raised relatively serious doubts of the long-term influence of software experience on cognitive function. In many cases, our results increased confidence in the effect of software enhancements on cognitive function. Given the limited availability of a complete set of measurements, these results indicate a serious need for further studies to evaluate the influence of software experience on cognitive function. We predicted that future results of this study would raise serious doubts of the effect of game experience and software updates on cognitive function. However, we also found a significant difference in survey accuracy when compared with conventional methods – a data aggregation analysis method could collect more data from surveys than conventional methods. This method enriched the cognitive function of surveys by reducing the influence of software updates within a 3-year period after taking the preliminary measurements [ M.A. D., b. E., M.A. D., Dwyer, S.H. J. ]. In contrast, a data aggregation analysis method significantly strengthened the association of software experience improvements and their impact on cognitive function within a 3-year period after taking a set of preliminary measurements [ M.A. D., b. E., M.A D. McCike, S.H. J. ]. Conversely, this method reduced the influence of game experience and software updates on cognitive function by 16 percent in the 3-year period after taking the improvements. Lastly, we subjectively evaluated the effect of a software experience enhancement, to show that the influence of software experience enhancements was positively associated with their impact on cognitive function. Methodological Analysis."
