        "id": "s-9e403c19f795841bedc7f7bd",
        "sourceId": 73,
        "text": "Forty-four percent of Connecticut residents supported Brown’s plan, which would cut taxes by a top rate of 17.1 percent. At the same time, it would keep the tax rate on individual federal income below 12.1 percent. The governor also came under fire Friday for his support for the House Republican budget reform amendment to the 12.1 percent tax rate. Brown’s plan to raise the tax rate on individual federal income — which was approved in the Senate — would add an additional $269 billion to the state’s operational budget. The rate increase wouldn’t prevent states from cutting taxes on individual federal income, but would add at least $335 billion to total state budgets. And it would prevent states from cutting health care programs that help pay for treatment through Medicare, Medicaid, Medicaid and Medicaid, among others. Economic analysts project that the proposed tax levy could close a $200-Billion budget deficit over the next decade. Economists forecast that a deficit of about $60 billion would be needed to replace and address that existing deficit by the beginning of next decade. Brown’s proposed reforms would see enormous impact on government spending. A tax cut could increase education spending by about $16.2 trillion and college spending by about $8.6 trillion. A cut to Social Security would increase state spending by about $12.7 trillion. Economists forecasts that a $1.812 trillion loss on government spending could be attributable to countless — not too many — tax cuts. Marc Musucci, a finance professor at George Washington University in New York, said the proposed tax cut could save corporations $100 billion over the decade. The Associated Press contributed reporting. Copyright  2019 The Washington Times, LLC. Click here for reprint permission. Authorities are searching for a high-reranking St. Paul police officer who was ordered into administrative custody after he launched an assault threat against a victim earlier this week by striking him to his head. Cop. Chris Plato, 23, was charged with public burglary, reckless endangerment, reckless endangerment, reckless endangerment and one count of attempted murder. Plato was released from the crime scene on Thursday afternoon in administrative custody, but authorities are still gathering information about his status. His arrest in St. Paul is the latest in a series of developments. Police Officer Anthony Boskel, who was previously charged with fatally shooting 17-year-old Phil Galloway, was also ordered into St. Paul custody Tuesday. Prior to his arrest, the officer told Boskel to use a deadly weapon. In response to a threat to use his weapon, Plato fired a rocket-loaded rifle and struck Galloway, according to a St. Paul report. The officer claims that Plato was trying to kill him, but that attempt led to no shooting. Plato then fired a rifle to kill Galloway, struck him to his head, and ultimately attempted to stop himself from using his weapon, according to the report. Plato now faces four counts of public burglary and reckless endangerment, the Washington Post reported in August. Galloway, who was shot five times, was identified by police officials upon arrival last Friday. He passed a DNA test that revealed no evidence of concussion, authorities say. Criminal investigators have not disclosed the motive for Galloway’s shooting. Homicide theories suggest that the highest-ranked police officer in the city is most likely to face similar charges soon. The Galaxy R2 is a peer-to-peer virtual reality headset developed by North Korea’s Kweizu. According to a Kweizu blog post published by Kweizu, the Galaxy R2 and other similar virtual reality products were developed by Kweizu. In a blog post, Kweizu emphasizes that the headset was initially intended as a social networking app — however, the company states that it has actually been working on developing the app. Kweizu developed a prototype of the Qualcomm Multi-Chear Gamepad, a virtual reality headset running Android Phone 5 that is scheduled for release in the second quarter. The headset was ultimately intended to be a gaming app, according to a blog post by North Korean outlet Kweizu. The company didn’t respond to requests for comment. Kweizu’s Executive Producer Lee Nong says that he doesn’t know whether they have any experience developing the Kweizu headset. For the latest news and updates about virtual reality technology, check out the NPR News section here or follow us on Twitter. Copyright 2011 NPR News PAALEYTON, N.Y. (NRS) — A 37-year-old former Jerseytown city council has stood down over allegations that he was being sexually attacked by a Jerseytown student."
