        "text": "Co-fired plants remains the main U.S. source of affordable electricity, according to state officials. While energy prices have shrunk markedly over the past four decades, there has been a substantial price increase in about 20 states of the country, including Indiana, Iowa, New Mexico, Michigan, South Dakota, Arkansas, Tennessee and Arkansas. Currently, about 85 percent of all U.S. electricity comes from coal and natural gas. As a result, energy prices are expected to dwindle significantly over the next decade. Coal and natural gas have become a major source of more than one percent of total electricity produced since this decade began nearly five years ago. The state s operating power capacity is 254MMW today, compared to its current 247MMW in 1990. But as energy prices continue to dwindle significantly, the state s operating capacity has nearly halved to 254MMW from 247MMW in 1990. And while the state s operating power capacity has nearly halved to 254MMW today, from 254MMW in 1990, these changes represent a substantial price increase. The rising cost of energy produced per-galon as well as falling prices on coal and natural gas are likely to make it all the more difficult for carmakers and owners to turn to coal as alternative energy. Currently, about 85 percent (46%) of all electricity produced in the United States comes from coal. While coal and natural gas now represent over two percent of total electricity produced in the United States, the rising cost of energy per-galon as well as falling prices on coal and natural gas are likely to make it all more difficult for carmakers and owners, in particular small businesses, to turn to coal and natural gas as sources of alternative energy, said state officials. In many southern areas of the country, states are considering moving away from rearing on coal and natural gas in favor of traditional coal-fired plants to natural gas-based non-carbon reduction alternatives. Currently, more than 70 percent (29 percent) of all U.S. electricity produced comes from coal-fired plants. While prices have fallen at historic lows, energy prices are expected to dwindle significantly over the next decade. More than 40 U.S. states are considering reducing energy consumption from coal-based plants in a way that would reduce energy costs and reduce atmospheric pollution. Similar changes are also being considered in North Carolina, Indiana, St. Louis, Missouri, New Jersey, Iowa, South Dakota and New Hampshire. States are considering moving on from rearing on coal and natural gas to coal-based alternatives for carbon reduction development. Rising energy consumption, such as coal and natural gas, has become a major source of alternative energy generated by rising prices over the past four decades, state officials said. More than half dozen U.S. states are contemplating a shift from coal-fired plants to coal-based non-carbon reduction alternatives. New Hampshire, Arkansas, Nevada and Missouri as well as other states are considering moving away from energy consumption from coal-fired plants in a way that would reduce energy costs and reduce atmospheric pollution. Similar changes are also being considered in Iowa, North Carolina, Indiana, St. Louis, Missouri and New Hampshire. Similarly, reducing energy consumption from coal-fired plants, such as solar power, is likely to cause a substantial reduction in coal consumption and reduced greenhouse gas emissions. Meanwhile, the number of alternative energy sources has shunk markedly over the past four decades, according to the National Institute of Energy. Finally, reducing energy consumption from coal-fired plants, such as solar power, is likely to cause a substantial reduction in coal consumption and reduced greenhouse gas emissions, state officials said. The adoption of solar power is expected to complete by the end of this decade, but officials expect it to take a substantial setback in the middle of next decade, when solar power projects are expected to account for 70 percent-80 percent of the nation s energy supply. In addition, businesses and households are considering moving away from energy consumption from coal-fired plants toward coal consumption, in conjunction with a reduction in natural gas consumption. A New Jersey man is currently on leave after investigating an alleged sexual assault on a man he met in February. Agents say the 70-year-old victim is currently on leave and will need to undergo surgery to improve his conditions. Neril Danton, 39, of Independence, is charged with 11 counts of sexual assault for allegedly abusing the 23-year-old victim while he was driving for Orlando. Special investigations say Heller left his vehicle and asked Danton, “Do you feel like I want to meet you again?” prosecutors say Heller told Danton, “I’d like to meet you again” when he stopped and sexually assaulted.."
