        "id": "s-835e79a047bffa7c9cb9f1ca",
        "sourceId": 341,
        "text": "The Nexus Pro holds a lot of promises, as is the case with the iPhone Pro. When Google releases its Nexus X, users will see a slightly bolstered 1080x1000 screen on the back of the original Nexus, and a slightly bolstered 1080x1000 screen size. Google may not be happy with this display design, since it gives a very similar look to what can be seen on the iPhone, but the company doubtlessly isn't going to want to move back to the Nexus X. Nexus X Comparison The Nexus and iPhone Pro seem to have some interest in the next Nexus X, with Google keen to pick the Nexus X over the expensive Nexus Pro with a more advanced spec. Of course, the comparison is only starting to get better. Google has put out a ton of good news about the Nexus X price tag, but it's also obvious that the company had the chance to showcase it at their Asia event this week when it announced that the Nexus X will be priced at $509. While this price tag should be a shocker, it's really great news for the laptop as a whole. It's inevitably going to be too expensive to put the next Nexus X on the market, and Google very much needs the Nexus X to throw it under the bus. Google Nexus X 2.5-core Edition Pro Google's Nexus Pro is a 1.5-core laptop with a 12.8 GHz Quad-Cor 8 CPU. This version is dubbed the \"Ginix 2.5-core Edition,\" offering a 1,299.5-inch x386.7-inch display with a mere 38GB of storage space. It's a big upgrade over the 1.5-core version of the Nexus X, offering a 1,84.8-inch x373.9-inch display with a mere 38GB of storage space. Available in a slightly larger, 10-inch-x 10-inch-8 version, it has 8GB of RAM, 64GB of graphics, over 800 Wi-Fi Express ports, and 64GB of drive space and storage. Google Nexus X Micro-Dishable Disc Google may be more at ease with the Google Nexus Pro than it is, but the Nexus Pro is a bit of an oversold according to how it's priced. As a side note, the Nexus X is a small bit cheaper than the original iPhone's Pro Micro-Dishable Disc, at about $12,900 (0,800 x 6,300). Originally, $50,899 A New Nexus X Ultra Display for Music Listen Perhaps one of the most interesting things about the Nexus Pro has been the ultra display, which will allow users to listen to movies and music in a coordinated manner without interacting with sites like the web or YouTube, and the on-the-fly ultra display seems to be solving these issues. Google Nexus X Ultra Display (only): 1,900-x259-inch Memory: 10.0 GB/h (300GB) RAM: 4.2 GB/h (256GB) Storage: 3.1 GB (only) Originally, $2 9,599 Google X-Genes X-Series Ultra-size Display (only): 1,900-x277-inch Memory: 10.0 GB/h (300GB) RAM: 4.2 GB/h (256GB) Storage: 3.1 GB (only) Originally, $2 9,599 Google X Ultra-size 7K The Google Nexus 7K is considered by many to be the next ultra-size laptop, but it's in a slightly different place from the Nexus X, delivering less RAM, faster graphics, new feature functionality, a camera with higher resolutions, and more. The Nexus 7K has a track record of less RAM and shorter battery life, but it's caught a bit off guard on normal-size devices. While having less RAM makes things a lot easier on normal-size devices, the 7K boasts a less powerful touch allocating system, a more precise touch-control feature, and support for faster graphics and easier scrolling. New Nexus 7K The Nexus 7K seems to catch a bit off the radar with the Nexus 7K, which is in a slightly different place from the Apple Nexus X. The new model is said to feature shorter battery life and less RAM than the previous Nexus X, and the new Nexus X may be the first step in in that direction. A modified rear camera is available for improved image quality on the device, but Google is up for higher pricing for it. :)"
