        "text": "1984: Robert Stall’s “Hell in Time” is nominated as the thirteenth Golden Globe Award winner. His second film, “Hell on My Mind,” is also referred to as unofficial Golden Globe’s Film of the Year Awards. 1985: Philip Fleming’s “Counter to the Holy House” is nominated for an Oscar, becoming the first film to be nominated, and among fifteen nominees for the twelveth Academy Award for Best Actor and Director. 1986: Ethan Johnson’s “The Lord” is nominated for the sixteenth Academy Award for Best Featured Picture, and became the reigning winner in the Eighth Academy Award for Best Actor and Director, and led the nomination for an impressive TV adaptation of “Shoot the Guardians” in “Season.” 1987: Mark Jackson’s “The Dragon,” is nominated for the ninth Academy Award, becoming the first film to be nominated for the Eighth Academy Award. 1991: Mark Jackson’s is also nominated for Best Actor and Director for the “movie of the Year” Awards. 1992: A.J. Watts’s “Next” is nominated among fifteen nominees for the “Putin of the Year” Awards, and nominated among twelve other nominees for the “Image of the Year” Academy Award. 1993: Simon Aiman’s “Space” is nominated for the ninth Academy Award, and remains regarded as one of the best-selling films of the decade. It also became the first film to be awarded a Television Academy Language Award nomination, which marks Aiman’s third nomination for a TV adaptation. 1994: Michael Johnson’s “Jones” is nominated for the seventeenth Academy Award for Best Featured Picture. “Jios” is also nominated for an Academy Award. In addition to being one of the top TV and magazine hits of one of the best-selling films of the decade, the film features an impressive TV adaptation of “The Lord’s Secret” with an Academy Language Award. 1995: Jean-Louis Vaughn’s “Next” and “Seating in the Stars” earn an Academy Award. 1996: Simon Aiman’s “Jason’s Diary,” which is challenged for an Academy Award twice, is considered one of the best-selling films of the decade and wins an Oscar. 1997: John Spielstein’s “The End of Time” and “The Origin” are nominated for an Academy Award and wins another Academy Award. 1997: A.J. Wattsbury’s “Next” is also nominated for an Oscar and is also nominated for the Academy “West of Life” Awards. 1998: The Golden Globe-winning adaptation of Jean-Louis Vaughn’s “Norther-forth” and Pratt Smith’s “Season” wins a film award and is still regarded as one of the most successful films of the decade. 1999: Christopher Petersen’s “Cold King” is nominated for an Academy Award. 2000: Aran Shah’s “Lauret,” which is in the running for second to appear and nominated, is considered one of the top hits of all films of the decade, featuring impressive adaptations of “What Am I Doing?” and “Where Will I Go.” Only three TV adaptations have been made in just a year and is still regarded as one of the best-selling films of a decade. 2003: Mark Clarke’s “Serious Men” is nominated for a Television Academy Language Award and is also nominated for the Academy “West of Life” Awards. 2004: Aran Shah’s “Lauret,” which is in the running for second to appear, is nominated for an Academy Award for Best Screenplay. 2006: The Italian-American “Ranio del Pintono” film, which debuted at number eleven on the Billboard Film Ranking, is nominated for an Academy Award for Best Screenplay after dropping to number eleven on Entertainment Weekly. The Italian-American adaptation of “Next,” is also the only film to receive an Academy Language Award (ranked seven on today’s Bloomberg Film Critics Poll), nominated for Best Visual Actor. 2011: Mark Jackson’s “Heroed,” which debuted at number eight on the Billboard Film Rankings, is nominated for the Eighth Academy Award for Best Supporting Actress. 2007: The best TV drama* is nominated for an Academy Award and is one of the best-selling films of the decade. Only three African American, Italian, and German films have each won Academy Awards."
