        "id": "s-bdcddd0318a670a8e60f1c41",
        "sourceId": 602,
        "text": "Since the ISIS Army seized Libya last week, the militants have been targeted in a series of shellings, airstrikes, self-provised clashes and assaults on the government. At the beginning of midday on Saturday, clashes lattered in after a mob opened fire on ISIS fighters and killed some of the supporters of Libya dictator Muammar Gaddafi. Local media reported that ISIS militants ousted the governor amid threats to topple him. An hour later, ISIS militants partially shot down a car in a military-held town of Longmi, a populated area 300 kilometers (40 miles) north of Tripoli. U.N. intelligence reports said that ISIS militants targeted a government building that served as a headquarters for insurgent operations. \"A group of ISIS fighters came from the town about 2:00 a.m. local on Saturday and blew up one house and killed 19 people living indoor,\" an Interior Ministry official, who only spoke on condition of anonymity, told reporters. Ten minutes later, 50 bombs launched at the government building, which were partially sealed off by ISIS fighters. Another 50 bombs launched at the government building, striking off residents by helicopter, the Interior Ministry said. In addition, shells were fired out of the government building, where 19 of the residents had been killed by ISIS fighters in the offensive. An hour later, in the same town, the militants came outside and shot down the administration office, a senior official with the International Military Operation Mission in Libya told Reuters. Early that morning, rockets, heavy bombs and tear gas burst into the besieged building. Eleven people were wounded. \"They fired on people who were in cold blood and still breathing, and we were trying to get out of the building when they refused to pull us over,\" Interior Ministry spokesman Eminem Mehmiyan told the Associated Press agency. \"We went outside, they opened fire on us and they held fire on us.\" At that point, ISIS militants shot dead five people, including two tourists who were non-resident, Interior Ministry spokesman Ramian Ragali said. Three of them are recovering in a hospital. ISIS militants shot down a car in Marat, south of the capital, Birmingham, and killed the fourth man, Interior Ministry spokesman Ragali told Reuters. Another senior government official had told the local media on Saturday that ISIS militants had targeted the administration office in a suburb of Birmingham, according to the Interior Ministry spokesman. There were no casualtties, the official said. \"There are no casualties,\" Abdjam Kamami, an office manager in the southern town of Koatu told Reuters. ISIS militants shot down a car in Alaqa, a military-held town north of Tripoli, and killed a fourth man. A government official, who spoke on condition of anonymity, said that ISIS militants targeted and shot down a car in a military-held town of Banahbeum, about 300 kilometers (40 miles) north of Tripoli. According to local media, ISIS militants smashed down a car in Omatini, a populated town north of Tripoli. The ISIS army bombed a refugee camp in Intrarra, about 300 kilometers (980 miles) west of the Nigerian capital of Tunisia, according to local media. \"The International Military Operation Mission in Libya intends to halt the targeting of this camp sometime in the future,\" Interior Ministry spokesman Tony Loichman told Reuters. Security Forces under investigation Meanwhile in the north of Benghazi, heavily armored security forces smashed down a car in a ISIS shelling and another in the town of Sanaz, the U.N. Embassy in Libya said. \"The activity of the security forces against the Gaddafi regime is under investigation,\" an Interior Ministry spokeswoman told Reuters. Meanwhile, in the eastern province of Aleppo, at least 25 high-level senior policemen were shot dead by Gaddafi's militants. The region's U.N. special representative John Kialy told Reuters on Saturday that Gaddafi was abandoning his administration to expose himself to a exiled army commander who was shot dead by militants who shot down his car. ABUTA (Reuters) - Libya'sDeputy Secretary of State John Kialy said decisive talks were in progress in the eastern province of Tripoli in response to failed insurgent operations against President Muammar Gaddafi last week."
