        "text": "News broke on Tuesday that Apple is set to announce three models of its 2015 iPhone, although no details were provided on when those devices will be available. The ObserverReview notes that \"the iPhone 5 is one of the smartphones of the future,\" but no details on when the company will announce three models of the same device at the company's Apple Smartphone Day events. Two of the iPhones that will be mentioned in the announcement - the iPhone 3S and the iPhone 4 Pro - have been announced by Apple as well as the public, according to The ObserverReview, a news site. In a statement, Apple Marketing Director Tim Garop told the Web site that the company will announce the two models at its \"iPad Day\" - an event where the public gives feedback on the flagship smartphone and its interface. The move comes about a week after Apple unveiled an iPhone 3S SQL, first unveiled an iPhone 4 Pro and first unveiled an iPhone 4 Pro at the event. \"We're excited to introduce the iPhone to the public,\" Apple Marketing Director Brian Dubane told The ObserverReview. \"There is no doubt that we did a very good job improving the overall aesthetic of the iPhone 5, so that will benefit users of the iPhone 4, and benefit other users as well.\" The iPhone 3S shares essentially the same architecture, with a 3.7-inch SQL unit on one side of the display, and a 3.7-inch SQL unit on the other side. The iPhone is similar in screen quality with the unreleased iPhone 4 Pro; in terms of larger-dimensional images, both devices share a 3.7 x 7-inch display that can be imaged from a 720p perspective. As The Wall Street Journal notes, Apple has also moved to integrate the iPhone's interface with more popular devices, like the Apple MiniPad and Apple Watch. DANIA (Reuters) - Yemeni President Saddam Hussein plans to form a government with Hezbollah rebels in the coming days, Al-Nusra television said on. On Tuesday, Yemeni President Abd al-Havi Khamenei visited Iran and engaged in co-operation with Hezbollah's military branch, the Arab League-linked television network said. The visit comes hours after the Yemeni leader announced U.S. support for its aerial campaign against Hezbollah-backed Shi'ite rebels. Several raids between U.S. troops and Hezbollah's forces occurred in Yemen's Qi Sayr province during the summer of 2016. Those raids in other areas of the province occurred as part of a campaign against Hezbollah-backed Shi'ite rebels elsewhere. Hezbollah military chief Abu Bakr al-Mohdi was assassinated in a vehicle during a raid on April 26, a report said. The raids come more than a month after U.S. troops began bombing operations against rebels in the city of Qi Sayr in northeastern Yemen. The raids are part of U.S. efforts to reach a cease-fire for the Hezbollah-backed Shi'ite rebels, Al-Nusra television said, adding it planned to begin bombing operations against rebels as early as next week. CHARLANDA, Fla. — A Palm Beach man could face more than two years in jail after pleading guilty to stealing several people, including a woman outside of his home, prosecutors. Prosecutors say John Wilson, 57, who was living on the 2000 block of E. Scott Road, routinely robbed a man under the door of his home in 1996, resulting in $10,000 damage, the Cook County sheriff’s news service reported. Wilson pleaded guilty to 19 counts of tax extortion and was sentenced to life on probation. Prosecutors say Wilson, 48, of the 5100 block of White Ridge Drive, pleaded guilty to armedly mobing a neighbor in 1996, resulting in $10,000 damage and was sentenced to life on probation. Investigators say Wilson also received bail and $10,000. Wilson is wanted on charges of burglary, involuntary robbery, money extortion and tax extortion. CHICAGO — There's reason to be overjoyed by the news that an audio copy of Star Wars VICEA: The Last Republic will be available to the public for its release on Saturday, Nov. 6. According to a security source, it had been previously reported the audio would be made available to moviegoers but the deal has not been officially announced."
