        "text": "Following a day of backlash from Democrats, the White House Monday announced it intended to repeal the GOP-backed American Health Care Act before passing a fully supported overhaul of the law. In a major blow for Republican leadership on both sides, Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer, R-Wash., proposed a partially repeal of the law in Monday's Congress by a 41-32 vote -- the first to pass fully supported overhaul of the law. The announcement came the day after the White House unveiled a package that would partially repeal and replace the GOP-backed health care bill. The House approved the measure by a fully Republican vote on Monday -- the second day after it passed -- and will remain the last Republican member, the landslide of the Democratic leadership said in a statement. \"We look forward to working with Congress to reach an agreement on the second attempted repeal of Bill Obamacare.\" \"This is a reminder that our party has had a difficult time over the past year in trying to compromise to rebuild our system and keep the economy from crashing down over the long term We now look forward to working with Congress to reach an effective solution, and expect it to come soon,\" Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer said in a statement. The House approved the measure by a 41-32 vote on Monday -- the second day after it passed -- and will remain the last Republican member with overwhelming veto-fueled bipartisan support. The announcement came the day after the White House approved a package that promises to partially repeal and replace the one that used to fund Obamacare -- including giving the Senate the power to pass a new budget. Following a growing backlash from Democrats on both sides, the White House decided to base the announcement of the measure on a promise to overturn the entirety of Obamacare and plans toimpose a tax on wealthy individuals. At the time, Senate Majority Leader Mitt Romney, R-Nev., who backed the Obamacare repeal, and co-sponsor Paul Ryan held a final negotiation when legislation replacing the Affordable Care Act was finally approved in the Senate with the Republican majority of the bill veto. @AP TreasurerThomas Frankney: We look forward to working with Congress to reach an agreement on the second attempted repeal of Bill Obamacare pic.twitter.com/1DfqqUY9N — Speaker Mitt Romney (@SenatorRymingney) November 27, 2017 \"We look forward to working with Congress to reach a compromise that would partially repeal and replace the GOP-backed health care bill,\" Romney said in a statement. But in a statement, the GOP has not reached a deal with the Democratic leadership to repeal the law. After a week of negotiations, on Monday, the White House finalized the measure and announced it would work on a final repeal bill -- a reform package -- that would subsequently become law. A clear majority of the House of Representatives would need to repeal Obamacare, a beleaguered health care law that many Republicans see as too difficult to replace. The White House approved the measure by a 41-32 vote on Monday -- the second day after it passed -- and will remain the last Republican member with overwhelming veto support. \"This is a reminder that our party has had a difficult time over the past year in trying to compromise to rebuild our system and keep the economy from crashing down,\" Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer, R-Wash, who has backed the Obamacare repeal, said in a statement. The announcement came the day after the White House approved a package that promises to partially repeal and replace the one that used to fund Obamacare -- including giving the Senate the power to pass a budget over the long term. But the final negotiation on the measure was cut short when legislation replacing the Affordable Care Act was finally approved in the Senate with the Republican majority of the bill veto by Democrats. \"We look forward to working with Congress to reach an effective solution, and expect it to come soon,\" the Democratic leadership said in a statement. Despite growing opposition from Democrats on both sides, Republicans remain deeply divided over repeal legislation. On Monday, House Democratic Speaker Tom Fisher, R-Texas, confirmed that he was not in favor of a major amendment of immigration law by stating that he is not in favor of passing a major repeal of the Affordable Care Act. \"I want to be clear again, I am not in favor of a major repeal. By insisting that I am not in favor of a major repeal, I am not now in favor of passing a major repeal,\" he said. This year, Democrats will need a clear majority of the House of Representatives to reach a deal that will include the final repeal of the Affordable Care Act.."
