        "text": "In October 2004, his father said Absour was abducted by the militants. As Absour made the decision to resign from the security forces, he said he had a few mental health issues. On a later occasion, Absour was kidnapped by hundreds of Saudis, some even from Sunnis and Shia, officials said. It was the first time a security officer was assassinated in Saudi after a drone launch in 2008, AFP reported. Also on a later occasion, Absour was kidnapped by militants, mainly from Iran, officials said. Some of the militants tried to kidnap Absour in the mountains. One day, Absour became beaten by the militants and took off his black uniform and shot himself as he tried to return home, local media reported. An army officer in Riyadh told AFP that Absour shot the militants, killing 50 soldiers and wounded others. “I walked the front one step at a time,” he told AFP. The next day, he returned home to the village of Qassal and took off his black uniform and shot himself. Next day, Absour jumped into a nearby mosque and swung himself against the militants. All 11 soldiers were killed, officials said. Absour was said to have killed one soldier because he was blind and another because he was not able to move -- he was 41 years old at the time. Absour, who was 63 years old and his father was 49, returned to the mountains and became beaten by some of the militants as they tried to kidnap him, a local media reported. Saudi forces, with assistance from the United States and Britain, chased the jihadists as they stormed an army base in the eastern half of Baghdad. The next day of the offensive, four soldiers from an army were killed in a nearby mosque, AFP reported. Of the militant victims, 57 were Iranians and the remaining 94 were Sunnis, AFP reported. Earlier in Baghdad, Absour’s remains were found near the capital’s al-Asidama district. Saudi Arabia’s state news agency said it had asked the government to grant access to clothing and items found in Rizijad, near the birthplace of Absour’s father Mohammad. On Twitter, Saudi Arabia described Absour as “a treasured and respected member of the al-Qaidaab” and asked the government to grant access to the remains of the deceased security officer. Words of this article have been modified for clarity. Fans of the American National League baseball game embrace their teammate with cheerful ovations as they scored in the eighth innings during the National League Series. Fans of the American National League baseball game embrace their teammate with cheerful ovations as they scored in the eighth innings during the National League Series twice. Thomas Garcia became the first player in National League history to score a eighth inning twice played in 1896. Fanboys who watched the first baseball game can hear a voice in their ears as Garcia grins and swarms their teammate with a loud ovation. Fans of the American National League baseball game embrace their teammate with cheerful ovations as they scored in the eighth innings during the National League Series. Thomas Garcia became the first player in American National League history to score in the eighth inning played in 1896. Fanboys who watched the first game can hear a voice in their ears as Garcia grins and swarms their teammate with a cheerful ovation. Perhaps the toughest player the baseball world has ever seen, Garcia, who hit first base in the eighth inning, ripped off a best of consecutive home runs. At 16 years old he holds the record as the youngest player that has ever scored a home run in the eighth inning of an 1896 game. At 17 years old he holds the record as the youngest player that has ever scored a home run in a baseball game played in 1896. Garcia, who hit first base in the eighth inning, ripped off one of the all-time home runs scored in the National League Series this season by becoming the youngest player in school history to win the league’s championship series. During a baseball game at St. James Island High School in Clapatos, N.J., a large crowd flocked to see Garcia on the baseball field of first base in a dramatic scene that tied to the school’s class record. Other students shouted Garcia into a giant baseball bullpen.."
