        "text": "Toronto Star Police Chief Sean McDowell said officers are increasing activity in the 400-acre area, but added they aren't seeing areas with large or extensive patrols become too busy. ( Mary Van Henden / Toronto Star Contributor ) Toronto Star Police Chief Daniel Wally said the city has 510,000 more officers working in the retail and entertainment industries, but added they aren't seeing areas with large or extensive patrols become too busy, either. Police Chief Daniel Wally said officers are increasing activity in the 400-acre area, but would not specify how many are in the retail and entertainment industries or if patrols are beginning to move in. \"There is a large portion of the area that is a police presence,\" Chief Wally said. McDowell said the city isn't bringing in too many officers, but said he does know people are coming in. Gary Fox, chief of public safety for the Toronto Star Police Service, said he's not heard of any law enforcement officers in the area and was not informed when agents were scheduled to arrive there. \"I've been seeing a lot of law enforcement officers out there in this area, but I've never heard of them anywhere other than they have been involved in some kind of criminal activity,\" Fox said. \"They've been there for over a week before agents said they were going to come out here.\" McDowell, who's been in the area for more than a year, said he's never seen any criminal activity of this nature and doesn't think crime is slowly to increase. \"I'm surprised I've seen a lot of them,\" he said. \"I've heard desperate agents tell them to come out here.\" McDowell said he doesn't know the situation yet, but said he expects authorities to be cautious of what's happening quickly. He said he wasn't notified of that situation at that time. \"I think we need to be careful and we've got to ensure we've got to ensure that we're not going to take all the steps that we have to take to prevent this crime, but we still have serious issues to deal with,\" he said. McDowell said agents arrived in the area on Wednesday to identify likely perpetrators and that he'll spend the next few days analyzing the case with police and other agencies. Josh Richards/CBC Toronto Star The smartphone revolution is bringing in massive sales of smart phones, but a new smart phone is getting a pretty good ride recently, and it's definitely going up in prices. The Sony Xperia Z7 Z was unveiled earlier this month, and a prototype picture of the device has been shown off to us. The new Z7 Z looks realistic, and we're surprised how it looks. There's going to be a lot of rumors about the Sony Xperia Z7 Z as of now, and the prices of the previous-generation smart phones are definitely going up too. The new Sony Xperia Z7 is a pretty good ride of the current Xperia Z7, but this does mean that the device is going to keep getting a lot of rumors between now and the end of the year. Sony is revealing a new smart phone at their annual CoinCon event this week. The device, the Xperia Z7, is expected to feature a 12\" x 10.8\" HD screen, a 15.0-inch touchscreen, a 4MP sensor and 256GB of storage. Adding to those hardware specs, the device will have 4GB RAM, an 8-inch Snapdragon display, 32GB of RAM, an 8MP camera, an 8MP Gmail laptop, an 8MP Gmail card, an 8MP Gmail chipset and up to 12 micro NPCs according to the Sony Business Rumors. Additionally, it will have a 2.5-inch HD Qualcomm Snapdragon 11 display, 4GB RAM, 1.4 TB of storage, 32GB of battery, an 8MP rear camera, a 4MP sensor, a 4MP camera, a 8MP Gmail chipset and up to 12 micro NPCs. That's going to be just a fraction of the original price tag for the new Android, iOS and Windows devices. Now, Sony is unveiling a new Windows update page, and on the top of the page is a list of the latest operating systems and hardware updates. Switch out on the side, switch back on the side again. The same device will have a big 12\" x 10.8\" screen, a 15.0-inch Qualcomm Snapdragon 11 display, 11.4 TB of RAM and 256GB of storage. Subscribe Loading...."
