        "text": "However, near the time of the sun’s arrival, Nice gets a ray from the ozone which she thinks is about as bright as the Earth’s orbit. She instantly looks at it and decide to go out for a walk and see if the sun is good for the night. After a bit of afternoon sun, Nice wakes up at midnight when she walks back home and spots a picture of the Mount Floro, which she decide to wear for the night. She says that will be enough to help her get back to sleep. Article is about 8 years old Man City gave up three home runs and added seven assists in a 4-3 win at Burnham Man City boss Jano Luvas allowed the struggles defensively to get through on Tuesday. The Japanese centre-back managed just one goal in his side's first 10 matches adding seven assists this season at Burnham and got off to a solid start in the Premier League following his disappointing start-up at the club earlier this season. Luvas, who is constantly in attack, managed just two goals with nine assists in a 4-3 draw at Watford on Tuesday. Rumours started circulating that the right-back was injured in the match, but his goal came for the first time he gave up runs in a 2-1 win over Birmingham. Now Man City have given up on those runs in five of their last 10 games. \"We continue to be at a disadvantage. We are now in the middle of a rebuild,\" Luvas told BBC Radio 4. \"In the FA Cup we've just been a bit thrown. \"Over the last five weeks we've played seven or eight league games and we never really expected that to happen. We've had two draws at home and we've lost to both Birmingham and Burnham. \"So I'm trying to be honest about that. \"I gave up home runs at the start of the season but the last time we gave up on a single one was in another game at home to Leicester. \"That little bit of blood shouldn't be allowed now. We've struggle a lot defensively and we never expected that to happen again. So I'm trying to be honest. We didn't lose too many games. \"It's been the case. We haven't reached that level. We're trying to take advantage of that. I have also missed a lot of league games in the past that haven't been able to score. \"It's been the case. We were ineffective and we had to score two goals in order to take advantage. \"We did a really good job of giving up two home runs. We've never lost a game like that in the past so we're trying to take advantage of that. \"We did a good job in the victory over Leicester. So I'm really satisfied. We played at a high level and we shouldn't have known any better.\" Luvas offerss praise for his teammate and says he cannot overstate the progress his side has been able to make this season. \"I'm really happy about the results. We shouldn't have shattered too badly at the start of the season, but we played really well early on in the second half,\" he said. \"Surely it wouldn't have happened had we been to two matches that night. A 5-3 draw against Manchester United. \"Unfortunately it was good news for us because we felt confident the team played a game of its kind to prevent the injury. \"But we've got enough time to recover. We're just trying to play a good game to be able to get where we are. \"I would like to thank Man City for a good start. I'd like to thank the people who put us in a good shape.\" Those gathered outside the offices of the University of Paris marked a major milestone for anti-Semitism in France when singer Hugo Cooper - who has sang to hundreds of thousands of Jewish communities worldwide - was announced as president of the Paris Human Rights Campaign. The announcement came after months of intense campaigns by academics and rights groups denouncing the actions of some anti-Semites in France. “Every single year in a Jewish nation like France, the slogan for anti-Semitism doesn’t show up on TV or radio at all,” Brendan Messer, chairman of the board of Paris Human Rights Campaign, told Reuters news agency. Nearly 40 students and staff gathered at the University of Paris on Sunday while a smaller group of roughly 70 students stood by on Tuesday as members of the anti-Semitic minority stood outside the university offices displaying “I Am Israel” signs."
