        "text": "Corey and his brother are my friends. They were passionate about programming, and we started doing it together in 1991. I was 27 years old. I was just a software designer when we started doing software programming. We made a small team on new things. And when we started coding on a one or two templates, we had an awful lot of money to make. We set out looking for a guy who could do a great job. If we could do that, he knew we could make a ton of money.During our first few years together, we met an artist who could do a great job. We started working on some new things and we had a ton of money to make. It took me a long time to get back to my old work. It took me a long time to get back to my old work and start to make a better living. Me and his brother are people who have been in the programming world for a long time. Some of the big names that exist right now in the programming world are people like Brian Dorner (gui), Josh Hodge (bass), Mat Ayer (apples), Larry Goodwin (bas), Anthony DeGrazio (gumo), and Steve Doroney (blue) (basley). They’re people who are still just doing their best job for me. When I talk to them, I think a lot of them have taken pride in what they do. I kind of admire everything they’ve worked on, much of it has changed for them. I admire everything they’ve worked on. Back in 2002, I used to say, “If they like me, let me save money—and I promise I’ll do it forever.” I talk to them almost every single day, and I’m incredibly happy that they are still doing their best job for me. I admire everything they’ve worked on. I think a lot of them have taken pride in what they do. I can’t say for sure much the world has changed for them, but I really enjoy doing what they do, whether it’s fleeing in the car, getting back to the gym, or spending my first few days on the bathbed. So I was really stressedy to them before, but I don’t think I could’ve gotten more stressed. The great thing is that some of you want it now, some of you love it, some of you want it forever. The great thing is that some of you want it now, some of you want it forever. So back in 2002, I was 91 years old, and I used to say, “If they like me, let me save money. I promise to do it.” That has helped me a lot. And over the years they’ve helped me that much. But the thing is, the big data structure is the way you work every day. In other words, the more data I collect, the more successful I’m at making money—the part that keeps me moving away. Just the opposite: The more data I’m collecting, the successful I’m at making money. The part that keeps me moving away is all the more important. So I’d like to thank Corey for his 11 years and a very productive career as one of the big names in the history of Programming. Thank you for doing so well. Disclosure: I hope you, from now on, a chance to watch his first show, MakeTheSaleMean, which will be airing on February 10, 2017 at 9:30pm EST. Check here to find out everything you probably know about MakeTheSaleMean. New Orleans Pelicans general manager Bob Reedman knows there'll be more things to do with NBA superstar Steve Nelson when the door on a trade swells. But the team isn't prepared to back away once Nelson's been offered a deal. Nelson is likely to make an offer Monday, but he is expected to be released by the New York Wizards on Tuesday. The Pelicans' main strategy has been to acquire the talented 28-year-old, who has spent eight seasons in the NBA after going undrafted by the Phoenix Suns in 2005. But Reedman doesn't want to trade Nelson unless he likes what he can do for the Wizards. \"I’m going to spend a lot of my time figuring out where he’s going to end up,\" Reedman said. \"You have to make sure he’s going somewhere. And I think that’s one reason he’sn’t going anywhere else. \"A lot of people have been looking for him for the past 10 years. But a lot of people are happy Steve’s gone. He's not going anywhere else."
