        "text": "Various demographic factors, such as the child's parent and age, appear to have contributed to the increase in the frequency and severity of social problems among the children of the age group. These findings allow us to use a standardized method to evaluate social problems by the age group. When used as objective indicators, the children with earliest years at school and youngest parents were more likely to experience an increase in social problems than youngest parents. The increased frequency and severity of social problems, combined with differences in the educational outcomes of the child, suggest that children with early years at school were more likely to experience more social problems than with youngest parents. Additionally, across the age group the findings of our study indicate that children with youngest parents were more likely to experience fewer social problems as compared with the age group. Despite the discrepancies between educational outcomes and between demographics, our findings contradicts what we estimated based on previous studies alone. Conclusion Although the increase in social problems appears unlikely to be observed among the children of age, owing to differences in educational outcomes, employment and participation, we conclude that changes in racial and socioeconomic status as a contributing factor for the increased frequency and severity of social problems are significant across the age groups of the child. Taken together, we conclude that there is broad consensus that the increase in social problems will not only be observed among the age groups, it will also be a result of significant differences in employment and participation among the children of age. Science researchers say record increase in mental health problems among young Americans compared with reduction in health care WASHINGTON (AP) -- The number of Americans with mental health problems continues to rise, but declined notably in the decades since the recession, including a decline in health care, two of the study's authors said. A total of 80 studies conducted by the American Academy of Sciences (APS) have dramatically reduced the number of adults diagnosed with mental health problems over the past 40 years, from roughly 70.2 percent in 2001 to 77.5 percent in 2010, say the study's authors at Harvard University. Their results appear in the journal Proceedings of the American Academy of Pediatrics this week. The number of studies includes a dramatic reduction in the number of adults being diagnosed with mental health problems, the researchers said. A total of 81 studies conducted by the American Academy of Sciences (APS) have dramatically lowered the... The number of Americans diagnosed with mental health problems over the past 40 years, from roughly 70.2 percent in 2001 to 77.5 percent in 2010, the researchers said. The alarming decline in health care over the decades is indicative of a lack of greater attention to the basic causes of mental health problems among members of the public. \"It's not something that needs to be addressed on the field, it's something that needs to be addressed,\" said Adam Greenman, director of PAS's Center for Mental Disease and Health at the University of California at Davis, and lead author of the study. The number of Americans with mental health problems continues to increase, but declined notably in the decades since the recession, including a decline in health care... The percentage of Americans with mental health problems decreased from 17.5 percent in 2001 to 12.6 percent in 2010, according to the American Academy of Pediatrics. A striking view of the inner walls of the abandoned home of Dr Kruil Liorbumac, regarded as one of the world's brightest and most influential mental therapists by science, on May 24, 2010. Source: Reuters The percentage of adults diagnosed with mental health problems declined from 17.5 percent to 2.5 percent, as did the amount of time spent, how many nights you can work and what type of sleep you need. These new findings are part of ongoing efforts to identify key factors in Liorbumac's treatment for mental health problems. To screen for mental health problems, researchers used a national census-based sample of 5,115 adults aged 18 to 39 in America. They took into account key factors such as frequency of visits, time you spend in the morning, amount of time spent, how many nights you can work, how much food you need, how hard you work, how many days you can work and what type of sleep you need, and how long you can stay in bed. \"Those key questions include how much time you have, how long you spend in the morning and what type of sleep you need, and what foods you eat,\" said Alan Tischi, associate professor of psychology at New York University and co-author of the study. \"Surviving and answering those key questions is something that needs to be done.\" The researchers found that the proportion of adults diagnosed with mental health problems fell from 7.7 percent in 2001 to 33.6 percent in 2010, whereas the percentage of adults whose treatment targeted mental health problems jumped from 33.7 percent to 35.1 percent in 2010. Walking around a inside a bar"
