        "sourceId": 860,
        "text": "Last December, President Trump's GOP plan was the longest and most complicated agreement on tax reform in Congress's history. The plan would raise tax rates by at least 25 percent, and force the richest 4 percent to pay slightly more than the bottom 10%. Researchers predict that the top 90% will pay at least $700 million or more in tax cuts than the bottom 10% will pay in 2017, for the first year in a row. The forecast numbers have not been correlated with inflation in low-income households. The increase in tax rates for Americans at the highest income level is expected to be the biggest in at least 40 years. Last December, the Republican-led Congress failed to pass more than $300 billion in tax cuts, and that would have increased the deficit to $1 trillion, including the top of 10%. Trump's GOP plan was the longest and most complicated agreement on tax reform in congressional history. The plan would have cost the top about $400 billion or less in the first fiscal year, and that would have helped the top 10% pay $282 billion or more in tax cuts in 2017. Senate Republicans failed to agree on an increase in the tax cuts during the first fiscal year. The Senate has flatly opposed the deal, which would have threatened to go along with an increase in the tax rate. Some Republicans have said it would raise tax rates for Americans making $1 trillion, while administration officials said the deal would help cut the highest rate for the wealthy. Mid-December, Senate Republicans released an executive order opposing Trump's plan over a 14-week period that would increase the top rate by half for middle-aged Americans and millionaires of Americans. For the second consecutive year. Trump's GOP plan would cut corporate benefits by more than 25 percent and reduce the tax rate for Americans making under $250,000 to nearly $1 trillion, for numerous millionaires of Americans. The White House warned in December that the deal would give the richest Americans an incentive to pay an up to 10 percent lower rate than the top of 10%. The White House, however, had threatened to go along with an increase in the tax cuts during the first fiscal year. Trump's GOP plan would cut corporate benefits by more than 25 percent and would reduce the tax rate for Americans making less than $250,000, while administration officials said the deal would help cut the highest rate for the wealthy. Senate Democrats denounced the agreement as the \"toughest ever agreement on tax reform,\" praising the text of the deal \"balanced or incomplete.\" After low-level discussions, the White House finally reached agreement on increasing the top rate by 15%, although there was no agreement on such a rate reduction. In mid-December, Senate Republicans and Senate Majority Leader Mitch McConnell of the Senate Democrats released an executive order challenging the Trump administration's plan boasting that the GOP plan would increase the top rate by half for Americans at the highest income level. Some Republicans warned the deal would give the richest Americans an incentive to pay an up to 10 percent lower rate than the top and force the top rate to increase by half. Last December, the Trump administration -- which is seeking a rate reduction for Americans making $1 trillion -- was looking for an alternative to reducing corporate benefits or gas taxes instead of an increase in the tax rate. The Senate unanimously opposed the deal, which was negotiated over a 14-week, one-sided period, forgetting the negotiating terms and political terms. The deal is described as the toughest ever agreement on tax reform in congressional history. Correction: In a previously published version of the article, the previously updated version of the article said Trump's plan would raise tax rates for Americans making less than $250,000 and for millionaires of Americans to pay. Follow Josh Clark on Twitter @JoshChamson. Samsung doesn’t want to give up the Galaxy S5 for nothing, but if it hadn’t already launched the Galaxy S5, I suspect there’d be a LOT of waiting room. Well, it doesn’t seem surprising that Samsung has discarded its plans for the Galaxy S5, and instead is planning the Galaxy S4, or Galaxy Note 4 as a device that will come with the ability to take a photo from the phone, or watch movies from the S5’s own app. News of the loss of the device last year came as a complete shock, with the Galaxy S5 still unveiled. Samsung has since confirmed that news, and a statement was posted on the “Sing S5” blog. The news has only been getting worse, as much of the Chinese media is reporting that a new version of the Galaxy S5 will be unveiled by the end of the third-quarter of 2018. Alas, so far this isn’t to say that Samsung has abandoned its plans for the next S5, commonly known as Galaxy Note 4, or a PC version of the Galaxy S5.."
