        "text": "In summary, the Office of the Budget said spending by the federal government on roads, rivers and infrastructure is expected to increase by about 8 percent through 2017, compared to $1.9 trillion over the same time frame last year. The office also used a different model to estimate the impacts of spending by the entire federal government, saying there has been no reduction in the debt situation over the past year. The office further said spending on roads and rivers is expected to increase by 3 percent to $42 trillion through 2017, compared to $479 trillion over the same time frame. In a slightly different estimate, the office predicted the national debt will increase by $3.6 trillion over the next six months. Further, the office said economic growth is expected to increase by 4.6 percent through 2017,compared to 7.2 percent over the same time frame last year and economic growth by 4.6 percent at the same time. Meanwhile, the Office of the Budget said the economy will increase spending by 7.2 percent to $2 trillion through 2017, creating an annual surplus of $2 trillion. The office further said to withstand with the current debt situation, the federal government will spend $19 trillion on national health care, $4 trillion on social security, and $40 trillion on foreign aid through 2017, creating an annual surplus of $17 trillion at the same time. In a different estimate, however, the office predicted unemployment will increase by 11 percent to $1.0 trillion and $1.0 trillion over the next six months. It also predicted unemployment will rise by 4 percent for the first time in 40 years. In summary, the Office of the Budget said the national debt is expected to increase by 8 percent through 2017, creating an annual surplus of $6.72 trillion. At the same time, the office said it expected to see the national debt rise by 8 percent over the next six months. The government has been dealing with a budget deficit in recent months and has carried out a significant spending cut on roads, rivers, and bridges. OSHA Secretary Lora Blasse told reporters Thursday, “This is about the president’s health and well-being.” “It’s not about the debts, this is about the president’s health and well-being,” Blasse said to reporters. According to the U.S. News and World Report, the federal government is expected to spend as much as $375 billion over the next six months on social security and infrastructure projects. The government has been dealing with a budget deficit in recent months and has carried out a significant spending cut on roads, rivers, and bridges. New England Patriots linebacker Jonathan Carson announced he will miss the 2017 season following surgery on a fractured left hip. Carson severely suffered a torn ACL in the Patriots’ 41-13 loss to the Atlanta Falcons. The 25-year-old said he hopes to continue his practice, which has been exacerbated by the injury, through training camp and said there are no plans to play in the NFL. The extent of Carson’ torn ACL and other injuries were revealed on a conference call Sunday, prior to the Broncos’ 37-25 loss to the Indianapolis Giants. Carson received 4,059 yards and six passing touchdowns in his final seven games of the season and is expected to return accordingly in Game Week 2. He said he is determined to continue practicing. \"The rest of the year is going to be all about practice,\" Carson said. \"I don't want to take extra time, as long as I can still keep a winning season I shouldn't be playing for extra time. I don't want to spend extra time in the gym. \"I definitely want to practice for the rest of the year but as long as I can keep a winning season going I shouldn't be playing for extra time. I'm determined to continue my practice.\" (Published Sunday, June 8, 2015) At least 12 people were killed and 94 have been injured in a shooting in South Brooklyn Thursday afternoon. (Photo: Justin Poll, Times Union, AP/Mediaite) WASHINGTON (WSUnion) -- Four people died from serious injuries in a shooting in South Brooklyn Thursday afternoon, the NYPD said. The shooting occurred at 11:45 p.m. Thursday in the 800 block of South Brooklyn at Third and Fourth Streets, NYPD spokeswoman Janet McDonald said. A 35-year-old man was seated in the 1000 block of South Brooklyn Avenue and did not suffer from gunfire or other serious injuries, McDonald said. The motivation for the shooting is yet to be determined. Authorities have not agreed yet on whether or not to charge him. At least 12 people were killed and 94 have been injured in a shooting in South Brooklyn Thursday afternoon. The motivation for the shooting is yet to be determined, McDonald said. The shooting took place in the 1200 block of South Brooklyn Avenue."
