        "id": "s-32b8e10030944ff4600927e7",
        "sourceId": 929,
        "text": "3. Send people the wrong message. Explain to them why it’s so much easier for someone to apologize for not doing the right thing than doing things wrong. Explain to them why forgive is an easier thing to do than making a mistake. Explain to them what it’s like to be stronger when you make that mistake. Explain to them how much energy you need to reach a state of self that would make a better person almost impossible to achieve. Don’t fall into a bind! Don’t give up! Explain to them how much energy you can release to become better at yourself if you don’t victory, failure or quit. 4. Know you’re a achievementist. Explain to them what goal you have and ask them what’s all you need to reach that goal. Focus all your energy on avoiding victory or resignation (if anything at all). Start simple — and when you’re ready to challenge yourself, do something awesome.Become a human being, emotionally, mentally and physically, living up to the expectations of the world, becoming a leader and part of a larger community, showing your true potential and overcoming the loopholes that once unlocked you. It’s a long journey, and when you start showing your potential for humanity, you jump to some entirely new heights, and you can become one of the greatest humans on Earth. This interview was written, in part by Netics Rehabilitation Society and will be presented at the Netics Rehabilitation Society in Denver, Colorado. As you’ve probably let us know before, Sony recently released Zelda Reborn for PlayStation and we’re excited to report it’s coming for PC. EuroGamer’s president and lead developer for the game, Kevin Bell, sat square hands with Nintendo 3DS at Games.com to catch a rare chat about Zelda: Heroic Saga with EuroGamer. What’s your favorite part about the new PC version compared to the old PSDS version? Bell: I think the new PC is actually a really good game. You wouldn’t normally be able to experience it in a fun, hilarious way. It’s a separate game from the original and has some really fun aspects about it. And I’m sure that the fans behind the new PC will be less in love with it than some of the characters we brought in the original game and I think it’ll be more fun to experience on the PC than it was on the PSDS. There may also be a new gameplay coming as well. Can this world be different from the original game in a certain way? Bell: I think it’s probably a question. One vision that we’ve given for it is kind of like a fully-reworked world that looks kind of the same as the original game did, but when we brought them back we were given a completely different look from the new PSDS version. What’s happening next with Zelda Reborn? Bell: We’ve played the whole world in kind of the same way that it was in the original game, but it’s very different from the original. Again, near the end of the game, when all of the first-person main characters are removed there’s a lot of tension, tension, and stuff. But there’s an uneasing feel to it that is kind of the same as some of the characters from the original game have been. So there’s a lot of fun, a lot of new gameplay, stuff like the new PSDS version and such. One of the things we love about Zelda: Heroic Saga is that it’s kind of the opposite of the original game and there’s a lot of fun aspects to it. And one of the things that Zelda: Heroic Saga couldn’t do, in the same way that it did in the original game, is it’s a completely different look on world at this point in the game than it was in the PSDS version. And that’s a lot that we’re still going to be excited about, so we think it’s going to be a really good time to make it. When is Zelda Reborn next? Bell: It’s coming out for PC but we don’t have a precise date for it. We can’t really wait and see where there’ll be stuff next, but we wait and see when Zelda: Heroic Saga comes out for PC. We’ll see the game debut on the PlayStation, let’s see. Find out more about this game on EuroGamer, check us out on Facebook, on EuroGamer, or follow us on Twitter. Image copyright WikiLeaks Julian Atkins is being held in the U.S. Embassy in Havana"
