        "id": "s-5758a06ab809a7484ceb4fdc",
        "sourceId": 953,
        "text": "It has not yet been evaluated the potential benefits of solar power storage. Plans have been announced for the SDR/SWS 70S and DND/DGD 70S, which could jumpstart the development of solar power sources. Experts predict the DQ700, a first-generation solar energy generator and power-hunging vehicle, could reach a top speed of 20mph (1.76 km/h). The University of Wisconsin's Kelly Nichols poses with a banner urging undocumented immigrants to give up voting rights outside the Wisconsin State House, Friday, Apr. 28, 2011, in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. Governor Scott Walker is signing legislation into the state legislature. (AP Photo/Sean Van Heal, AP) WASHINGTON (AP) — Wisconsin lawmakers are expected to act on a bill that would allow more than 90 million people to enter the state without trial. The bill is expected to come into effect after about 11 months of debate. The bill would require 90 million people within the state to cast their ballots, including most undocumented immigrants. The Senate in Wisconsin is scheduled for a statewide vote in November on bills that would violate the constitution and repeal the right to vote entirely. The bill attempts to hinder the state's efforts to prevent provisions of federal laws from allowing undocumented immigrants to be prosecutiond on felony charges, including a life sentence. \"We've spent the past two years trying to ensure Wisconsin residents have the right to vote legitimately, fairly and openly,\" said House Majority Leader Nathan Leahy, a Democrat who is emerging as the favorite to lead the state Senate. \"This bill has passed both the House and the Senate in a way that better serves the people of Wisconsin.\" If unsupported by the Senate, the bill is set to come into effect on Nov. 21, a week before the June general election. It is expected to be approved by the state House of Representatives at some point in the process. If approved, the bill would require the statewide support of the state's Senate. It would also prevent federal laws from allowing about 90 million undocumented immigrants to enter the state, be prosecuted on felony charges, and be charged and deported. Supporters of the bill say the law's provisions, including deferred action, deportations and even a life sentence, could be too targeted at gay undocumented immigrants. Approximately 93 percent of Wisconsin's population is white. More than 54 million are held without trial. White Americans make up 87.4 percent of the state's population. Hispanics account for about 29.4 percent of Wisconsin's population. nationwide, about one-fourth of the state's 77.6 million residents are white. Approximately 60 percent of the state's total residents are 65 years old or older. \"Without this bill become law, Wisconsin would be stuck in an era of deferred action and deportations,\" said Casey Guin, a law professor at Rutgers University. Opponents of the bill argue it could serve as a too broad disciplinary deportation policy. It would include few Senate votes and say it's too targeted at immigrants from other parts of the state. Although the bill is expected to pass the Senate after 11 months of debate, which may become law by then, supporters of the current measure argue it could set a precedent for passage in the future. Deferred action and deportations are aimed at gay undocumented immigrants, while current deportation policies see people held without trial. By the end of the year, 71,515 gay and lesbian men have been held under house arrest in detention centers nationwide, according to an annual report by the Centers for Disease Control. Advocates say Wisconsin's right-to-vo provisions are unconstitutional. They argue they're broad, poorly implemented and are therefore unconstitutional. \"When does a resident have right to vote? We haven't considered it,\" said Beryla Smith, spokeswoman for the Wisconsin Voting Alliance, a group that advocates for non-voting rights in the state. \"It's not going to be a token of protection for our residents.\" Im Smith, a legal adviser to the National Coalition on LGBT Rights, said most states except Wisconsin would not require residents 65 years old or older to cast ballots. The state would require residents to be below 10 years of statutory age in order to cast a ballot. Wisconsin's senate has said it will consider future bills to uphold some of the law's provisions, but it is unlikely to consider any new ones until the general election later this month. Just who is the waitress?"
