        "sourceId": 1014,
        "text": "\"He knows how tough it can be to play elsewhere because there's a different side here and it's just tough to play. He's a young player with a great talent.\" PHILADLPHIA -- Giants general manager Libby Jindal said Monday he does not intend to force tight end Darland Smith to leave college, but said this is to maximize his abilities. Jindal said Smith brought in a lot of opportunities to his team last season and now the Giants are ready to deliver. \"He had a lot of opportunities under his belt to his team,\" said Jindal. \"So he brought in some really talented players, who are really tight ends. But when he first came here he had a lot of opportunities under his belt because he knew he wanted to play and he wanted to play. So he gave us a great opportunity. So I'm surprised how talented he is.\" The 5-foot-3, 2,259-pound Smith was named the Defensive Player of the Week last season. That season, he caught 80 passes for 1,166 yards, six touchdowns and six interceptions. Smith, who took the No. 2 NFL ranked Philadelphia Eagles in the seventh round of the 2013 draft, will continue to be a pivotal figure in the Giants' tight end attack. \"He will know when he's playing right now,\" said Smith. \"So he'll know what he has. He's had a lot of great games and he's had a great campaign. We'll let him know when he's ready and he'll be on our club. So I'm sure he'll have the best chance of playing.\" The 26-year-old Smith has been eligible to play next Saturday after having undergone surgery to replace the swollen hamstring suffered in the second half of his freshman season at college. That hamstring has been removed from the season opener against the Denver Broncos, as the Giants are 7-2-1 from 1-point angle. Defensive coordinator Tom McCurty remains on the roster after the hamstring suffered, and Matt Henningman remains off the team's injury list. American-led coalition forces carried out air strikes on Syria on Sunday, the largest U.S.-led military operation since Syrian President Maria Bashar al-Assad was removed from power last month. The conflict began more than a year ago when al Assad fired nonlethal surface-range ballistic weapons with the aim of de-escalating the use of ground-range chemical weapons against the government, senior U.S. officials said. \"A campaign by al Qaeda-linked militias aimed at displacing the Syrian government marks the latest round of U.S.-led coalition activities aimed at overthrow al Assad's government,\" the U.S. military said in a statement. Syrian forces fired weapons including car bombs, an Su-51 missile, a Cherith missile, a surface-range ballistic missile and non-lethal ground-range ballistic weapons. The launches were the latest in a series of activities aimed at overthrow the government of Bashar al Assad. U.S. military officials said they also took off seven F-16 fighters from a Syrian Air Force base in Hama on Sunday after fighting to overthrow the government of the jihadist leader. U.S. military forces took off seven F-16 fighters in an offensive against Syrian forces in Hama on Sunday. Earlier in the day, a White House official told The New York Times that President Donald Trump had warned al Assad not to overthrow the U.S. government. \"If the president continues to do so, he will immediately remove the dictator,\" the U.S. official said. The White House said al Assad's militias are \"infringing\" on the security in Syria. Al Assad's Syrian Army has been a ally in U.S.-backed opposition in Iraq. The U.S. embassy in Moscow said on Sunday it had carried out approximately 600 air strikes against al Assad's ground-range chemical weapons. The U.S.-led Syrian Air Force has been fighting alongside al Qaeda-linked militias recently. The air strikes were the latest in a wider series of counterterrorism offensives launched by U.S.-led coalition forces that have been embroiled in a decade-long war. U.S. military officials said U.S. Army forces took off seven F-16 fighters from a U.S. Army base in Hama on Sunday following a military campaign by militias."
