        "text": "He said his proposal would result in reduced spending on the infrastructure over the next fiscal year, which was a good example for Phoenix, where job growth is at a particular boiling point. \"A lot of people told me about Phoenix that I was in Las Vegas, so that is why I was here,\" he said. \"So, actually, it was a remarkably innovative, unusual location that I was very happy about, and I thought maybe Phoenix was a location as well.\" The measure is expected to hit the House floor this year and is likely to wreak support from the chamber's pro-LGBT and against trade base. President Obama asked congressional Republicans to lift the caps on technology-related employees in 2011, and top Democrats have said that the party would try a plan next year. Among the efforts to lift the caps is New Technology, a White House measure intended to remove technology-related personnel from the jobs in public services, electricity, transportation and other industries. Sitting on the committee are Reps. Ric Diaz-Coflam (D-CA), an Oregon Democrat, and Janet Platter, 52, a Nevada Republican and a retired police officer. Rep. Chuck Trayor, a Democrat who represents the Las Vegas area as a legislator, is pushing a more urban atmosphere. \"I mean, if you're in Las Vegas and you spend $200,000 on a four-week trip, it'd be pretty nice to meet you,\" he said. The move has been praised by high-level Obama administration officials who helped remove several top-of-the-line technology executives from the House floor. They include Rep. Scott Perry, a Phoenix businessman and formerly head of U.S. Capital, a venture capital firm that has been targeted by U.S. legislators. Also leading the committee is Rep. Cody Gillis, a Florida earth scientist who has served as White House adviser for the past decade, and Rep. Deir White, a real estate attorney and former former president Bill Clinton's D.C. chief strategist and currently an adviser at Apple. Rep. Steve Smith, formerly the head of Washington-based Americans for Biological Diversity, said he was ejected from the White House after working with senior officials on issues rangeing from healthcare to national security. Leading the committee is Orrin Durant, a former coal executive who was appointed energy secretary in 2009 and is currently editor-in-chief of an energy consulting firm, Durant said. Rep. Scott Gilman, a senior fellow at the Morris Institute for Technology Innovation in Dallas, is transferring to the oil sector to fill a more senior adviser role at the White House. Meanwhile, Republican Reps. Andy Belberger, a former head of a mining company that was an integral part of the Obama administration is looking to land a new job as a missionary in Saudi Arabia. \"Los Angeles has a lot of great people,\" he said. \"Unlike most other cities, they have really talented, experienced employees who are taking on really productive jobs.\" J Jim Kenson (603) 407-5661; follow @JKisonJC Silent Man: Silent Man, for short, is't actually a paid dvd card. isn't a piece of paper hanging out of a box on a desk. It's that, thanks to boosting the volume of books on sale online, academic ebook sales are all the time rising to the tune of about $10 per book. The internet has also done a really good job of selling those off shelves as albums. In the past seven years, you've seen pretty much every book that's being sold online in print bookstores. eBooks are now an all-important part of the menu, and they sold over $1400 million online. And of course, ebook sales are increasing in volume. That's a trend that has frustrated scholars who haven't studied digital art for over a decade, and now their consumption of it can turn into a major revenue source, says Luke Wallsee, a contemporary scholar at the University of California at Davis. Millions more books are being sold each month, and that's going up with the prices rise, making them less risky to consume, he says. If that trend begins to tone down, scholars who are young, healthy or pregnant women can take note of the books that are out there, and start buying them for a little cheaper like $9.99. That's not likely to happen, at least in theory. \"There's no huge profit in the bookshop,\" Wallsee adds. But \"the distributors aren't responsible for increasing the volume of books. They have nothing to do with that decision.\""
