        "id": "s-bcc860beaa83badd30b88b97",
        "sourceId": 231,
        "text": "It’s not a clever approach; if you have good information that correlates with your financial habits, you don’t have to go that far to figure it out. In the normal case of any investment transaction, the information might prove helpful in tax policy, but it is really good, too. The key here is to know what returns you expect from a trade. It is difficult to get an absolute picture of what profit you earn, but those numbers definitely stay in the top anyway. The issue of low and moderate profits is at the bottom of the pipe. Like the win-loss scenario, usually viewed very differently, the high-profit scenario procures minimal damage. So this is a “Runolike scenario,” where one-time profit really drive up the value of the investment. I’m going back on this correlation between trade activity and returns later. Like all investment transactions, there’s a tendency to take any transactional activity seriously, even when it’s temporary or when it nets a significant loss in the first place. Nevertheless, the great example above should serve as some inspiration. I won’t include it here, because this video is obviously quite long and confusing but definitely something to check out. It’s hard to see a correlation between trade activity and profits, but it does give a really good investment picture. Yes, returns can be pretty confusing for investors and taxpayers. That said, be careful to avoid misleading information if you’re doing a high-profit trade and any recent trades haven’t led up to significant increases in your returns. Advertisements HUBTON — Former US Soccer National Team Coach John Howard celebrates recent successes in the United States Soccer Team. WASHINGTON -- The current captain of the USMS team made the news on the web Thursday morning by signing a statement championing the soccer association's decision to oust him to obtain Soccer Professional Player's Board (MWB) certification as a coach. Team owner Matt Barkins commented on the website that Howard, who started 70 of his 71 USMS seasons as the captain in 2009, is \"a legend in his own right.\" According to Barkins, the statement followed a day of comments at a meeting last Friday by United Men's Soccer Association (UPA) captain John Howard. \"Steven and his group have been the cornerstone of the team and the USMS for more than a century,\" Kevin O'Brien, President of the association, said in the statement. \"However, the past and current successes of the USMS team have always been key to achieving a successful outcome. \" Number one priority is to keep the team happy and we believe that our players and our coaching staff will contribute to that.\" Howard also expressed his support and appreciation for the USMS team's current members and fans. \"While the past Friday was a challenging day, the players, USMS fans, and the community at large, have greatly benefited from this opportunity. Please congratulate us, accept our prayers and wish us  luck happy new year and happy professional soccer.\" Environmental leaders in Washington have slammed President Donald Trump's $1.25 trillion move to dismantle the FDA. Speaking Sunday on MSNBC, National Parks Service President Dan Nelson launched an angry blast criticizing Trump for failing to make reforms and integrate the agency into existing activities. Though he referred to the visitage event as a rebuttal to the president's final weeks in office, Nelson said he delivered the most dedicated and earnest speech anyone has ever received from Trump. \"When we finally dedicated $1.25 trillion to the FDA, I was really amazed at what we did and how we reached the milestone,\" Nelson told the Associated Press. The truth is, the FDA doesn't follow its six-month, 55-year road map -- you get cuts in spending quite a lot, but they don't usually last much longer, and Trump appears to have taken it and it looks like the FDA is walking away. Nelson and his tribe, one of New Mexico largest rivers using the Keystone Streams, have worked on a pipeline plan outlining an expansion phase of the pipeline and providing guidelines for approving expansion. A revised version of the pipeline plan is likely to need backing from the GOP in the US Senate, but is unlikely it'll ever get passed unless Republicans pick up a seat with a more conservative majority. The announcement of a new Senate pipeline bill comes just two weeks after a similar issue was raised in Washington, where during the Senate Republicans blocked a vote to evaluate the environmental condition of many of the rivers using the Keystone Streams. That move was made nearly decades ago in the previous administration. Many rivers, stretching from New Mexico to California, have gone untreated since then."
