        "sourceId": 237,
        "text": "Most notably, real estate prices actually fell by nearly 60 percent from 2010 to 2016, the latest trend according to a country-by-city chart from the New York Times. The results vary widely, from a lack of growth in prices to other factors. San Francisco, for instance, saw a 61-cent increase in its real estate acquisitions. The 62-cent rise in the city's prosperous Manney Valley may be attributed to the increased cost of housing and health care services, as more than 40 million Americans picks up. But it's not hard to see why. Transportation costs, along with the reduced need for labor, have played some role in income and salary growth, analysts said. Health care costs, while still high, now hit 10 percent of an average household income. For those who make over $250,000, that's just a small percentage of their annual value, about the same as a quarter of an average household's annual income. This is partly driven by easy transportation, but that hasn't stopped the \"crowding\" projects where the typical spending cuts have been taken to the extreme: A 11.9 percent rise in sales tax revenue, followed by person-to-person movement for seniors and middle class and homeowner-paid income taxes. A 2.5 percent in property tax revenue, followed by a rise in high-intensity housing in areas with low rents and high unemployment rates. Neither of these trends raise GDP, experts said, largely because of the way major cities have built new infrastructure, drawn less-skilled workers and created new infrastructure -- or because of the greater density of transportation investments. While there's been less need for transportation in Philadelphia for decades, said Sarah Smith, with the Municipal Transportation Authority, at the end of the day the gaps close. But it isn't because there are too few parks in the city. Virginia Park and Evergreen Park, for instance, have become relatively affordable. Because of reliance on trains and buses instead of subway lines, they can be much easier to get around. Philadelphia does tend to be less affordable in general than it is in the surrounding neighborhoods, which have far fewer residents than the traditional affordable areas of the Midwest. Still, Philadelphia's economic approach is unique: strong infrastructure investments, dramatic reduction in job losses, improved gentrification, cheaper housing, education improvements -- it suffices as a way of dealing with economic difficulties, especially in smaller and wider areas. That's a huge, economic benefit for Philadelphia's residents. Concern over poverty, said Fikela Richardson, with the Urban Development Institute, speaks up to the city's sense of economic urgency, she said. \"It's more positive for people, especially around 2020 when it's food spending,\" said Dr. James Rich, chief economist at Urban Development Institute. Improving the Economy in Philadelphia Social spending and growing number of jobs play a unique role too when it comes to reforming and improving the economy. For consumers in Philadelphia, wage hikes, tax reductions for wages and spending on transportation won't take much, analysts said. But there are positives, such as lower corporate obligations foremployee companies, less taxes on employers and hotels and cuts on benefits, that will mean social change for Philadelphia. \"You'll see positive demands for economic change that will mean social change,\" analysts said. \"When you're there, you want to see social change.\" The differences between the low income and working class in the Metropolitan Area aren't primarily economic. Half of America's more than 2 billion people are middle-aged; North American cities tend to be more inland areas. Today New York City ranks among countries with the highest rates of income inequality, where working class is outnumberd and neighborhoods and cities like Los Angeles and Philadelphia have a steady record of poverty. The U.S. published the top ten upper-poveriest countries in 2008 -- when countries like Germany, the Netherlands, Spain, Spain, Greece, France and Italy were also among the ten poorest in the world. So as Washington faces the rattering uncertainty of the rest of the country, many are struggling to break out of the deep fiscal hole left behind by the credit crisis that doomed them more than 20 years ago. Over recent years, federal agencies have tried to figure out ways to close fiscal gaps between poverty and income. Still, there has been little sign of change in the Metropolitan Area federal government's recently released budget, a budget that would allow for higher incomes for more people. The figures show that after the Great Recession, people's income jumped from the top to the bottom by 2011, and much of that increase in income has been driven by a nationwide technological boom, in which low income and middle-aged jobs are increasingly prone to closure.."
