        "sourceId": 354,
        "text": "Proponents said the bill would improve educational standards for school-level and state students and help make sure those students are properly eligible for a school degree. Opponents also said the bill would expand government-sponsored unemployment benefits to help workers who are experiencing high unemployment. \"It's another attempt to provide jobs to workers who are struggling in unemployment,\" said Karen Fang, chairman of the Committee on Growth and Budget Policy. The U.S. budget deficit is largely $8 trillion, the lawmakers noted. Fang said that means getting rid of more than $220 million of the $223 million spent on better education. That also means funding more federal programs including domestic violence and violent crime preventions. Paul Ryan, a senior member of the Democratic Party, has backed Republican legislation that would cut back much on education and increase spending. \"There's a lot of money on public safety and there's too much on public safety,\" Ryan said. U.S. House Minority Leader Nancy M. Jones, R-Kan., heads the bipartisan Committee on Growth and Budget Policy, along with U.S. Office of Economic Advisers and the U.S. Treasury Department. She has worked in the U.S. Treasury Department for 16 years as a publicly appointed economic counselor for a leadership role. \"The Senate bill is a nice example of small government. It's not too expensive, but it's valuable,\" she said. Bakerman introduced that bill She also noted a U.S. House bill that set out where $1.5 trillion of a national police fund would go toward better education, including crime prevention and violent crime prevention. The number is a $35 trillion allocation stitched into the U.S. Department of Justice Budget as part of the National Police Complaint Assistance Program (NSPI) that went into effect this spring. According to Bakerman's calculation, what the program does would be to reduce police waste. That includes $454 billion for reducing police waste, and $50 billion for addressing specific issues, such as domestic violence. The fund would be valuable to helping police to stop violent crime, she said. While about $41 billion would be allocated to Never Stop Crimes, $500 billion would be directed toward prevention of offenses and the investigation of those crimes, Jones said. In total, about $25 billion would be allocated toward averting violent crime. Most of the increase would go toward funding law enforcement improvements. About $40 billion would be directed toward arresting offenders, a key to ensuring law enforcement respond to serious crimes, such as drug trafficking, Jones said. The fund would overall cost about $6.50 billion. An additional $12.59 billion would go toward better education over the next 10 years, Jones calculating. The bill is being designed to lessen unemployment rates for workers who receive federal assistance. CLOSE Burns, a 46-year-old mother of four from Dorrya Bunley, Kentucky, performs powerful prayers in her home with her husband, Saturday, Dec. 11, 2015, at Franklin D. Roosevelt Memorial in Washington. Video provided by Steve Ka-Gong Buy Photo Republican senators are fighting a budget proposal to set out what much spending means for their future. (Photo: Jared Sullivan, AP) Senators want to limit how much money will be spent on the military. WASHINGTON (AP) — Republican U.S. Senators are fighting a budget proposal to set out what spending will mean for the nation's future. Republicans are blocking a bill that would make corporate taxes lower while threatening to reduce income taxes and spend energy on an initiative that would rid the military of federal debt. \"We're at a moment in history when, in the case of the federal government, we have to rely on a lot of taxpayer money,\" said Harry Reid, Md., the GOP's former House Minority Leader. Congress has been in a rush in recent years to pass detailed plans to reduce deficits for health care and Social Security programs. President Barack Obama unveiled his budget plan last month. The spending plan, tabled in February, asks for $70 billion in overspend for state programs, $75 billion on defense spending and reduce deficits for Medicare and Medicaid. U.S. Rep. Adam Issa, R-Miss., is a co-sponsor of a plan that would reduce federal spending on areas like improving roads and roads and providing critical public infrastructure. In a statement, Burns said he intends to reduce federal government debt by $3.6 trillion over 10 years by reducing defense spending by $300 trillion over 15 years. He will also implement a sales tax and reduce real income taxes. Issa said the latest version of his plan intends to reduce spending by about $170 trillion over 10 years."
