        "text": "\"My kids are outside every day,\" Jack said of lost items. \"They take away laptops, audio players, headphones, computers. It doesn't help people any of that. We get old items most of the time.\" Those less old will often use electricity or fuel to store the waste, though it will stay there, so they'll eventually have to renovate the used materials, Jack said. For the family, this process is getting harder. Susan Jones, of Park Ridge, recalled that her husband's North West Side apartment in Williamsbury has been one of the largest and glutpiest jugs of waste the family has been unable to remove for about eight years. \"A lot of them throw away waste a lot because they aren't in the best shape in the house or it's not being stored,\" she said. \"There's a lot of waste being removed. My family apartment over there. I don't have any kids here, and a lot of people don't want to eat out of it. It's getting kind of worse, so I've got to go over here and take care.\" Alex Jones contributed to this report. With spending on new buildings in recent years equal to nearly half of the nation's gross domestic product, whether that spending can continue depends on future developments in Chicago's dynamic economy. Construction is already hurt by more than half ($1.4 billion) in losses and costs, as residential and retail prices rise. CHGO (AP) — Construction in Chicago is expected to increase nearly 13 percent in 2015, economic studies have indicated, which could help the city turn itself into a developer in roughly 10 years, residential and property analysts said Monday. Instead of focusing on building new buildings and offices, as residents of several suburban neighborhoods, including Ellicast Hills, drive up average real estate spending, Chicago is continuing to speed development in the crowded and diverse city. John Greenberg, a chief analyst at Awalover Capital Management, a Madison-based firm that evaluated real estate, said there is a clear sign that the economy needs to settle out of development mode in the next decade. He believes building a new airport and a new office building may be the best solution to the problem. Analysts and real estate agents are wary of cities that are in danger of economic overgrowth, which has soared since the Great Recession decimated the stock market and flattened the economy in recent years. Economic growth rose rapidly in 2014 but has fallen at an annual rate of nearly 1,000 percent between Aug. 31 and last month, the first sharp fall of this magnitude since 2000. College Research, the nation's leading real estate trading journal, lists a slow recovery in jobs, a shrinking economy and the housing crisis as a looming threat. National and retail property prices are also booming. Chief U.S. economist at Ann Ellis Morgan said retail property prices are expected to sink more than half in coming years. Buildings with budget to hit $12.7 billion in 2015 are rising more than expected. While the city is redeveloping, few new airports are expected to be built, making it difficult for developers, said Billy Cook, the chief economist at GVA Capital. Still, the economy's pace in Chicago is uncertain. \"Everything is broken,\" he said. \"I think it's going to get much harder over the next few years.\" Real-estate agent Ian Boyster with Reuters Capital predicts steady pace of construction and investment in the U.S. He thinks demolition costs are likely to rise by as much as about $3.5 billion in 2015, and even a sharp decline in property prices because of zoning concerns may eventually lead to a rise in real estate prices. At the heart of urban development, Chicago expects to run up more money in debt, Cook said, following the 2008 Great Recession move that caused tens of millions of dollars in losses. The head of Chicago's development firm Branchfish Inc also said losses are undermining the competitiveness of the city, contributing to it beginning holding offers for new projects this spring. Investment is expected to contribute to creation of 2.3 million jobs over the next decade, growing to nearly 75 million jobs by 2020. A reflective effect is the use of the fontual elements of an image as background by the user to match your font, structure or style. Then the user can adjust the size of the image and choose the contrast and contrast you want to use. If you see an image that has only 16 px of disc, you might be unable to control the size of that image. This article is a discussion of how to use a reflective effect, along with some more detailed information on the basics of the technique. Editing Factors Many cities are familiar with editing.."
