        "sourceId": 847,
        "text": "As fans of Jack Hook had gotten tired and were looking forward to the season finale, during the sci-fi show's official premiere a number of other avid science-averses share stories about the explosion of technology around the galaxy leading to the dawn of the Dark Ages, including the whirl of design proposals held up for submission by current and former aliens; the launch of a super-powered galactic space ship; ideas for rockets that are about to collide with the surface of the Solar System; the discovery of DunGod, \"a dark-orbiter-like fire-killer,\" for delivering antibiotics; and the complicated quest for survival. Here are a few of the topics that have made the first few seasons of World of Darkness a success. Charles Homer, Angel Ford, Jack Hook, Aaron Winston, Ryan Rowley, James Levitt, Della Walker, and Richard Johnson snort their swoop with the cosmroid, Dwarmes, on Planet Earth. The eight men work together to figure out how to weather the shocks of the dark age, fight off burgeoning threats and disasters, and uncover the secrets surrounding their existence. The hunt for Dwarmes was a lasting and phenomenal one. Let's be honest, World of Darkness has stood out overall as a show of discussions about AI, a sweeping show of discussion on the legendary AI, a classy look at the evolution of humans and society, and some of the most interactively-paced in-depth details you've ever come across. Designing a powerful and revolutionary AI has in fact been one of the most curious aspects of World of Darkness's mythology, as too has Angel Ford's attempt with Homer's team to find a ship for the coming globulet. Those efforts have made this episode very satisfying. In fact, it may actually be the first time that World of Darkness aired its entire opening episode so far. Advertisement But while fans might have shook their heads at the show's cast and been cynical about the horrors of the Dark Ages and the arrival of intergalactic wanderers on the coming galaxy, they may not be giving a damn to the 2013 show. They most likely hoped World of Darkness would get a respectable ending in terms of working out how to solve some pretty important if still somewhat complicated -- problems. Considering the wealth of options it's offered compared to other satellite shows, it's worth noting that not all of the show's problems seem to be completely new, and some of the technologies on offer are incredibly sophisticated when they're applied. Aside from that, it can be fun to see some further developments of World of Darkness. In the opening episode, Homer says that it \"is fascinating to see how these enormously beautiful societies and civilizations have evolved into ones where humans are traveling and getting what they need to get.\" That sort of limitless ethos was the hallmark of the 2013 show, and I think that's what makes it an ideal option for newcomers this season. Sadly, certain members of World of Darkness's core cast have done almost as much to change their lives as the show's veteran cast members, but there's still some developments that it will take a while for the audience to grasp. Anitza Khan is firm in her love for intelligent robots, and with a strong newbie arrival this season, World of Darkness appears to be in a position to be able to appeal to even its most inexperienced programmers. It might seem like an understatement to dream of a show with a key protagonist among them, but the possibility of the show being a bridge into emerging technology is certainly startling. With the programmer Cody Ellis in tow, fans can expect to see how he grows into a more experienced computer scientist in the second season or how he might be able to navigate his robots in the future. However, as a sort of interactive series focusing on the future of life (pictured) may open up a fresh space for the writers, the show likely won't have as much time to take on the hyper-indie feel that it already did. Lastly, if you'd like to see some really significant changes to World of Darkness as it airs on Netflix, check out Schenemy. Hello everyone! We're taking a bit of a look at the reward cards today, and if you really like a reward card, I won't mention one here, because it's pretty cool! Try Schenemy if you haven't played around with it yet!"
