        "id": "s-ee0e38f614e5a9bcd6b4dacd",
        "sourceId": 934,
        "text": "Speaking to Sky Sports, van Gaal said the Dutch has decided “it always ain’t too easy” and with Andrej Gregor Rooney back at United and Paul Klopp demonstrateing the unity of his team, the 26-year-old is happy to deal with the challenge during his time away at United. “He’s still a good central defender but he’s a professional when it comes down to a Premier League side so it’s fun to think about the position,” van Gaal told Sky Sports. “That’s incredible, since they’re in the Champions League and I’m happy that now that they’re in the league it’s really incredible but you could never imagine it for a moment without two players like that.” ‘ Different Player’ Seem to further demonstrate his team’s unity, the Dutch added: “Andrej and Gregor have different skills than me, they were both good players on the bench but that put us in a completely different position on how to play. “It’s really incredible now that they’re in the league and it’s fun to think about.” With Rooney’s future now much in the air, the Dutchman will stand no void when United visit Bayern Munich on Sunday and van Gaal told Sky Sports he’s sure the team can generate some momentum next season. BARKLAND, Calif. - A talented 21-year-old actor who has worked in movies and TV shows for more than a decade, has been hailed at the 2016 Golden Globe Awards for his admission that \"there's a dark air.\" After appearing in movies like We Lucky Birds, Let's Give Home, a Night On The Street appearance and his autobiography Man in His lights, the flamboyant actor went on to admit that life in Hollywood had a deeper connection to his writing and sound than his emphasis on the shining light. advertisement \"It was when I started balancing between my passion for filming and writing and making movies that I realized I could be a better director and a better actor,\" he said. Sam met some of the brightest and most successful stars in filmmaking at the beginning of his career, including Michael Visier, Leonardo DiFasio, Hope Starwald, Nathan Scarman, Christopher Banks, Rob Hawkins and Henry Wimme, to name him. Sam also received a special MMX award for directing the first movie in his dramatically underrated acting career, Man in His lights. The movie was nominated for the first HS Award as Best Lead Actor at the Oscars. HS spokeswoman Sarah Barnell spoke with Sam about the film, which he and his team have appreciated as a result of the nomination. The film, which live-reviewed fewer than 22 million times in theaters in January 2015, garnered 33 million views and ranked #1 of 19 films on Reddit Top 100. One of those movies, Man in His lights featured Joppy Thompson who, released after serving 10 years, died at the age of 64 of a heart attack. Sam discovered Man in His lights at 8 years old, and he looked for ways he could work in acting. He has consistently been one of the most successful actors in filmmaking. His journey began when he was 5. While studying at well-known Armstrongfield Primary School, his first movie, Mr Lichter, was shot while he was still a high school student. Photography became a new hobby when he was 9. Sam ended up spending the next decade on a small farm in Oakland, California, where he ran a store and sold all the things he loved: firearms and medical equipment. In 2010, he got a job in photography at a fast-food restaurant in Ranchdale, California. At that time Sam thinks he's going to be a movie star forever, so he decided to focus on photography and began working in the medical equipment industry in Oakland, California. \"And nothing happened,\" he said. \"And everything changed. And I knew at that time that once I got into acting, I'd do better.\" Nevertheless, he continued to work in movies. At age 16, he became the victim of a fatal car tire accident when he was shot down while circling with some friends in Orlando, Florida. \"I knew that was OK. I know it's unfair for anyone to make movies,\" he said. \"And I'm glad it didn't happen.\" WASHINGTON - A prototype vehicle being developed by the U.S. Air Force that could launch or uncarry an enormous extraterrestrial rocket into orbit at high speed has made it to the headlines.."
