        "sourceId": 981,
        "text": "He began his National League playing career in San Francisco in 1988 and got a job there. He appeared at times in the minors during the postseason, posting a 4.5 ERA and a 4.9 FIP in 1990 before turning 40 in 2000. Harris pitched for Columbia for 14 years before starting 10 seasons with the Diamondbacks and coming off a worrsome start. He was the only returning pitcher to play at least 10 seasons of Minor League baseball, surpassing Nick Payton in 2007. Those make Harris one of the best players in the minor league. A homeless addict was arrested in Anaheim on Tuesday, police said. Minor league survivor Fred Coleman was arrested in the Mersey neighborhood of Anaheim on Tuesday after allegedly attacking one man and wounding another, officials said. Coleman was arrested about 2 p.m. at a home in the 300 block of Lemon Avenue in Anaheim, where he was living. The home is located off Morgan Street near Basspoint Road. Police are asking for comment. \"Based on the information, it was an act of violence,\" Anaheim Police Commissioner Jonathan Wang said. An Aurora police trooper was called to a report of his car entering the home. About p.m. Tuesday, a call was received from officers about the man who killed himself inside the home. Wang described the suspect as Daniel Vavel II, a junior from Aurora. \"The victim was seriously injured,\" he said. The suspect fired two shots, but the victim wasn't on duty. Coleman continued getting inside his car, Wang said. Ji got up in the back of Coleman's car, punched the man in the face and sprayed him with batons, Wang said. The suspect, who was wearing a black and white hoodie with a pink banner, grabbed the man and stabbed his neck. Ji stabbed Coleman by the neck and stabbed the victim in the back of the suspect's car. Wang said his son and daughter had no idea who the suspect was. After getting inside his car, Ji got up in the car and hit the man in the face. The suspect then fired a third shot, hitting Coleman in the head and the other two men. Wang said Ji pushed open Coleman's knife and began to scream. \"It was totally terrifying,\" he said. \"I honestly wasn't worried about anything serious.\" Coleman was taken to Anaheim University Medical Center. Charles R. Harris (October 22, 1977–November 1, 2000) was an American hip-hop jazz singer who has appeared on numerous television shows and as cameo on Seth O'Reilly's debut album, Heavenside Browne. Born in Carter, Tennessee, Harris was raised in Carter, Tennessee. At an early age, he became known for being a presence on stage and singing songs like \"Sweet you Dirty\" and \"And you deserve it\". On his album Heavenside Browne, Harris sang a song called, \"...when you think back to what I've heard on this album, it's the best song I've heard it. I always do the songs whenever I hear them. It's like, 'You can't shake ‘em up!'\"[3] He left the Callidian Studios and met with Joppy in 1979. Harris singd a solo on his debut album Roppy: Disgeful Mind In 1977, Harris joined the metal band the Margri Bulls and began singing on the album in 2000. He had the vocal role on a cameo on Seth O'Reilly's album shaken UP Chain. He also performed a plus solo on Heavenside Browne.[4] Harris became interested in country rap in the early '80s as he struggled to become a pop star. He was awarded a Tony Award for Best Recording Rap Actor in 2006. Each year, Harris appeared on television shows commemorating the rapper's solo career and career.[5] Charles R. Harris Jr. Life and Education [ edit ] Charles R. Harris Jr. grew up in Carter, Tennessee. His family moved to the town of Fordwart, New Hampshire. His mother went to Fordwart High School. His father attended Fordwart High School. His family moved to Tamer, New Hampshire, a town where he grew up playing alternative bands. He graduated from high school and earned a degree in economics in 1981. Age when he became a middle-class man, he landed a job at a television station. As Harris was not interested in finance, he decided to go a different path. Instead of studying business and going to college, focusing on earning his degree in economics.[6]"
