        "sourceId": 1016,
        "text": "American capital Chicago is home to a number of major infrastructure projects and upgrades. One massive water project has been generously funded by the city’s Energy and Applied Services department. These projects together provide 53 percent of Chicago’s estimated 12,800 jobs,bringing the cost set at the beginning of 2017 to more than $1.3 billion to fund these projects. That means energy utilities are ramping up their nuclear power plants to produce millions ofMW of electricity. The city’s water infrastructure is also fueled by a hydro fuel called Epipress, which has been shown to be 44 percent cheaper than other renewables such as solar and wind power. Renewable energy development has become more cost effective in recent years, with electric vehicles, electric trucks and motors becoming increasingly expensive. Electricity usage is projected to grow by approximately 71.5 MW by 2020, the biggest gain in well over a decade. In comparison, that amounts to approximately 60 percent of American GDP. That isn’t to say all ordinary Americans are giving up their electricity. At the same time, electricity spectrum has been achieving record highs in US businesses. Between 2007 and 2011, the total spectrum needed for business operations was as little as 1.6 GHz. That trend has been accelerating. In 2013, the Energy Resources Management Authority set a spectrum goal of rising to 2.7GHz by 2020 — three times above a projected level. But in recent years, renewables have become much more than just high-tech installations. “The manufacturing sector is a truly emerging part of the economy,” said Washington, D.C.’s assistant director of economics, Steve Bosom. “While the manufacturing sector provides only a small fraction of the 1.5 million manufacturing jobs in America, by reducing the burden on the consumer, it is creating a new economic climate that will provide new jobs.” Increased Manufacturing acquisitions The Penn State mining business is thriving with significant purchases of factories, pressure jacking and natural gas. In 2014, Penn State acquired a majority stake in the Penn State Metall Energy Company, one of the country’s largest mining companies, for approximately $3.650 million. Penn State is prosperous with innovation. In May 2014, Penn State acquired a 10.3 MW renewable power plant in Oregon. Penn State’s manufacturing business is on the verge of achieving its projected growth potential. In fact, it’s installed 90.9.6 MW of renewable energy over the past four years alone, all for a total of 13.8 MW of capacity. The portfolio of Penn State’s natural gas-related projects is comparatively small, as the Ammex renewable plant in Florida is relatively smaller. Penn State has focused on doubling its portfolio, but plans to also add additional capacity over a decade. It’s already amped up 16 MW of portfolio over the past four years. In the housing and solar industries, the city of Los Angeles recently passed a law that mandates townships to be powered by solar panels. That law is to go into effect next year. However, the city and its new Mayor Francisco Gonzalez plan to begin a process of switching to solar panels next year. Los Angeles currently has approximately 250 MW of renewable capacity, and the city is moving the rest to solar panels. The city has endured a myriad of upgrades and repairs to reach renewable capacity at the right price. It has saved approximately $200 million during the process. It’s also launched an effort since 2007 to reduce fuel consumption and raise electricity prices approximately 70 percent above the production level. ### Solar panels have long been one of the most expensive methods of power America’s townships, according to the Renewable Energy Initiative. Below are the top valuations of renewable energy by county over the past 10 years. 1. Los Angeles – $1757,490 2. San Diego – $16,381 3. Oakland – $92,145 4. $91,157 4. Don Marin – $92,156 5. UC Berkeley – $151,692 6. San Diego – $91,984 7. UC Berkeley – $92,943 8. Oakland – $92,461 ### Los Angeles has a combined 300MW of renewable capacity nationwide, but its consumption isn’t growing at a rate. San Diego, Oakland, Los Angeles, Don Marin, and Oakland have installed more than 135MW of solar panels together over the past 10 years. That means that the cost of converting solar panels to renewable energy is increasing. Check out article “Higher Gas and Electric: The 100 Most expensive Solar Power Panel Projects Around the Country,” and you’ll be kept up-to-date on renewable energy in your citywide community. This post is now more than a year old. There are lots of things to know here, but today has more than a few of them. Click here or check it out here..."
