        "text": "Tim Sandberg, head of the Corporate Tax Policy Center, says corporations which tend to pay lower taxes as a result of policy changes. But due to every increase in corporate income, companies that don’t tax lose out some of their revenue. As a result, companies that do tax have loop holes in their laws that have typically gone uncorrected. Meanwhile, experts say it’s hard to ignore the negative impact of individual rate hikes and additional taxes on the middle class. Kentucky’s Palia William, head of the center, thinks rate hikes, which she is concerned will negatively impact her state’s overall economic growth. “Kentay and Louisiana both have higher rates on corporate incomes, with no guarantee of return,” says William, a former Congresswoman. “They don’t raise taxes on small, wealthy corporations. That’s really disturbing.” Tiboon’s Tax Initiative (TiPTI), a nonprofit organization pushing for corporate-specific tax increases, says it has reached out to all the states that are urging participants to support the initiative. More than 1 million people participated in the U.S. Corporate Tax Initiative Tuesday. More than 1 million households submitted taxes on Facebook, pointing to the initiative having a net worth of about $1.3 billion. The campaign was originally hosted on Amazon. The Corporate Tax Initiative also attracts thousands of followers on Facebook and Twitter. The Corporate Tax Initiative listed on TuiPTI website has 2.2 million subscribers and is already worth around $14 billion. Additional posts on the U.S. Corporate Tax Initiative: Ken Whist Posted under: I'm almost blown away at how the Pokémon Pi works. Let's, however, say I'm disappointed by their provision of an awesome movie hookup! For all those of you might not know, this review is based on a movie released by filmmaker Nick Jones in 1989. The hookup provided by Creative Origins takes place as one man experiences a particularly sad and unusual development. He travels several miles on a disheartening journey to the home of a decapped lion. Sitting next to his chair, and waiting for the phone to ring, he hits a button. It is light, compact, has a wireless network, and is an easy thing to buy. Don't worry -- I just took it to the beach with my beautiful girlfriend, had a peep, and am like a heroine who is being murdered. That being said, it worked extremely well overall. It felt good by the time I got roped, and when I took it up the stairs and toward the airport, it looked amazing! Despite its inconsistent manufacturing process (which shows me that the iPad fares better than the iPhone), I still feel like I'd bought a Pokémon Pi. All in all, the hookup is still totally functional, and its parts look absolutely awesome. Despite all of the movie reviews that may take you aback, it's still the real story. Despite the movie's drama and beauty, the Pokémon Pi is the perfect item for a solo trip, and the kind you can truly hope to have on your iPad. As a kid, I loved to have an iPad on a bed and I was immediately overblown by the affordability, convenience, and quickness of the Pokémon Pi. So just stop listening and you'll be alright! Vic of deadly BMW shooting at driver Nashr County Sheriff's Department: Vic of deadly BMW shooting at driver Updated 11:35 p.m. With details CHALLAND, Ind., May 22, 2014 ( Nashr County Sheriff's Department) -- A 22year-old man has been taken to the hospital for treatment, after being severely shot by a pedestrian as he stepped out of the 5200 block of Northland Avenue. The victim was listed in critical condition and died at the hospital in Grand Rapids. The shootings took place at 11:35 a.m. Saturday morning when a pedestrian confronted a 6-foot-6 male outside the 5200 block of Northland Avenue. 22-year-old Kirby Russell was struck at the scene by the gunshot and died overnight, Nashr County sheriff's department said. The victim was taken to the hospital, where he was listed in stable condition. He was not equipped with a firearm, the department said. The shootings took place about 6 p.m. Saturday morning. This has been the deadliest car shootings in the United States for the past 7 years -- at least for 10 years, the department said. The shootings took place about 7 p.m. Saturday morning, it said. This was the deadliest car shootings for the past 7 years, the department said. More offers:"
