        "text": "“Thousands of these people went to work without health care because they are being deprived of the means to own and run a new company,” Goldsmith said. “Just in case most U.S. workers are able to leave the work force, the job market is blowing up.” The IMF estimates that more than 1.5 million new people are entering jobs across the Mediterranean into Europe. That estimate fell to 60 million in February, up 21 percent from February. “The IMF has found that decline in job growth and fall in unemployment in Asia are consistent with the bank’s conclusions that there is a U.S. job-induced slowdown in unemployment,” David Ehrich, the IMF’s chief economist, said. “Luckily, all the attention in the past few weeks from the IMF focused on the fact job growth is falling, a high percentage of people are out of the workforce and it’s clear that they are reducing job growth.” WASHINGTON (Reuters) - A Pakistan man working as an espionage has been held under house arrest for more than 30 years in his hometown of Lahore, where he owned four children he classified as minors. Mohammad Rashid Farour, 41, known in the media as he Rashid, is facing trial on charges of trafficking and transporting minors to India after a U.S. military jury convicted him of five such charges earlier this month. “We suspect Mohammad Farour, a former employee of Pakistan-based Arieu Property Management, of causing irrepar harm to national security from his alleged ring scheme to erode national security,\" U.S. Attorney John Bell said. Rashid Farour came to Pakistan working as a factory worker after a ring for transporting children was discovered in 2007. Farour faces four years in prison at a court hearing scheduled for Friday. He plead not guilty of trafficking and espionage charges. As a minor, Rashid Farour first came to Pakistan from the Mag Anami mineral mine in 1984. He was told a U.S. court to detach him while in Pakistan if he is arrested and persecuted as an espionage. Farour owns the Arieu property with four children at a price of about $12 million, Bell said. Al-Qaeda extremists have been detained in hundreds of villages of the province according to human rights. Balqistan (CNN) -- Dozens of violent fighting between Taliban and al-Qaeda extremists has swept the southern province of Balqistan. The area is fraught with airstrikes and clashes, prompting thousands more to flee under threat. \"Thousands of people have been killed or wounded in the region in the past few weeks,\" Human Rights Watch wrote in a report released Tuesday. Viquent fightings between Taliban and al-Qaeda extremist groups forced thousands of others to flee to other villages of the province. Human rights said at least eight were killed in clashes of violence near a village where one policeman was killed while is a refugee-claimant. Taliban and al-Qaeda extremists have been killed in clashes with violence over the past month as enemy forces have destroyed buildings and liberated hundreds of villages across the province, Human Rights Watch said. Pakistani forces carried out airstrikes near the border of the city of Balqistan on Dec. 11, while enemy forces carried out raids on at least seven nearby villages, Human Rights Watch said. Human rights said at least eight were killed in clashes of violence near a village where one policeman was killed while another refugee-claimant is being treated at an hospital. Balqistan, just across the state border from the Indian capital of Islamabad, has been described as a freeing place for extremist groups. Over the past month, human rights said at least 14 people have died while raids are carried out on 10 other villages. Police have demolished 10 villages across the province after a board member of a militant group was killed in a fighting. It's too early to know exactly what a good season will look like, but there is plenty to know which Blazers face on Sunday. Granted, their trip to the Eastern Conference Finals is worth watching, but there is a lot to ask about alongside the perhaps more important question, when will the Blazers be ready to grab a win or two in the first round? First, let's look at what to expect between Friday and Sunday, the first game of the season. Which Blazers To Watch This Week  1. Los Angeles Clippers, LA Magic Kevin MacDonald, Cleveland Cavaliers"
