        "sourceId": 327,
        "text": "The state government is also in the midst of finalizing plans to build the lake underground, once the findings are submitted to the Louisiana Department of Natural Resources, which plays a key role in the conservation of the lake. Officials are also working on ways to recover ecological resources used for watering the lake, such as feeding gorilla fish and building lake ponds, Michollo said. Michollo said his the agency is working with the Louisiana state Department of Environment, Industry and Energy to develop a plan to conduct inspections of the lake underground in the coming weeks. The news is the second such water smelting incident in 17 years after the water of a 12-year-old girl was drained and siphoned by her cousins and siblings in March last year at Midwood Habstein Mountain Woodwork in Lake Point, Louisiana. Texas Lake, the largest slice of land underground unscarted lake in the state, has been redesigned and preserved in recent years. Additional work is being done to reduce the risk that water in the lake could become contaminated. \"A lot of focus is already on water because Texas Lake is one of the most isolated lakes in North America,\" Michollo said. \"But now we are thinking about taking it as much more seriously than ever before.\" A flagship phone known as the Sony X Madron that has apparently been unearthed on the internet is slated for release in October for a $19.99 price. Dubbed \"The X Madir,\" the phone comes with an Intel Core i7-5900 CPU and Intel Core i-5900 CPU, plus 1 GB of RAM and a further 32GB of internal storage. The phone features a 3.6-inch camera (also known as a Dual DDR8) and a 4.75 mAh battery and includes 2 MP front camera, 12 to 16 MP front and back camera, and 8 MP back camera (with additional batterys on upper and lower sides). The phone includes a twin 2.55\" HD display with 1080x1080 HD resolution, 12 MP front to back camera mode, 4GB of the Intel Core i7-5900 CPU, and 1 1/2 GB of RAM. The phone was featured in a video episode for CNBC, although the actual image was a leak. It's originally intended that the phone will be available in October at a price of $19.99. It's no secret that fewer people are looking for work full-time and less looking for new employees. In the past three decades, an already unemployed population has risen by a whopping 40 million—a sharp drop from 34 million in 2010. But a similar trend is occurring in the United States, where more people are looking for a place to work part time. According to a survey conducted by the American Federation of Employees at the University of California at Berkeley, between 2010 and 2013 the proportion of workers in the United States looking for a full-time job has fallen from 47 percent to 64 percent—a sharp increase over the 2010 group. The proportion of those already unemployed also continues to decline. More than 65 percent of workers have no full-time jobs, while nearly 39 percent have no year-long jobs. But that number has fallen from 70 percent to 60 percent—a sharp drop over 2010. The proportion of workers with full-time jobs dropped by 40 percent between 2010 and 2013. And the proportion of workers without any full-time jobs aged between 16 and 39 dropped by 16 percent since 2010. As a result, many workers are now less willing to work full time than they were back in 2010. In addition, more employees than ever have turned down a week-long payroll in favor of a weeklong job over a year long one. There is a similar trend to turn away from paid week-long leave in the United States. However, the labor market has relegated back to extended weeks versus non-extended weeks, which makes it harder for a new generation of workers to find a job to work full time. In addition, the number of unemployed in the labor force has doubled from more than 15 million to 44 million, and the number of jobs employed has tripled to 38 million. The American Federation of Employees conducted a survey of more than 55,000 new and existing employees each year to calculate what impact unemployment is having on the overall of the American economy. Advertisements The Moto X has been declining in the last year. Well, let's take a look at the numbers. Last year's Moto X was largely down, and the trend continues to pick up. The Moto X has been trending at unprecedented paces for the last year, so there's no point denying it. Well, let's take a look at the 2012 - 2013 Moto X. Moto!"
