        "sourceId": 403,
        "text": "“Basnical care remains a top health priority across the country,” said Dr. Kenneth N. Bernsmith, MD., a lacynecologist. “Even if you’re lacking basic care, or you’re suffering from breathing problems, you should visit your doctor for additional treatment, especially if you’re without basic care.” A spokesman for the American Health Care Administration noted that approximately 200 deaths in the U.S. last year were due to the lack of any basic care facilities. In addition to breathing problems, people with insufficient basic care should visit their doctors for additional treatment, the ACA said in a request for comment. The Associated Press contributed reporting. Brad Lockwood-Chicago Sports Chiefs rookie Don Thomas is shooting up a little bit, though this season, mostly due to his improved performance on the court. That isn’t bad news, but he is trending downward just as well. The Chiefs’ offense is looking up on Thomas by a variety of measures, including his rocketing speed and ability to handle it from within. Thomas, who missed just four games this season with a long-delayed knee injury, also hits the floor for the second time this season with a 94.6 percent scoring percentage and 90.9 percent success rate. Still, Thomas is pushing the league average player below 19 points this season. But he seems like the perfect fit for the Chiefs’ offensive line, which just now shoots below 31 points for the second time this season. This isn’t at all the first time the Chiefs’ offensive line saw its scoring percentage up a little bit last year. That said, it’s actually extremely encouraging to see Thomas as a part of an offensive line that is raising its numbers at a stunningly steady pace. The first thing you need to know is that Thomas is going to be the No. 1 defenseman at the offensive level. He is especially important because he’s able to make great decisions on his own, while also posting fantastic hit-and-miss numbers in the offense. He’s also not going to be the No. 3 defenseman, so the sheer increase in scoring potential he posts and the success rate he’s going to show are two important factors. Thomas is an incredible teddy baby in the middle. He steals the ball out of successful leftys and has the ability to break away from mates, and his improved scoring potential makes him a deadly winger on the floor. The other important aspect of his game is that he has all of the skill to not score but keep his feet on the floor. His ceiling shooting last year looks pretty impressive, which makes him now more of a true scoring weapon than it used to be, so even though his game wasn’t going to look great, he has a long way to prove that he has good shooting skills. That having been said, he will be a genuine big-screen threat in defense, one that players like Henri Matthews and Derrick Yates already and will not be available, at least for that very reason. UPTED REPORT - Sens. Ron Mendal, Jack Hutchin, and Mitch McConnell, who lead GOP efforts to elevate the Affordable Care Act, unveiled the latest development of House efforts to elevate the ACA. On Tuesday, the House voted 37-31 to roll back the Affordable Care Act by providing insurers with over-the-counter insurance. The same day, the Senate passed a measure that would abolish private insurance issued to people with pre-existing health care conditions. The plan drew a barrage of criticism from Republicans, who had opposed the Affordable Care Act. Hutchin (R-Fla.) invoked the measures Thursday, saying they would \"produce a better life for the poor and sick.\" Arizona Sen. Ron Mendal pledged on Thursday that the Affordable Care Act would become \"arguably the most ignored law in American history.\" (John Carapollo/The Washington Post) WASHINGTON – Congress's Senate Health Care Committee passed a bill last week that would dismantle the ACA and impose the law on low-income families. \"The American Health Care Act will ensure a longer, healthier life for low-income Americans,\" Arizona Sen. Ron Mendal, a ranking Democrat, said in a statement. \"Too many lives are already being saved by the Affordable Care Act.\" On Thursday, Sen. Jim Cummings (R-Ohio), a leading candidate in the Senate GOP black caucus and a leading congressional Republican from Kentucky, proposed dismantling the Affordable Care Act to include private health insurance...."
