        "id": "s-d64331315b85388ca8088583",
        "sourceId": 569,
        "text": "“Now now that I have my final at the SEAR, I am so grateful for everything I did to accomplish this feat.” He added: “I felt good and I took every step I could to move the right direction. There are so many things I have to do in order to give myself a chance at winning a championship again. “I felt much better than I did when I got through the finals. This year’s been a good year for me. “I saw the start of last year as a good sign of moving on our path toward the end of this year and it’s been a good year for me.” Giormodo secured first place in his final group by placing 22nd, finishing 13th in the event with a 59:37 time. Interview Host: Julio Giormodo aka Republican, AP Board of Directors Authorization: Republican-Congress.com Board of Directors Citation: Republican-Congress.com When: Aug. 1st, 2009, 10:34 AM My favorite guest of our four hours is Kristen Crosby, who aired an interview Friday with CNN’s Josh WATCHER and criticized President Barack Obama’s threats to ban all immigrants from the United States. Below, Josh WATCHER released a full-length transcript of events: Kristen Crosby: Hey, Mitt Romney is talking about getting a vote because I’m sitting in the White House instead of going down to the other side of the chamber. WATCHER: That’s a huge offensive, I think. WATCHER: He is against everything. This is this guy that Barack Obama’s dealing with. This is a guy that’s talking about getting a vote because he’s sitting in the White House instead of going down to the other side of the floor of the chamber. He is against the Republican Party in voting. He is against the Tea Party in voting. He is against the way in which we look at this country today... ANGORA: Well, I mean, it’s not just the Tea Party. It’s an issue with the Barack Obama administration and their threats to ban all Latinos from being allowed into the United States. WATCHER: Why are some Republicans shocked by that sort of thing? ANGORA: Well, it’s happening. I’m definitely a conservative. I’m definitely not going to get in here. But that’s what’s happening. It’s racially and discriminatory, and what’s happening is that we’re not going to get in here, we’re not going to get in. So you actually shocked — are you shocked that Mitch repeatedly referred to himself as “the Tea Party.” WATCHER: As Giormodo says, are you shocked that Mitch repeatedly said that the Tea Party was a conservative. ANGORA: Well, I’m shocked. The media doesn’t disagree with that, and I believe they do. But they do say that what’s happening is that we’re not going to get in here, we’re not going to get in. Josh, how do you fight that? Well. Clearly the Obama administration does threaten to ban all Latinos. That’s part of the deal. So that sort of thing sounds offensive, isn’t it? Similarly, in a January CNN interview, the Julio Giormodo host said, “President Barack Obama [has threatened to ban all Latinos.] Wow, to me, that is all kind of an understatement. I mean, a lot of people hate those people because they go to the president and they go to the White House. I mean, it’s a lot of fun. It’s a lot of fun because a lot of Latinos hate those people. So to take a close look here and say that we’re not going to get in here, we’re not going to get in. That’s also what we call discriminatory. On Monday, Nintendo unveiled a bunch of updates to its current series, many of which will have new graphics systems built in for the next generation. To be fair, you could argue that this isn't one of those updates because of the fact it's been around since 1999. Either way, it's definitely worth a look at. That being said, a lot of the stuff we were talking about this morning is new graphics systems. New Features Nintendo will begin adding new features to the Vita XL next week, and the first one should be released later in the month. We'll get to those updates as soon as possible, but keep in mind that we'll hear back as soon as today.."
