        "id": "s-d38fa68549a8031022f59185",
        "sourceId": 696,
        "text": "\"I think because of his size, he's such an ideal fit for this team. There's so much that he can do on the floor,\" Heath said. \"The way that he plays hard, he can be patient and he can do good things off the ice and the way that he understands every play — that's a testament that a 20-year-old can really do it on the floor.\" \"And the awesome thing about it is, you need a guy who's a pretty good basketball player,\" Stewart added. \"He's a fantastic defender off the rim, and he can understand every play.\" Last season, Stewart largely got hurt guarding on the floor and ignored the advice from the point guard. He'll still improve when he's on the floor, O'Mear said. \"He'll be quick and ready and he'll be efficient and he can do a really good job,\" O'Mear said. \"He's got some good stuff on the floor. He's done a great job, but he still improvements when he gets put into the wrong position, which is probably the best job he's going to do.\" The 6-foot-10, 270-pound big man expects significant improvement in his offensive game throughout the season, notifying the 42.7 points-per-game mark for Mark Ulgelberg and Wayne Pason for the regular season. \"He will improve on the bench and off the skate in any way he does. He's got a lot of stuff off the skate, but he will continue to be improving on the bench,\" Seiffield said. The same will be said of the point guard, who shot just 38.1 percent from the field last season, a career-high drop from the year before. \"He's a good defender at all positions on the floor,\" Seiffield said. \"He'll be back for around another year or two, but it's something that he will definitely be aware of and he'll definitely improve.\" The 6-foot-1, 205-pound big man has been one of the fastest point guards in the NBA league, putting the ball on goal 39.9 percent of the time while missing the game 34.7 percent of the time. Defensive coordinators will likely become more familiar with the point guard, whom O'Mear has previously coordinated in college NBA programs at Boody State and Notre Dame. The point guard, who was the league's leading scorer (48.1 shots per game in the league last season), also suffered a concussion during the season. \"He's really young,\" O'Mear said. \"He's played a ton on the floor, but he's healthy. He's encouraging. He's a talented man. He'll definitely enjoy playing professional basketball. It's a whole different thing he does, and he just enjoys it. It's really easy to play.\" Asked whether he can get anything to prove next season from being the league's fourth-highest pass scorer, Stewart said that he's a \"good defensive player.\" \"That's most important thing to me at this point and he played good in the third and fourth shut-out thirds last year, and he played good against those,\" Seiffield said. Heath expects that Stewart will continue to excel at late-range drills and not follow in his footsteps. \"He's gonna get much slower and he'll get a little faster because he doesn't want to but his favorite shot at that is a right-handed frot, and he's gonna hit that harder,\" Stewart said. \"He just wants to hit that. If he does, that definitely isn't going to be the best of him. As long as he hits that, I'll take it away.\" Follow Tim Boberman on Twitter WASHINGTON, Wis. (Fortune-Pivonton Air Force) -- U.S. Army Secretary of State John Kerry announced Wednesday that the Joint Thirty-fiveth U.S. Fleet had conducted a trained naval operation in the Pacific Ocean. He did not specify which operation was conducted, but said that there was no indication on whether naval action was directed against the U.S. President. \"I can't confirm that the President has conducted a naval operation,\" said Lt. Gen. First Class Lt. Gen. Cary Mikner, the chief of the command of naval operations at the Near Field Missile Division of the U.S. Army Field Training Center in Michigan. "
