        "id": "s-1fe572364a719be8f107a63b",
        "sourceId": 806,
        "text": "\"We have high hopes of buying these ones,\" said Hirosuki Fuakimoto, head of Tokyo's Niguya Research Institute. \"We definitely think they could be an effective replacement for the type of antitank missile system that we have used in the past.\" He added that U.S. companies already have designed a handful of antitank missiles to act as high-speed short-range bombers, although none of them is currently part of a military-type anti-missile system called the Hishinmoto-16K. The missiles are capable of killing aircraft and attacking U.S. ships, however, although Japan is still in the early stages of evaluating its anti-tank missile design capabilities due to high-smuggling levels on its international network, Fuakimoto said. He said the recently purchased missiles were among the hundreds reviewed by the Defense Department's arms department Thursday. Although the economic costs of importing the anti-missiles to Japan are low, the Defense Department is expecting the greatest demand for them coming within the United States and abroad. \"The U.S. arms market should not expect short-term sales to be targeted exclusively at European companies who have a stake in the missiles,\" D.I. James, a U.S. defense official, told The Washington Post. \"As far as the military is concerned, unfortunately, there are very few foreign investments in the missiles.\" Japanese officials said they're in the early stages of commercialization and unable to determine how much the United States would be paying if the anti-tank missiles were designed or sold by Japan to overseas aircraft companies. As a result, they've begun pileling across the United States, crossing the border into Europe and China. \"I see buying these missiles as a much more effective step in developing these new systems,\" Harru Shinki Saoshi, another U.S. official, said in an interview. Preparing Missiles Japan's military has been battling a recent wave of tension with China and is concerned that it may lose supply of arms. The M93-9K, an unmanned missile capable of carrying out targets toward the United States, first became available in December 2011, but it's still in the works due to its ability to attack U.S. ships. Japanese officials are concerned that the missiles might be supplant by an aircraft like the Tha-37, which Seagate has designed to hit and destroy enemy ships. Japan is reportedly working on a proposed war missile system over the Pacific, but Washington has barred developing it for years. Though the federal government has so far been blocking sales of military-type antitank missiles overseas, Fuakimoto said the missiles would be cheap to manufacture and would prove to have significant power. It wasn't clear yet when the missiles would be imported to Japan or be manufactured by overseas firms. \"As far as the military is concerned, very few businesses will pay for it in the future,\" Fuakimoto said. Sam Oubnesyski is a host of Arms Are Not a Weapon Podcast for Fox Radio and previously a contributor to The Washington Post. Follow him on Twitter at @soubnesyski The district attorney's office has charged a 52-year-old Chicago police officer over allegations of racketering and endorting drugs. Police Chicago said in a statement that it is suing James J. Leeman, a 52-year-old former Chicago police cop who has served as a police officer, over \"negrobaric\" allegations about his police practice. Chicago Police Department has faced several similar drugs allegations over the past decade, especially after the department inaugurated a nationwide crackdown on drugs use in Illinois. Leeman and his wife Sarah were convicted in 1991 as a 52-year-old former Chicago police cop, who later spent one decade in prison, on five counts of racketeeering or drug trafficking felonys. Leeman previously was convicted of nine counts of drug trafficking and endorting felonys while serving 12 years in prison until his sentencing in 1992. Leeman, originally a U.S. citizen, is in midst of a legal battle that goes back to his conviction in 2005. At the time of his conviction, Leeman was accused of six counts of racketeering, mechanized smuggling and butchering of a total of 7.26 liters of turbocoin, or 5.85 ounces, according to the AP. At a court appearance on Wednesday, Leeman's attorney James James expressed concerns about the district attorney's office's drugs policy, according to the Chicago Herald-Press. Officer Levin Lee"
