        "text": "WASHINGTON (MarketWatch) - President Barack Obama will impose an additional $12.6 trillion in new tax credits over the next decade to fund the construction of new U.S. solar panels in one of America's top producers of electricity, officials said. The Obama administration would use the remaining $1.59 trillion in a tax levy commonly known as the Bush tax to construct new U.S. solar panels in one of the country's most important producers of electricity.\"An additional $5.0 trillion would mark the beginning of our major cost sharing strategy for small power utilities and lower the U.S.A. gas price for electricity,\" the White House said in a press release.President Barack Obama would impose an additional $12.5 trillion to fund the construction of new U.S. solar panels. Details of where he would use tax credits were not made available public today.The prospect of a new unprecedented tax credit, formally announced in October 2010, has sparked concern among some economists that it could more than doubleshot U.S. electricity prices over the next 10 years. Critics have said it will cause a 5-fold drop in electricity prices and lead to less viable investment in solar infrastructure across the country.“Washington administration officials are particularly pleased with the president’s move to lift the Bush-era tax credit and meet America’s ever-creasing demand for clean energy,” said Christian McCle, president of the National Coalition for Responsible Energy. In a report released Monday, the coalition estimates the move would lower U.S. electricity prices by about 20 percent over the next decade.The new tax credit, announced in October 2010, would boost the costs for power utilities to build new, reimagined U.S. solar panels. But many economists had previously estimated the credit would generate more than $1.2 trillion in new revenue each year. The U.S. House of Representatives passed an amendment to expand the tax credit to replace the Bush tax credit, to go into effect on July 15. The house estimates about 25 percent of that revenue would come from non-Solar-panel sources - about 10 percent of the amount granted the current tax credit. At the same time as the revenue announcement, Obama administration said Monday it had increased the total tax credit for new electricity panel construction to $12.9 trillion, according to Citizensgroup, an advocacy nonprofit. The tax credit would directly generate $1.82 trillion in projected revenue for the 2020 year.“We are delighted to announce the amount granted so far in the 2018 fiscal year,\" Robert Henning Jr., senior vice president for Electricity and Community Development, said in a statement. \"This amount is part of an ongoing effort to develop a clean, robust, affordable energy mix that supports America's fast-moving electricity market share.\"The Obama administration's new tax credit will help power utilities build $3.6 billion plus $1.2 trillion worth of new solar panels by 2020, according to the Electricity Association of America, a $24 billion increase from its current $1.22 trillion tax credit. The new tax credit will work even worse for the solar industry, as power utilities would face a five-fold drop in the cost of solar panels. By 2020, the total cost of new solar panels will climb by about half to $73 billion, which amounts to a $24 billion cost to the electricity industry, according to a report released last week. Expendures for existing solar panels will exceed about $1.1 trillion by 2020. Two-thirds of revenue from existing panels receiving the new tax credit is expected to increase about 20 percent. “Renewable energy presentss an exciting opportunity for businesses and consumers, who are being increasingly forced to switch to fossil fuels and electricity sources at lower gas prices,\" McCle said in an interview. James Stanton, a physics and engineering professor at the University of California, Berkeley, and chairman of the Intergovernmental Partners' Panel on Climate Change, said the new tax credit will lower electricity prices across the country by as much as $1.1 trillion over the next few years. Supply and demand continue to hold massive advantages to the electricity industry, Stanton said. “Boosting the tax credit not only will help our electricity industry thrive and grow competitively, it will also boost supply, which has become one of the most important factors behind the recession,” he said in a statement. Solar Regrowth Plan WASHINGTON - The U.S. Senate is preparing to pass a controversial plan to speed up U.S. solar development. The plan would remove the restriction on the tax credit for building the nation's new panels. Lawmakers would also explore ways to coast up the electricity industry including the tax credit. On Sunday, March 1, 2017, President Donald Trump arrives at the Trump Tower in New York. (Jonathan Johnson/The Washington Post)."
