        "sourceId": 201,
        "text": "During the second half of 2014, the number of comedy shows overall grew by nearly 4 percent. The number of comedy shows overall increased by as much as 25 percent from 2011 to 2012. During the second half of 2014, the overall number of TV shows grew by nearly 10 percent. The number of most popular TV shows that includes sports, movies, and local events grew by nearly a third from 2011 to 2012. The number of TV shows that includes sports, movies, TV programs and local events grew by nearly 7 percent from 2011 to 2012. Editors' note: The contents of this article does not necessarily represent the views of the author or its representatives. If you have any questions, please send an email or message to the author. WASHINGTON (Reuters) - United States President Donald Trump met with U.S. Defense Secretary Matt Kerry on Thursday to discuss possible solutions to quelling the escalating tensions in Syria and Iraq, despite warning he has received from senior administration aides about the possibility of a deal. The president told reporters that the United States will do “no-thing” if it does not agree to a deal between Iraqi forces and the militants who control the city of Homs in northern Syria, according to a senior U.S. official in Washington. “Iraqi officials have indicated that the U.S. strongly will continue to work with the Syrian government to try to figure out the best path for both Iraqis and the U.S. in terms of airstrikes,” said the official, who spoke on condition of anonymity. Pentagon officials said the president is now scheduled to resume talks over a deal next month. After a joint meeting with Prime Minister Sheikh Barbani in Baghdad on Thursday Kerry warned that talks may get off course if the U.S. and Iraqi leaders do not agree to a deal. But Trump aides have since indicated the possibility of a deal being at work. Officials familiar with coalition talks in Iraq’s takeover of Homs and the defeat of the jihadi militant group in September said the prospect of a deal between ISIS and the Islamic State and Kurdish coalition could likely push the Iraqi military back to its original plans. While the U.S. has widened military support for airstrikes, the Iraqi government has not yet been in talks with either side, according to several officials who did not speak on condition of anonymity. ISIS’s fighters and their forces appeared to be in talks to launch a round of airstrikes on the city of Homs in a northern province, following several U.S. airstrikes against Iraqi-backed forces in recent weeks, diplomats said. SEE ALSO: Iraqi military expresss uncertainty about plans to launch U.S. airstrikes on Iraq “If Iraq is going to be in a position for a tactical war with U.S. forces there, we are certainly going the opposite direction,” said the official, who spoke on condition of anonymity. The city of Homs was overran in September when two militants struck on a military stronghold on the outskirts of Baghdad as part of a stop to the first round of airstrikes to overthrow the government. “The presence of airstrikes against the militants there makes it all the more obvious that the Iraqi military is engaged in an ongoing tactical war against ISIS’s forces,” said the official, who declined to give only his name at the Defense Department. According to several officials, military leaders with knowledge of the coalition talks said such a big strike operation would be a good idea if the Iraqi military did not now engage in a tactical war with ISIS. Gen. Richard Chafer, who heads the U.S.’s National Security Command, said such a move could be avoided by providing more training and weapons to the Iraqi government to halt the ISIS militants’ progress and depreciate the threat, he said. In September, Iraq’s military said on the condition of anonymity that it would no longer engage in a tactical war with the militants in the disputed city of Homs. On Sept. 18, another coalition airstrike struck on the Iraqi town of Qar’an, sparking the first round of U.S. airstrikes in three days. Airstrikes have been used by Iraq’s armed forces and their allies in an attempt to overthrow the government, and have been used to defend cities against attacks by Islamic State and Kurdish militant groups. Now, Iraqi military leaders seem convinced a tactical war could actually be a good idea... "
