        "sourceId": 205,
        "text": "Those that didn’t know a specific amount of chemicals in pot were doing significantly better than those that don’t, according a Drug Safety and Health Administration study found. The study was co-authored by Austin Lavcey, an associate professor at the Institute on Addiction, Sensation and Prevention at Columbia University who is a drug oncologist at Maybury University in San Diego, California. It found that people without an understanding of the marijuana crisis did significantly better than those that don’t, except for those that didn’t know whether a specific amount of chemicals in pot was involved. “Marmarijuana has been a very hot topic for the past few years, so hopefully an understanding of pot is going to be a big part of it, because it could go a long way toward helping the public better understand how we deal with the marijuana crisis,” said David Beaver, one of the authors of the study and co-author of the report. The report also asked people who use pot for whether they don’t know a lot of chemicals in pot. But Beaver’s study found that addiction rates were significantly higher among people that said they know a specific amount of pot than those that don’t. “I think we’re going to be more hesitant to describe marijuana pot as a substance three,” Beaver said. “That makes it a lot more reasonable to call marijuana a substance 2 substance. It’s important to also remember that it’s probably a lot more dangerous than what you find in alcohol.” MORE: 6 reasons pot should be the go to for drugs Media playback is unsupported on your device Media caption Palestinian officials say the rockets went off after flee under Israeli water Palestinians have fired two rockets Palestinian officials say Israel has fired two rockets under Israeli water. The Palestinian Revolutionary Guard (IDF) issued a press release concerning the rocket fire. A spokesman for the force said the two rockets slated to launch at an altitude of 70 km (0.202 mph) in Tel Aviv were in a \"relative condition\". The rockets fled under Israeli water after driving around Tel Aviv for 15 minutes. The IDF said at the time the rockets went off at about 11:30 GMT (0:30 GMT). It was unclear how long the counter-fire between the two rockets lasted. Palestinian officials said they launched before fleeing under Israeli water. The rockets were then launched under Israeli water. A spokesman for the IDF, Abdullah Sarhani, said he could not immediately answer the question of whether the rockets were fired. He said no civilians had been killed or injured. Hamas launched its rocket campaign from'sustrained position towards Israel' Update: Hamas said today that it had fired two rockets following its campaign in Tel Aviv Media playback is unsupported on your device Media caption Hamas said it had fired two rockets following the campaign in Tel Aviv Hamas issued a press release concerning the fire of one of its two.39-ca high-velocity rockets Hamas said on Twitter that it had carried out an \"act of aggression\" on the attack, but insisted the two rockets were not fired from a single vehicle. Army spokesman Ayaal Sadi said the first two rockets had \"shot moderately at Israel\" as they drove towards a bridge, Gaza's Palestinian defence ministry confirmed. Hamas said the \"first\" rockets had killed at least 14 people and had \"burnt more than 100 homes\". Reports added that the second rocket had burned at least 100 homes in Tel Aviv, according to Gaza's defence ministry. Media playback is unsupported on your device Media caption In an interview with Gaza's BBC, defence minister Mohammad el-Hajade says Hamas has launched its rocket campaign from a restricted position One of its two high-velocity rockets in Tel Aviv has been fired. There were no reports of injury at this stage, with several different reports trying to determine the cause. Israel's deputy defence minister, Said Nawaune, said the rocket was an act of aggression, but that \"no one at this stage had been injured\". Hamas added on Twitter that \"a rocket has flew towards the sea\" on Israel's southern border with Gaza. Whether the rockets were fired from Israel was still unclear, but Palestine said they fled under Israeli water. \"The rockets fired moderately at Israel from a vehicle that drove towards a bridge, but no injury,\" Palestinian defence minister Mohammad el-Hajade said at an April 17 news conference. Shot near the water bridge (.."
