        "sourceId": 367,
        "text": "NPR, which did not publicly comment on the allegations, on Monday said it will investigate whether President Trump engaged in criminalal acts, including money laundering. \"The Call,\" the latest episode of \"Donald Trump Live,\" will air from 11 a.m.-11 p.m. ET Monday on ABC, CBS and CBS. During the program, NPR will remain engaged in prime-time news coverage on ABC, NBC and other cable news networks. \"Today, we are delighted to announce that our investigation into President Trump's money laundering is ongoing,\" NPR, CEO Greg Tather, said in a statement. \"We strongly reject allegations that President Trump engaged in money laundering.\" \"The Call\" also followed a second news program that NPR began in 2002, called Listen and Make America Great. NPR did not provide a complete explanation about the allegations, but the network released a written statement about its investigations. \"NPR remained committed to upholding the core values of peace, justice, justice and freedom,\" the organization said in a statement. \"NPR always believed that peace and freedom are values, and Mr. Trump has a strong commitment to honor those values.\" The organization, which founded the People for Prosperity of America in 2005, added: \"We continue to believe that peace, justice and freedom are values.\" DOUGO (Reuters) - Turkish Prime Minister Mikhail Arsin arrives in Istanbul Thursday, July 27, 2017, in the custody of Iraqi President Mahmoud Abbas, after an event marking the birth anniversary of Moammar Gadhafi and Bashar (Assad). Turkey's Prime Minister Mikhail Arsin speaks to the media during the opening of the 22nd Annual Russian-Turkish Conference in Staul, Turkey July 27, 2017. REUTERS/Poter Helican/File Photo Please enable JavaScript. Please enable JavaScript to watch this video WASHINGTON (Reuters) - Russian President Vladimir Putin and President Mikhail Arsin are moving forward with allegations of sexual misconduct involving the two highest-level officials in the U.S. government, the first ever to go as far, a Congressional committee said on Wednesday. Complaints against Arsin, Turkey’s top official, and pro-Turkish politicians like Thomas Milly and retired Lt. Gen. Moammar Gadhafi, who was a top commander in the U.S. military during the Cold War, are expected to begin in July. The charges against Arsin are the largest to be filed in public-house history and were brought before a congressional committee set up by Vice President Joe Biden to investigate sexual interactions between the two White House officials. The committee was set to meet later this month for a hearing on the allegations, but it was too early to rule on if any of the complainants knew about Arsin’s false reports. It was not clear whether the complainants were adults. Arsin supporters have lobbied in recent months about the need for Arsin and his accusations of corruption to be brought to justice. “You need to be heard. You don’t need to be heard. You are talking. You don’t need to be heard, but you need to come together and you will be who you are,” Arsin told supporters at a campaign event commemorating the birth and 27th birthday of former Libyan leader Moammar Gadhafi, who was elected president in May this year. “You just need — you can’t be afraid — to live like a man’s life,” Arsin said, adding: “I do not need to be questioned or a heard by the public. I do not.” Supporters of Arsin, who ran for the presidency in 2008 and is not a Republican presidential candidate, said he has raised questions about past corruption and had promoted his accusations on social media. In July, Arsin was on a holiday state trip to Libya, where a man claimed that he had sexually punched a young grandmother at a birthday party. In his interview with reporters, Arsin said he was not sure if over the past few days he knew about any of the alleged incidents. A number of the complainants are likely to testify in coming months, but the committee said that most of them were aware the allegations were false, and several of them were still under investigation, according to the New York Times. Italian craft drinks company Pirodo is trying to expand its range of fresh drinks and fresh drinks in its Colombian stores, trying to create a \"modern\" feeling around its storefront in the northeastern city of San Marino..."
