        "sourceId": 854,
        "text": "If the shooting took place under any circumstances, police believe the freshman could be charged with attempted murder, King said. Police will update their investigation with additional information, King said. Photo: Michael O'Malley Photo: Michael O'Malley Image 1 of / 8 Caption Close Miami freshman charged with attempted murder 1 / 8 Back to Gallery A Miami freshman has been charged with attempted murder after the Miami shooting that fatally wounded an 8-year-old student. The 8-year-old student, who was hit in both ankles with a chemical weapon spray, was identified by police early Saturday. She was taking the classes in a new room at Tulsa Beach Elementary School in the Orlando suburb, according to police reports. Brandon Garcia, a fourth freshman, was listed in life-threatening condition at Tulsa Hospital after the shooting. Garcia was fatally wounded in the 6300 block of North Tower about 12:30 a.m. Saturday, Sgt. Tim King said. Garcia was going to be studying at Lee High School, according to police reports. \"She's going to hit you hard because you all know something is going to blow you away,\" King said. Investigators are still searching for the student's whereabout at the school. Police said the shooting is believed to have occurred in Orlando. An 8-year-old was taken to the hospital, police said. A freshman has been detained at Franklin County jail on a charge of attempted murder, Miami police said. She's been charged with first-degree attempted sexual assault on a minor, and second-degree attempted homicide of a minor. - \"She's going to hurt some of us. She's been with her family and she was going to do her best,\" Miami Police Chief John Blazer said. Police said the girl was trying to walk onto the house's hallway and was lying down on the floor.Immediately after, the girl was hit in her bicep, wounding her back, police said. The 8-year-old student suffered minor fractures and was taken to her hospital, Blazer said. No date of arrests was immediately released. Miami Board of Education president David Nail was out of the school the next morning when he said he saw a picture of the girl from Nail's class. The president said students were told she was panicked as she was going to high school. \"I've been with her all my life,\" Lee said. The girl's mother, Lee, immediately reached out to their freshman daughter, Brandon Garcia, for help. \"She's going to dance. She's going to dance,\" Lee said. \"She's been with the family and she's going to do her best.\" Miami Police Department spokeswoman Susan Pollard described the arrests as \"overwhelming,\" adding that the student was going to the classes. \"She was feeling good. She was going to dance two or three times in a row at that point,\" Pollard said. Three people were arrested on suspicion of assault. Kristilla White, a 17-year-old freshman at the University of Miami, is charged with self-inflicting a minor and a fourth-degree attempted murder charge. \"She was currently in custody,\" Nail said. Three people were arrested on suspicion of assault. Kristilla White, a 17-year-old freshman at the University of Miami, is charged with self-inflicting on a minor and a fourth-degree attempted murder charge. \"She was currently in custody,\" Nail said. White was admitted to the Washington Medical Center at about 7:15 a.m. Saturday, according to police reports. Detectives and a crime specialist were on the scene to determine what led to the shooting, Nail said. Officers identified the ninth-grader as in her mid-40s. No name was given to date, police said. The girl went to her mother's house in the 1300 block of North Tower Drive, Nail said. She was on her way to a supply store in the U.S., on Fifth Street, when a loader came blown open. The girl's father, Phil, said he did not know where the girl was or who the mother was. He did not know much about the other children, who were identified Saturday morning. The girl's father said she has been his family's daughter for more than 20 years. \"This is the most important story all children in Miami can tell about her,\" his father said Saturday. The goal of the Miami shooting is to prevent a teenager from hitting another child with a chemical weapon, Nail said. \"This is not a story about protecting the child as much as defending the child,\" Nail said."
