        "text": "\"The U.S. Olympic Athletic Committee on Wednesday night notified that it was working with International Olympic Committee and the NCAA to ensure a more competitive environment,\" Olympic Athletic Committee Chair spokeswoman Carol Cofb said. \"The Olympic Athletic Committee will continue to work with the International Olympic Committee and coordinator with U.S. senators to set new rules and goals.\" U.S. Olympic Athletic Committee chair Robert Benitte expressed his disappointment at Trump's loss, saying it will be a \"night of greatness.\" He issued a statement on the organization's website. He also shared his disappointment at the president's loss. \"I'm deeply dismayed that President Donald Trump has lost,\" said Benitte. \"We look forward to working together to ensure a safe and competitive environment, as well as improve the lives of our children. I do look forward to Congress to take a step forward, especially by acting on the Supreme Court.\" \"I am grateful for President Trump's victory,\" he said. \"For decades, we have worked together to fight for the interests of the athletes in a way that was never possible.\" The Olympic Athletic Committee has recognized and welcomed Trump's victory. College athletics, which founded after 9/11, has been a key partner in the fight to provide a more inclusive environment for its young players and coaches, as well as for faculty and children. \"President Trump has had a great working relationship with college athletics,\" David Seegel, executive director of the organization, said in a news release. \"He'll continue to work with Congress to set new goals and goals, making college athletics a better place for our children.\" Trump's actions as president have drawn widespread criticism from college athletics and even transgender students, with some citing the academic mandate for transgender students and the repeal of a federal law that banned women from college athletics. Phoenix residents including Rove and Alice, who will both become NCAA champions, speak at a rally at Todd J. Downey Middle School in Washington, D.C., on May 16, 2015. In Anaheim, Arizona, Phoenix was once the bridge to America, between a city of peace and freedom, and a city of openness and inclusion. Throughout the 70’s and 80’s, Phoenix was the home of the many metro cities and universities that shaped the image of America. “To see LGBT people in this country, and really making an impact on the world, that city is so special for me.” Kaine Rove, 50, of Anaheim, Arizona, the co-founder of Communities for Downtown Change, an LGBT advocacy group that works actively in several cities. “When you move a little city, it’s really hard to find people. That’s why I feel like the cities of Phoenix and Washington are a very special place. To see people like these who are part of LGBT community in this country, and are really making an impact on the world, that city is so very special for me.” Kaine Rove, 50, of Anaheim, Arizona, the co-founder of Communities for Downtown Change, an LGBT advocacy group that works actively in several American cities. Kaine Rove speaks at a rally in Washington, D.C., on May 16, 2015. In Anaheim, Arizona Alice is married and is living a life of her own, which she’s never even done before. When she was in college, she was battling to legalize marriage equality for gays in Arizona. After the Supreme Court challenged her 2010 case, Alice left Arizona to continue her legal career in the state, after many attempts. After many attempts and many years in solitary, Rove resigned from her high school. A few years later, she moved to Washington, D.C., to continue wrestling. “For me, everybody is just like, ‘Oh my god, I’m back,'” she said. “There are a lot of people out there who don’t really understand it, and don’t really understand it.” In many ways, the ramifyings of the Phoenix revolution explain why Rove needs to change the way she views life. Rove was born in Phoenix, and her family resides in Washington. She and her twins moved to Washington State in 2003. “I came down here and I just really like Phoenix,” she said. “So that’s where I spent the rest of my life.” Ultimately, she continued her career in wrestling. She spent each year at George Washington, D.C., University, where she holds an NCAA title in wrestling. Rove says she’s very grateful for her achievement. “I’ve been fighting the biggest championships in the U.S., and I am so grateful for it,” she said. * * * *"
