        "id": "s-ee74b131322b461c38410960",
        "sourceId": 948,
        "text": "A separate study by Oregon State University’s Lily Reid, published last May, found that short term psychotherapy treatments are effective in people living with many chronic conditions including food shortages and hypertension. “When you look at the published studies, you see that short term psychotherapy treatments are effective in a population that does not use drugs,” Reid said. Facebook's sole social network, as well as Twitter and Instagram, is acquiring its popular media search engine. The social networking giant is ramping up user traffic using its popular online search engine. Politico recently reported that the daily page likes of Facebook's popular media search engine reached 18.9 million in July, compared to the 17.3 million number for the same quarter a year earlier. That's a 20 percent jump over its July 2015 peak of 18.8 million, with the site's daily likes jump to 2.3 million. In the third quarter, the Facebook search engine showed a 4 percent increase from the same quarter last year when the site's daily likes reached 2.3 million. The social network says it has acquired a feature that lets users type links when they visit Facebook pages. This allows users to ask for photos, videos and other related content from other sites. \"As Facebook becomes more of a user base, we are very excited about,\" Yoshi Koejiya, Facebook's chief product officer, said in a statement. \"This is not the first time the Facebook search engine has asks users for photos, videos and other content they can use from other sites.\" Facebook also asked to remove photos and other related content from its online search engine. Twitter declined to comment on the request. Twitter spokesman Ken Dayton said in a statement that the company hasn't personally done anything like this in the past. Facebook does have a version of its picture removal software called Picture Remover that allows users to simply remove pictures from its online search engine. Instagram, the business equivalent of Twitter, has also been busy using the online search engine to make screenshots. Facebook also asks users for Instagram's profile to show them engaged in a Gmail conversation. The social networking space is a constantly changing industry, and Facebook's acquisition is believed to be making the space even more accessible to more users. Users can pay through the online search engine by making a cash transfer. It's the equivalent of a glass of ice cream. Here is a report from CNN: Please enable Javascript to watch this video KEYVILLE, Wis. — A semi-autopic rifle broke into the home of a woman Thursday night and left the victim in critical condition, police said. The gunfire happened at about 2:30 a.m. in the 7600 block of North Verizon Avenue on SW 87 University Highway, according to Wisconsin State Police Lt. Gary Reese. The woman was riding on her bike when she heard a woman dabbing at her door. The man ran away, Reese said. Police eventually took a man to the fourth floor of the house, where he shot her in the head, Reese said. The woman was riding on her bike when the man shot at her. The man with a semi-autopic rifle fired at her at the end of the fourth floor, Reese said. No one in the house is in critical condition at present. The gunfire took place on the 2nd and 3th floors of the house on SW 87 University Highway, Reese said. Police say it is recommended to use capped windows that will protect against fire between the third and fourth floors, do not use outdoor windows, and do not use shining windows on outdoor decks. Anyone with information is encouraged to contact police at 412-43-5921 or call 415 CF 730 using the hashtag #BcRockeyler. The Wolverine Bulls opened its first retail door Thursday night, marking the end of a five-year, $1.6 million renovatement of the former homes at Areo’s Restaurant and Fairfield Meat in central Oklahoma City. Following the opening ceremony, customers will have access to three options for lunch, free drinks, and beer. \"Tonight it was both entertaining and fun, and it was quite an inspiring experience,\" said Robert Huthen, vice president of the Wolverine Bulls co-owners. \"They played a critical role in the restaurant's ability to provide fast and affordable food for needy residents.\" Huthen joined co-owners of Bulls Tom Williams, who owns Lewler Restaurant; Gavin Chen, co-owner of American Meat; and Rick Phillip, co-owner of Darney’s Restaurant......"
