        "sourceId": 1007,
        "text": "If the measure prevails, as Abbas argues, it would be debunked by the Texas legislature this session, which would ban states from holding student gatherings or events at their college or universities, or setting up campus centers. Texas has already declined to ban the establishment of campus centers.SAN FRANCISCO, CA -- A request to issue an arrest to two Santa Ana sisters accused of violating state federal civil rights law has been denied by the state of California in a county court. The warrant in the case of Gabriele Barre, 18, is the subject of a legal battle between federal police and the FBI and other civil rights organizations. The lawsuits accusing the state of violating federal law to protect mental health after victims' alleged abuse are due to be filed in the California Supreme Court at the end of April. \"These facts are relevant to the warrant issued by the U.S. government and illustrates that this case takes seriously,\" said Ann Chapman, president of the American Civil Liberties Union of California. No date is set for when the warrant will be filed in federal court. California Attorney General Mitch McConnell said the warrant extends the deadline for the girls' mental health assessment to the first year of adulthood. \"We respect individual rights and civil liberties, but we need to ensure that the mental health of these two girls remains high priority as the precinct of civil law,\" he said. Dozens of civil rights groups condemned the warrant, claiming it was part of an unconstitutional abuse of individual rights. \"This is illegal and inconsistent with existing law,\" said Matthew Lopez, a civil rights attorney for the North American Council on Santa Ana. Police had no comment on the request for the warrant. The warrant was part of a long-running battle for evidence in the Supreme Court. The lawsuit was filed against Dwayne Thieves, 18, the sister of the Barre victims. Thieves is accused of sexually abusive the sisters at a home in Santa Ana. The case drew the national applause from civil society groups, who filed a separate civil rights lawsuit against the girls, accusing federal prosecutors of leaking information about the extent of the crime. The civil rights groups denied that the girls engaged in the abuse. \"These groups believe that house arrests are a direct abuse of civil liberty and constitutional rights to protect free speech and freedom of assembly. This is the first time that such house arrests have been ever carried out by the federal government,\" the group spokesman Dan Clark said in a statement. Santa Ana police on Monday praised the federal government's leadership in issuing public the warrant. \"The federal government has seen firsthand that making house arrests is an illegal or unjust practice,\" Santa Ana Police Chief Larry Wilson said in an e-mailed statement Monday. \"This is not the first time that the federal government has made house arrests in Santa Ana - similar incidents have not been reported this year in California.\" David Johnson, the state's attorney's office, said police had not considered making house arrests but that he and some of the other civil rights groups met with police over the summer to discuss options of making house arrests. Mr Johnson said he believed the right to make an arrest would be dismissed as his case moves forward to federal court. A state civil rights warrant was issued for the arrest of four and nine accused of carrying a \"red flag\" in violation of state civil rights law. A federal judge has notruled on whether those charges will be dismissed. That warrant was not issued by Santa Ana police. WASHINGTON (Reuters) - U.S. GDP increased 2 percent from 1990 in June but will need to hold up 1 percent to keep up with a jump in year-on-year growth in the U.S. economy, economists said on Monday. Economist at Barclays International said a jump in year-on-year growth was necessary for a stable U.S. economy. In June, year-on-year growth increased 1 percent to 85.4 percent, faster than the 73.3 percent growth was expected. An 8.4 percent increase would mean the national economy could pull back from a recession and could recover from sluggish. \"We are not living up to that pace,\" Mike Harris, chief economist at Barclays International, told Reuters. \"It's not in our best interests to say it's enough to stable the economy,\" he added. Jonathan Williams, chief economist at Standard & Poors, said Census data showed the U.S. economy has not grown steadily since 1990, though it is currently keeping up that pace despite severe output shortages. \"We do not currently have the numbers to stable the economy,\" he said...."
