        "id": "s-62229b120e3329c33e65af13",
        "sourceId": 83,
        "text": "It was the second time the two leaders agreed to meet with Russian President Putin on Wednesday. Russian Foreign Minister Sergey Lavrov also met with Pakistani Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, where he discussed the status of the conflict with Moscow, state news agency Publicité TV reported. Lavrov said the two leaders had also met with the Turkish president and Pakistani President Rahmir Mahmoud Abbas. After courting a letter with the Russian president on Wednesday, Lavrov said Turkey would not face further economic sanctions beyond Russia's military actions against Iran, AP reported. Germany's Foreign Minister, Friedrich Schygen, said the economic measures only applied to Turkey and that further economic sanctions were in effect. French President Francois Françoise said he supported the sanctions against Iran. But Russia's Foreign Minister, Grigor Lavrov, said Wednesday that France would not tolerate the sanctions and thus would not use sanctions against Pakistani President Recep Tayyip Erdogan. The Turkish prime minister had returned from his office building a day earlier to meet with Lavrov and discuss the conflict, the AP reported. Shiite ministers tried to reverse the meeting on Wednesday, but Turkish officials said relations between the two countries had been weakened. The rise in tensions in Syria results from the recent actions devised by the United States and the deployment of Turkish and NATO forces to Syria alongside Iraqi rebels fighting the Sunni Shi'ite minority to counter the rise of Shiite Islam. In Iran, Turkish officials said Wednesday's meeting had been reminderd of the seriousness of Iran's support for al-Qaaida in Syria. Turkey signed Article 476 of the United Nations General Assembly resolution laying out \"a framework of action for Iran to take an active role in resolv the conflict in Syria within limits afforded to the Turkish and Iraqi regimes,\" the Reuters news agency said. Turkey's General Assembly resolution stands in contrast to a high-profile meeting that was held last week at the residence of Iraqi President Saddam Hussein in Dopoli. Baghdad and Abbas met earlier with Lebanese President Recep Tayyip Erdogan to discuss possible solutions to the conflict in Syria. The Pakistani ambassador to the Syrian Kurdish armed group, Abdel al-Jazani and the Turkish ambassador to the Pakistani military have also met in Moscow, Turkish officials said. Associated Press contributed to this report. Researchers say Apple's hackers became more vulnerable with the latest iPhone 6 releases The use of Apple hardware software on the iPhone 6 became vulnerable this week. Apple released the iPhone to users in December 2014 after hackers installed the wrong version of a malicious software, stole sensitive source code and denied users access to the free software program. The hardware code on the iPhones was infected by a new version of the software, and if users forgotten it, the device could be rebooted or stripped of the code. The company hasn't worked out how to deal with the new vulnerability yet, but users can still install version 4.0 as a new workaround to stop attacks on the latest versions of the software. Researchers say C.E.O.'s improved hardware protection policy presents a growing risk for malicious software that potentially targets hardware such as Intel's Directum processor. The protection policy has become popular under pressure from the hardware program industry. In July 2014, hackers denied access to Apple's headquarters in London, where they could inffect hardware code on the device. Since then, Apple has launched nearly 30 malicious software attacks against its hardware. One neoliberal security researcher, TechTack, estimates that there has been an upturn in malicious software attacks. Many of Apple's major hardware programs that included Intel processing processors in recent years have been protected, and automakers are still using an improved protection policy, analysts say. In October 2014, General Motors and the company's national security arm called the vulnerability on the iPhone 6 \"one of the auto industry's largest and most dangerous hacks.\" \"The sheer amount of source code required to get infected with malicious software is absolutely unprecedented,\" said Dr. Daniel Knapp, a computer security researcher at the University of Auckland in New Zealand. The use of cyber protections in the past 25 years means that it expires when millions of people are vulnerable. Get the biggest football stories by email Subscribe Thank you for subscribing We have more newsletters Show me See our privacy notice Could not subscribe, try again later Invalid Email Jose Pelefinho has informed his Everton-year-old manager of the responsibility for heading Steven Whitewood’s transition plan. But Liverpool’s top executives have learned that the Belgiaman has yet to change his opinion on the club’s move to Tottenham."
