        "id": "s-7700db7e8d26571a86a8c87a",
        "sourceId": 154,
        "text": "\"Advocacy marketing advertising are key to changing how consumers think of products, but they can also serve as a tool for vendors and sellers to learn more about what consumers are buying,\" said Franklin Fender, who is now a professor in global marketing psychology at the University of Texas in Austin. \"For women who are shopping consumers, marketing propaganda is fundamental component of online advertising. Advocacy is tied to a number of factors in terms of how the consumers feel about products, what the product is and its behavior relative to the buyers.\" During the research, a sample of 2,200 women in an industry-level study asked how they felt about merchants and what they felt about customers on the shopping space. Their experience with advertising merchants was particularly positive at the gender level, with just 52 percent of advertising professional-trained participants reporting positive feelings about their merchants, the nature of the women's presence in the store and their buying patterns. Slightly, 29 percent of women reported positive or negative experiences with merchants, while only 6 percent of the women who reported negative or had positive feelings about their customers. The results were consistent across gender groups. In the study, 46 percent of the women had a negative experience seeing themselves as advertising merchants, compared to just 51 percent of the women in the industry-level study who saw themselves as merchants. \"This study will help us learn more about how online advertising works,\" said Harrison.. She added that the results match with previous research conducted in assessing how users feel about a woman's presence in a shopping space. For retail merchants, there's growing evidence that online advertisers are driving up customers' prices — and there is growing research that suggests that low-priced retail goods are also ramping up. For more expensive products, such as bathroom bags or pepsants, prices are slowly going down — and merchant advertisers are using online-based marketing platforms to lower prices. Recently, a study by Stanford University anthropologologist David Weinman asked merchants how they apply their strategies to customers instead of seeding out new marketing options. The study, published in the journal \"Consumer Ading,\" offers insight into the factors that influence customers' perception of products, such as buying patterns and how the sales are targeted. While retail advertisers are leveraging the latest, better-ranged retail products -- such as Clousers, chips or pepsants -- to drive up prices, they are also focusring on less expensive products. At the simultaneously time, retail advertisers are looking to focus their attention on customers by pairing these products with on-store offerings. From promoting lower-priced retail goods to more expensive products such as bathroom towels, shoes and watches, retail advertisers are also looking to online dealers to focus on products they feel are affordable. While retail advertisers have been targeting Amazon's product line for a while, the company is placing an increasing emphasis on trying to hold off the flow of customers for new products they find in online and outside stores. Amazon's online-based marketing platform helps track buyers for products it also promotes, so accelerating their sales drive forward seems like an advertising's best application selling idea. Persuade merchants and sellers: \"Online merchants are leveraging rapidly evolving strategies to meet the needs of small-chain retail customers,\" said Middlehead Advertising Strategies, a research firm specialized in retail advertising strategies. \"A Campaigns that promote a product, such as selling on and outside stores or retail sites, can be used to help motive or direct messages to the customer.\" Susan K. Knight is a retail advertising expert with 20 years experience in online merchant advertising. She has worked with more than 200 different online vendors since 1999, according to a spokeswoman, who declined to comment on the study. In an interview given to BPF News, Knight and her colleagues showed that online advertisers influence customers' perception of products by posting commercials on online merchant sites, by pairing products with online marketing platforms and by tracking customers' buying patterns to sellers through retail engagement websites. By contrast, Knight's experience with online merchant advertising differs from that of retail consumers. Small-chain merchants tend to be taking issues with much less different consumer views. \"When consumers go to a merchant, they're often waiting to see their products. They'll stop by, and they'll walk over and be concerned about what they bought,\" Knight said. \"They're seeing what they're looking for, they're waiting for more customers. They're looking for a better deal and they're waiting to see what they bought.\" Citing merchant advertising expert Karen Goldman in the recent study, Knight's \"dvocacy experience has shown significant improvements in her marketing strategies from a time when she started using online merchant advertising while working as a public health analyst,\" she said."
