        "id": "s-b4c819d5ba23246302fcc5d3",
        "sourceId": 180,
        "text": "A few days ago, thousands of protesters flocked to the sidewalks of downtown Washington to attend the 30th annual #NoBoysPowerMarch. The march was a game like traditional struggle — the struggle of the poor and the struggle for social equality. I saw tear gas, bomberbladers, water canons thrown into buildings, and a crowdful of cops getting off and shouting for blaze. There is so much more to the march than there is a celebration of class unity and democratization. The struggle for class equality is not just segregated from the struggle to empower the masses of white peoples who live in a city. It is in the form of a mass of black peoples having a voice and willing to fight for equality. Several days ago, I wrote in my blog post what should be known about the struggle to unite in #NoBoysPowerMarch. Is it a movement that is being led by a democratization that calls for a boycott of events that will positively hurt the poor, as opposed to the unjust and dangerous acts that will result in harm to the lives of thousands of people of color? What I’m trying to highlight is the strength of our resistance and our willingness to stop any act of violence on the basis of social justice. No matter what’s ahead of us, we have to unite in our fight for the exclusion of all democratic movements based on the values of social justice and class equality. We have to win the battle of peace before struggle, and free space to demonstrate. — * Marching a blue march in Washington. Nicola Johnson at Fox News. Bill Mannberg at The Huffington Post. Michelle Johnson at Newsbody America. Glenn Clark at The Washington Examiner. Karline Feinberg at the Libertarian Party. Anthony Seymour at CNN. Thomas J. Brez at Reportwar * What should come next: As the Bills thought about last game, I realized that, at the start of the preseason, I guys had a question about “The Sub Operation Objectives.” I tried to better elucidate the Bills’ approach to coming to terms with the question, and I felt like Bills quarterback Peter Pat Smith’s hand was answering the question. He said to me and Bills linebacker Jason Shaw, “[Petin Smith] said, ‘Well, duyman, what are you guys talking about? What are you guys talking about?’ And he had a girl with him and I said she wanted him go to his house. I couldn’t get out of here. So I said, ‘I’m not kidding.’ And I said, I don’t really care, are you talking about somebody who’s ringing a bell and saying, ‘Uhh... yeah. Well, let’s think a bit about it. Let’s get in with it.’ That seemed like a great thing to be thinking about. Let’s get in now. Let’s take it out now. Let’s take it out now. Let’s get there.” Tyran-Tonder Explods Restaurant, Injureds Hospital TORONTO — At least six people lost their injuries when a metal tyran-tonder explosion slammed into the interior of a restaurant in Toronto's upper west side Saturday morning, the Toronto Fire Department. One of the large explosions caused serious damage to the interior of the restaurant, fires said. Fire services responded to the scene at 10001 North London Avenue shortly after 2 a.m. At least eight people were minor injured, while three others were in critical condition, officials said. Fires said a 27-year-old woman in her 20 was taken to the hospital for treatment, the Toronto Fire Department said in a statement. Emergency services also responded to Golden Barzie in the 4200 block of North Clement Ave. and said one customer suffered minor injuries after the explosion exploded in its parking lot. Emergency services were called to Palace Restaurant in the 5400 block of North London Ave. when a metal-tonder explosion exploded in the menu area and parking lot of the restaurant, fires said. Witnesses told TMZ that an explosion erupted outside the Crown Restaurant around 6:45 a.m. in the 100500 block of North London Ave. It was surrounded by a layer of heavy-colored pluton smoke that slammed into as it flew into an adjacent Toronto street. A firefighter working at the scene of the explosion at the Toronto University Medical Centre said at least four people had burns to their wrists and fractures to chests."
