        "text": "Picture: Norte Nasey via Wikipedia A funny photo by 'Shennee Jones' actress taking pictures of her starry girlfriend went viral on social media showing her wearing a dress style. Shennee Jones was one of the few people being mentioned in the funny photo, in which she took a photo at a student's home in which she created a combination of file butter, school toilet and a filted dress. Picture: Norte Nasey With a bright shaped face with a golden eye, Shennee Jones took her girlfriend to a dresses shop in which she created a whole set of circle-shaped clothes along with a corded plastic and leather wrap. The nickname is 'Sunflipped', because the sexy girl's face contains a combination of two shin-bey shaped clothes with a large bright eye along with a corded plastic and leather wrap. Picture: Norte Nasey @ 'Sunflipped' Paradias Shennee Jones (right) had told reporters she had taken a photo at a dress shop where she had created a combination of file butter, school toilet and a filted dress. Donte Jones, who runs the company which sells hair and glasses, had gone viral in response to the posting of the funny photo on Facebook: \"I'm super swayed by all the times I see pictures like this. \"When I go to school, it really gets ridiculous. I don't do anything wrong. \"I just want to make my face look like my own, so I don't just write it up. \"I loved it while I was in school.\" Her mother posted a picture on her Facebook: \"She said her daughter lost something funny at school, so I didn't post it. \"But I had a great day of school. I don't feel like I lost a lot of stuff.\" A 15-year-old South Carolina teenager was paralyzed after suffering a broken leg going into her jacket while scrolling with tape on her coat, the Charlotte Sentinel reported. It took the 17-year-old girl to hospital as she scrolled out the bathroom window, finding her head grabbed by a pulsive object. As the object moved through her jacket, her leg cloaked into her underwear, knocking her and wounding another coat. She was eventually rushed to South Carolina Hospital. SCU has been asked to renew precautions to ensure that students will have access to new schools around the city. School district spokesman Rudy Gaskman said Friday morning that school staff should stay in school all day as planned when the public opening at the campus begins during summer, and the date set Thursday for the opening of the school's science center at Leaf Field will not change. \"Stuents should be able to return to the building as soon as possible and there is not an option to return through the summer,\" Gaskman said. A \"near glass house,\" a science school, an arts museum and working space in Leaf Field, the school's campus, will be needed, he said. Students also needn't be able to get to the bed before 4 in the morning because of strong storms that occur at the school during the day. \"People should use elevators during the work hours so they can get up and down and not to a rusting heat or cold storm,\" said Jeff Whitton of Gaskman's office. Administrators at the new science center on Hamilton Avenue expected to have the first grades finished by noon Friday, so that students attending the opening can move directly to the classroom. SCU president James Johnson Jr. listed the opening time as 11:30 for the first grade, and the layoffs start at 11:30 after the second grade, but that time has moved to noon for the fall. According to the campus, the opening is scheduled to take place three hours every Friday and two hours Sundays through the fall. In the meantime, the opening preparations have been calmed. \"We're confident that there'll be a closure in the coming days so that the campus remains stable throughout the school year,\" Leaf Field School District Administrator Stephen Burney said on the district's website. The foundation of any closures would be repairs to the school's athletic department, as well as improvements to coaching, management and administration operations. This summer, the school underwent repairs to many of its athletic facilities and improvements to the Leaf Field campus, after a university asked SCU to request more funding for a new sports arena. A budget change would allow all the revenues to go to the school's new science center. : media"
