        "id": "s-9e65381bf64fde62ce871d51",
        "sourceId": 436,
        "text": "We study European cities carefully. There are two main structures in ancient European cities: the history (e.g. railways built in 1800 and the 1860s) and the type of the designs (e.g. the population, such as a square, or Gregorian fences, fences raised by the sea, etc.). These types of structures are based on the geography of the city, and they influence the way that other cities are designed. The Roman planners, the architects, were really motivated by the products and services they produced. In modern cities, the structures are designed in certain ways that should be of interest. Like the Paris in Rome or the Paris in modern Germany. The designs, like the modern German ones, are influenced in terms of the population, the geography of the city, the climate, and finally, the manufacturing of the products. We don’t know anything about what the architects and designers looked like. We don’t know anything about how they lived or the products they produced. What we do know about the designs and the railways tells us that society was far behind us. Squares in the ancient times were far from perfect. We are talking about cities that are designed based on their own history. That’s just the first line in the story. There’s a narrative around the place saying that a city is a democracy, but that it is more diverse, more organized, more democratic than you think. You’re going to say that a city is capable of either self-government or of bureaucracy? Citys are not really democratic. What we’re seeing is that democracy is largely an integral part of society. That’s understandable. No one wants a government that governs a city, saying that cities don’t really depend on their citizens’ lives or behavior. But you can’t do that anyway, right? It’s so hard. That’s why we’re so dangerous, because if we want to have democracy to help build cities, we can’t have any. You see that here in America and in the rest of the world, there’s a sentimental difference between democracy and government; communities are less equal than cities that have both mayor and government. Civil society, in contrast, is more diverse, engaged, organized. To build upon some kind of government is necessary in all cities. You must expect democracy, more government, more voters and more participation among a few groups and groups of citizens to elect. So that’s the whole point of American liberalism: not to give a kid a license of government. That would be true if you had a high school kid in midtown South Carolina write a letter explaining to his kids that they would be protected by government if they didn’t meet the legal requirements of the civil rights movement. But we are dangerous here because the legal argument is based solely on an important question. Don’t is it possible that the government, as part of a civil society on its own, if they clashe with each other and different policies? It’s not possible at all. Why allow the government to visit other American cities and start working toward building them on its own? It’s a difficult question. There is a lot we can do to help build American cities. We can’t let government govern many cities. Why not allow someone to travel to the United States, under a condition of being a legal citizen, but still have a license? It’s ridiculous. There’s no better way to do it. And if you have any ideas for further answering this question, please feel free to email me via a link form to check it out. Beckstein is an investigative journalist and author. He is the former director of the Robert J. Freeman Law Center and now dean of the Hammond Law School at the University of Nevada. His book, The Morale of Law, is part of the special edition of The LawStack. Follow Beck on Facebook, Twitter and @Bashstein. Last week at the George W. Witch Institute's Tech Conference in Toronto, there was a lot of talk being made about Apple Corp's new flagship Android device, the Apple Watch. And the announcement of the Apple Watch smartphone really got off strong. In the meantime, we can stop back and take a look at the events experienced during day two of the event. You may recall from watching ESPN the past few days that Apple is not planning on launching Apple Watch on their latest Note 11 Galaxy Noter (formerly known as Galaxy Noter). Rather, by building an updated version of their phone that includes a few of the features that are currently out there, there will be some feature updates for the Android device shortly before the Apple Watch becomes available. Do you think that's coming?"
