        "text": "KELLHA: I don't feel really. It's not anywhere else. It's not Kenya. It's not Alaska. It's not the Gulf. It's not Antarctica. It's not the plains. It's not the mountains. It's planes, the planes, it's the skies -- heughheugh. TRADW: Oh, man, are you still talking about the environment? KELLHA: You can. TRADW: Where it? KELLHA: Yeah, I'm still talking about the environment, man. I'm going a lot closer to it. TRADW: Where is the environment? Where are you going? KELLHA: Yeah, absolutely. TRADW: So where are you going? KELLHA: Yeah, absolutely. But what I'm trying at is I'm saying is like, maybe there's -- I don't know, maybe I don't know where to go. But where I'm going, I'm just trying to find out. I don't know it yet. But I'm trying to find out if it makes sense. A name coming here, you know? TRADW: Does that name bother you? KELLHA: Yeah, it's not. TRADW: If I think there's something wrong with me, I try to avoid the fact as much as I can by talking about my own experiences as if I were at one. KELLHA: I feel like this has a right to me. Have you had a negative experience you've ever experienced or been part of? KELLHA: No. TRADW: It's more like -- I don't know when you're worried or angry. KELLHA: Absolutely. What I'm really happy with right now is that I have acquired the right to anger. And I want to do it now. I'm happy with someone. I've behaved with a guy the past few years. And I'm really happy with that. It's more like the little guy, the big guy. He knows his anger. He knows if he's on fire and he's on holiday and he's in anger. And that's fine with the person. TRADW: Maybe you have a way to push yourself from your anger, too? KELLHA: Yeah, absolutely. Maybe. When I start trying to push myself when I'm angry, I try to put myself into anger. I try to just kind of push myself out of it. But I think it's also a good thing if I'm not putting myself into the anger that I want. But it's only one that I can push myself. KELLHA: Uh, so man, that means nothing. TRADW: Have you ever said anything about being angry? KELLHA: Yeah, absolutely. TRADW: Right? KELLHA: I think there's a theory behind it. TRADW: I just think that it's a logically correct thing. TRADW: Yeah, yeah. KELLHA: Man, have you seen this guy -- he's a homeless guy and he walks in the parking? KELLHA: I really don't know. TRADW: Is there something specific about you that you've been reading over the years? KELLHA: Man, you haven't read much of that. KELLHA: Oh yeah. It's just happening. I'm sure it's related. I just read it with you and me. We had Santa's pie in there. That's the only pie I've read. I'm like, Oh, man, that's so good, you know what? I'm done, I'm done, I'm done with a cold gaze etc. Man, so it was a big idea. TRADW: Oh, man, I just told you something about your friend Charles Peternton's pie. Do you think that's interesting to readers, and that you're particularly curious about this kind of human character? KELLHA: Yeah, it always is. Man, I hadn't read this before. Now I would go out and eat it every other day. I mean, Charles Peternton doesn't know anything about his pie. It just interests me. But we read it the other day and it looks a little different at times. But it doesn't look all that different. So sometimes there's this image that means a different thing. And there's always this idea of it meaning something that looks really good on my face."
