        "sourceId": 160,
        "text": "The bill is deeply feared among some environmentalists, according to Doug Riverberg, the research director for the Foundation for Civil Liberties and Environmental Justice. It would serve as a rallying cry against states seeking to repeal the pot ban, he said. Some states could consider repealing the ban as early as the end of this fiscal year, he said. Such states face more restricted marijuana laws, Riverberg said. As many as four legislatures in New York and Lane County, Fla., are expected to consider legislation removing the restriction during each session. That means that state lawmakers are motivated, Riverberg said. Sen. Bernie Sanders, R-La Mari, told FOX News earlier that he had favored bringing the bill to the Senate. And state Sen. Rob Ford Jr., R-Sahvia, has said he would support bringing the bill to the Senate. Since Wednesday, Ford and the Smokal Law Council have expressed support for the measure, highlighting the need to make sure that the Senate doesn't intimidate state lawmakers into acting. \"We're trying to persuade state lawmakers into considering the issue,\" Ford said, remarking \"we have no assurance that someone's ever going to testify against that law.\" He also said he had a goal of getting the bill his way through the Senate, noting that state lawmakers would be more likely to raise questions about reversing it than he does. 'No much incentive' But state Sen. Robert T. Nyles Jr., R-La Mari, said he hasn't supported repealing the restriction, but recently voiced his support forbringing it to the Senate. Nyles said that the problem with the bill would be such that it would not be included in a hearing about medical marijuana, which legislators would have to prove their approval for, other than smoking marijuana. And it would not be submitted to a closed Senate committee. \"It doesn't give legislators any incentive to intervene on this issue,\" he said. A young Pakistani woman carrying a bullish-colored anti-Hezbollah machine gun is seen by Pakistani media during demonstration in the streets near al Qaeda headquarters on April 20, 2017, in the Afghan province of Istanbul. (Ed Zaaid Ahmed/Reuters) More than 24,000 Americans have lost their weapons since they were extradite to Afghanistan as part of the Islamist-led government's disarmament campaign, according to Western officials. KAR BAAN, Afghanistan (AFP) - Pakistani forces will extort hundreds of kilograms of machine guns as well as bundles of ammunition from Afghanistan's independent government in June and allow it to bomb two rebels, Western officials said. The extradition will bring about 200 official men from an wing of al Qaeda-affiliated in Pakistan that is working to dismantle the self-proclaimed government. Officials of Pakistan Foreign Minister Abu Bakr al-Mukhtibu told a foreign news agency, for instance, he doesn't want the weapons to be turned over to police. He is expected to meet with U.S. officials on Friday to discuss options on how to conduct the government's disarmament, a spokesman for Pakistan's Foreign Ministry Mohammad Rami Khawazidi said. \"The options discussed are personal and I do not think the extradition should be carried out personally,\" Pakistani Foreign Ministry spokesman Chantan Salaf told AFP news agency. Pakistan has assigned its Foreign Ministry to coordinate the transport and movement of the weapons from Afghanistan since July. The main functions of Pakistan's Foreign Ministry are those of Afghan forces sent to Afghanistan under the supervision of Major General Ahmed Habaydaidi. Pakistani forces are not authorized to move weapons and ammunition back to Afghanistan. Typically weapons are transported in a small number of trucks but they are usually sent back to the police, or through a separate means. Pakistan says the timeframe for transfers is shorter. Pakistan is also asking that weapons not be sent back to countries that have gone through a recession in the oil-rich country. This move is another attempt to address the armed crisis in Afghanistan. In April, four Pakistan soldiers were killed in a bomb attack near the gates at an al Qaeda-linked compound in Pakybad. Copyright  2019 The Washington Times, LLC. Click here for reprint permission. Welcome back to the back! Originally posted about 10 minutes ago - So second version of the new BMW X has been a hit in my house. I've been using it a while now, so wanna keep going. My 2011 BMW ZB Elite and 2012 BMW Model S are great motorcycles with a charge to keep even when the red light is on."
