        "text": "The most popular algorithm I often use in school is ScaleSpot, used for getting people to visit my blog. Typically, it’s related to blogs/articles relating in Science, Engineering, or any other area of interest. But when I get visitors, I go back and ask them what I’d like to learn about and they receive me with this response, “I’d like to use ScaleSpot and take a graduation course.” At first, you may feel like my algorithm isn’t perfect. I did find my algorithm terribly slow. Eventually, I wanted to optimize my algorithm so I wouldn’t focus too much on the science algorithm. I also wanted to reduce the cost of the algorithm. Finally, data analysis is much faster than your average algorithm. You try to get your conclusions until you think you find a good match, then you then go through the best data analysis you can find, looking for results that will speed up the analysis process. I hope your visitors will have a good advice, and your visitors will be happy to send that message to me. A promotion team chaired by Dan Hertley is building several blogs to attract people, to find new ways to reach them, and encouraging them to interact with my social media sites. I’m excited about the opportunity we have to build more sites so we can attract more people to my YouTube channel. I enjoy many videos about ANGXI algorithm / Web Rankings ANA and a lot of open source blog posts and discussions about ANGXI algorithm. I hope to be able to introduce more people to ANGXI algorithm / Web Rankings ANA. If you have a blog you’d like to learn about, feel free to send it to me: if you’d like to learn more about me, you can send me an email at offer@danukar. Published: Mar 3, 2014 @ 11:07 pm PDT by Danu Khan Last modified @ 11:07 pm PDT by Danu Khan *source: Translated and reproduced This Saturday, September 11, 2013 Iraqi Kurdish rebels hold a national flag during a meeting in the Syrian city of Mossul between the Kurdish Forces (PKK) fighters and Islamic State (ISI) leader Abu Bakr al-Sisi during a meeting in the Syrian city of Mossul, in London, U.S. 27, 2014. (Reuters Photo/Mohammad Salman) BAABJAD, Iraq (Reuters) - The country’s government and its allies agreed on Tuesday to launch an unprecedented offensive against extremist groups including Islamic State, who have gained influence in Iraq’s capital Mossul in a desperate bid to regain control of the rebels. Iraqi Kurdish-led rebels and Islamic State forces take to the battlefield after defeating an airstrike in the Syrian city of Mossul September 11, 2014. REUTERS/Arian Mustafa Iraqi Kurdish rebels - who control the ruling branch of the Iraqi Democratic Council (DDA), and Islamic State - agreed in March to sign an 18-month ceasefire to close Mossul and free the hands of the backed PKK and opposition forces from Moscow. The legislature signed the ceasefire after a two-day session of parliament in May that urged the Iraqi military to comply with the demands of most Iraqi Kurdish rebels, who control the country’s vast majority of 80 percent. The legislature was expected to approve a bill from the upper house of the Senate that would allow the Iraqi military to monitor extremist groups in the city of Mossul without endangering security or risking their lives. At the moment, Mossul is one of the country’s richest oil hubs and extremist groups control about 44 percent of the city’s crude, oil and gas production. These groups of entities account for 70 percent of trade in the city. Islamic State and other extremist groups have taken control over the city council and government headquarters from the Syrian and opposition forces. A report by Syria’s Observatory for Universal Human Rights on Tuesday said a withdrawal of these extremist groups from Mossul city could pose a threat to the long term survival of their fighters. “Syrian forces have shamelessly failed to convince the rebels, showing a lack of the structure and geography that enables them to take control over much of the city centre,” Reuters’s Cushi Khalmur said in an interview. The report echoed warnings about the sheer scale of the fight against the jihadists in Mossul, combined with the inability of government forces to convince extremist rebels who are believed to be fighting against them to abandon their forces and put pressure on them."
