        "text": "\"We look forward to working closely with our local agencies and the Los Angeles Foundation to bring awareness of Richard Miller's death,\" said Brendan O'Sullivan, spokesman for Los Angeles Fire and Rescue Services. \"Our thoughts and sympathies are with Richard's family, who knew him well throughout his life, and wish him well, but we will never forget this legendary figure.\" The memorial will be held Tuesday on the south side of I-5 until the end of Sept. 1, to mark the 75th anniversary of Miller, who died in 1959. It is scheduled to begin Monday morning at 7:30 a.m. and run until 6:45 p.m. Tuesday. The city's veterans and the military played such a critical role in Miller's death, said Lt. John P. Lash, who lives in the southbound area where the memorial was held. \"The veterans had an enormous, significant influence in the community,\" he said. United spent about $40 million last year to fund the memorial, which includes $10 million for two music halls, and $2 million for a wide range of programs, including housing, education, health care and art programs. City officials, politicians, law enforcement officers and others traveled around the street gave speeches, held demonstrations, gave speeches and  expressed their full sympathies for United. The memorial, which includes a auditorium and music hall, was installed after Miller's death and has been a historic site for nearly 75 years. Gov. Jerry Ricky erected the memorial last year under the moniker \"memory Day.\" Tuesday also marks a unifying day for the United Foundation in California, which is among many of the nation's most influential institutions. \"It's a historic day that we recognize those who stood up for our city through their service and who made a difference in our community,\" said Scott Ericsen, United's executive director. \"It's important that some of the veterans are not just people who served well and well in the community, as they sat down here, but who facilitated the city's economic growth and prosperity.\" The foundation spent $92 million annually on a wide range of programs, including an \"American Health\" program given to more than 49,000 Americans from 1949 to 2002, according to the California Department of Health. Other programs include an \"inter-racial identity\" program called \"sub-color bandha,\" and other programs like a \"self-recognitive fitness exercise\" called \"same walking.\" The foundation also spent about $3 million last year on programs that included emergency medical assistance, classes for people with post-preterm sick symptoms and classes for ambulance patients, which were donated to health care centers throughout the city. \"It is an important step in the right direction for our community and making sure Los Angeles has a community of people who will always do as much as we can to honor the life and legacy of Richard Miller, who made a difference to the city, our family and to our community,\" said Andrew Robson, president of United of Los Angeles. The foundation, which has about 300 members on both sides of I-5 in Los Angeles, maintains a music and art museum and research center in California, as well as several commercial buildings throughout the city. The memorial includes other buildings from California, including the St. George Family Museum, the Freedom Memorial, and the Museum of America. The memorial was put in place in 1959 by author Hugh Gleason and actor David Riverzan. \"In terms of the foundation,\" said Robson, \"it's really something that really honors people's contributions to the city.\" The foundation's membership is felt at campuses in San Julio Flats, San Francisco Bay and San Jose, museums and art centers throughout the city, as well as research sites and medical facilities. Sen. Jerry Graves of San Jose said the memorial is an important step toward serving its community. \"This street is a place where people can see the past,\" he said. \"We hope to turn it into a learning center where people can explore the recent past.\" PATAGO, Va. - An inspection of a Pasadena apartment began Thursday after finding the exterior roof was empty and most landlords didn't like or agree with plans to revamp the apartments there. \"Gov. Scott Bush said the apartment was a business,\" a Pasadena business manager wrote in an email to a ticket buyer: Top story: Lots of about renting to stay in Va.-Stuff Pasadena because Gov. Scott Bush said this morning that Thursday's apartment was a business. pic.twitter.com/DGG1xR6nV — Ryan Scott (@renwrickson) November 24, 2017 ADVERTISEMENT:"
