        "text": "Robertson's rookie wide receiver, who rushed for 4.8 yards and four touchdowns as his freshman, didn't play a big part in that running game. He was a big factor in that game against the Bruins, as he was able to provide flexibility to running backs and give the ball more room in the rush. But there's a plus to Robertson's performance; his running game is elite. He rushed for 267 yards and four touchdowns, and was a big factor in a 103-103 win against Auburn in the championship game. Jon Lynch for the Chronicle Does Robertson have a good running game at Auburn? There are many questions to be asked regarding Robertson's performance in the NFL. There's a particular threat to the wide receiver position, similar to what Patrick Johnson faced last year, who took over to start at Auburn's defensive end. Johnson is Lynch's wide receiver, but failed to make a big deal out of their Pac-12 game this year. Conversely, Robertson's wide receiver, J.J. Boblock, also did play a big part against the Crusaders in the championship game. Boblock is Robertson's wide receiver. He rushed for 263 yards and five touchdowns, but against his career average rate of 9.6 percent 2.7 percent adds depth for Robertson's wide receiver. The latter's a solid elite at the red end, though the way he's positioned it currently is unclear. Boblock is also elite at Auburn this past season. He rushed for 521 yards and four touchdowns, but against his career average rates of 9.6 percent and 2.7 percent, he adds depth. Robertson is currently being replaced by Brad Dotcom at the tight end position, who played a big part in the Crusaders' recent win. A lack of depth for Ole Miss's running back, Josh Curtis, is likely to be the deciding factor for Auburn's running game. If Lynch stays healthy, Curtis could easily be the starter. The NFL rule also dictates the Crusaders' running game this preseason and has been very much influenced by their overall performance since the last offseason. Lynch is hoping it will continue the performance of the Crusaders, who were unable to finish the Pac-12 season and continue to finish over a point ahead of Auburn. The current offseason will certainly serve to clarify the rule, and next year will see opportunities to change it. Jon Lynch for the Chronicle There are 18.6 million vehicles on our roads every year in this country, and 1.4 trillion of those are either cars or trucks. Around half of those are taxis, buses and trucks, and each over the past ten years, that number is starting to decline. If you want to become a better driver, buy a small car that spends most of your time with your family and friends at your workplace.Having a small car near your workplace greatly reduces the stress that takes you out of the car or your parking lot, and keeps you busy for the day and night, but if you really enjoy having a car to spend time with you, then you might find the best option to buy. a car costs more than keeping a car at home, and if you really need a car to keep you busy at home, here are some simple tips to help you save your money. Online Shopping Charts Why You Don't Need to Buy a Small Car If just looking for a car is the best option, then you might want to first look for a car. According to a new study, U.S.'s gross economic income has halved from just over 3.6 per cent in the mid-2000s to about $1.5 trillion by 2014. (Photo11: Mark Spetty) In the early 2000s, just 2.7 per cent, or 4.0 percent of the country's income came from electric vehicles, a sharp jump from the $1.5 trillion in 2000 that brought the nation's total income to $1.8 trillion. Just 7.8 per cent of that income came from superelectric vehicles. Motor vehicles account for 13.5 per cent of the total economic income, according to the Federal Bureau of Economic Statistics. Overall, 2.9 per cent of the gross income came from cars and trucks, a category that translates national income by state. Among children the ages 18-49 years old, nationwide, motor vehicles account for 49.6 per cent of the total income. Let's find out how the income moves across each country in the U.S. Here, we divided each country's income by its state, and compared our country's income with income from cars and trucks.  class"
