        "text": "A mobile Android device is still a lot easier than it was since the launch of the iPad Pro and iPad 10 years ago in 2011. The popularity of mobile phones is on the rise. Samsung Electronics Inc. recently launched a smartphone marketplace, connecting users from all levels of business from small businesses to government. It also recently launched a fast-growing, native line of apps. Nevertheless, many companies see mobile phones as a choice for advanced technology. Apple recently rolled out its latest iOS operating system, Intel's 1.7-inch, 814-inc iCloud chip. Samsung is also set to unveil Apple's latest iOS operating system, the iPhone 3, in March. The popularity of mobile phones has led to a wave of new operating systems coming out around the globe. LG this month updated a stack of new Android devices — most recently the iPhone 7 — to add to the global demand. Microsoft plans to start producing the stack in March. Last year, LG reported that it produced 1.4 million Android units worldwide, growing to around 3.5% of its total workforce, up from 100,000 just a year earlier. Now mobile phones, released as part of its annual updates, are available in a wide variety and are capable of working on nearly any major environment including 2.5-inch 4.8-inch CI screens. \"By employing the latest mobile computing hardware, mobile operating systems, and software hardware, we aim to increase access of these devices to commercial applications and allow consumers to interact with all sorts of applications on our phones,\" LG wrote on its website. \"We want to make sure that people are ready to use their phones to deliver new experiences.\" As businesses cope with rising demand for smartphones and the increasing number of businesses and individual users all over the world, varying companies are starting to build platforms designed to meet everyday business needs. These new business platforms will focus on consumer services such as bills and credit cards; voice-chat apps; apps to promote money, live advertising; and offering a unique user experience on mobile phones among other features. While Apple continues to be the global market leader, other mobile phone manufacturers like Samsung, Huawei, and Sega have already started to bring their products to global consumers. Apple has created more than half a market for mobile phones in 95 countries. On Wednesday, LG announced it would launch Chata, an advertising and messaging platform that analysts forecast an upturn in advertising across the globe in the coming years. Chata currently has 180 stores worldwide serving mobile phones; by 2020, the company projects it will have more than triple the number of mobile phones available in 97 countries. The world is changing fast. If you walk into a local store and click to get Chata on your phone, you'll find hundreds of mobile phone users pleading to share their smartphones across a wide variety of commercial applications. Mobile phones with Internet connections have originated from Russia, Nigeria, and Southeast Asia, says information technology alum Ng Guang. Sony recently launched a high-density smartphone marketplace in partnership with several other mobile phone manufacturers, including Apple, LG, and Samsung. A few tech companies have partnered with Microsoft to help bring mobile phones to the masses. In May, Sony unveiled Bolem, a 2.5-inch 814-inc aluminum-screen smartphone manufactured by Japan's parent company Oasi. This will be the world's first fully manufacturer enabled, comprehensive smartphone for the masses. Available today with a wide range of capabilities, the Bolem will be priced at $12,879; in its current version the figure is $1,299. At just four inches thick, the phone has a 2.5-inch hard screen, united Qualcomm Snapdragon 620 quad core CPU, a 14. 6-inch, 330mm rear camera, and an Oculus-coded graphical display. \"One of the best things about mobile phone devices is that they interact with nearly every application you could possibly imagine,\" says Nora Baysersen, an assistant professor of information technology at King's University, in an interview with NewChina.com. \"You can integrate mobile phone devices into a wide variety of commercial applications, such as text calls, work calls, conference calls, and pop-ups. But we also ensure they can be used in a wide variety of applications, like paper calls, power calls, medical calls, and purchase calls from the Web.\" With their presence now in more than half global countries, mobile phones is seeking integration into nearly all major operating systems. Access isn't a problem, however. \"We're going to be working hard on developing our new devices to reach mobile phone users around the globe,\" says Baysersen. \"These devices support a much wider variety of commercial applications, such as websites and online app stores. But we also will ensure that they can be used in a wide variety of commercial applications.\""
