        "sourceId": 559,
        "text": "The Senate Intelligence Panel, which led the investigation into Mueller, received a memo announcing Mueller’s resignation at the end of January, the same day the panel approved his resignation. The Jan. 29 memo reads, “The office of the special attorney general will review the call for the FBI to conduct a special investigation into the Secretary’s alleged ties with foreign leaders during the campaign.” The memo was followed by a statement from Chuck Gramer, the head of the Senate Intelligence Panel, which recommended that the FBI hire a special counsel doing an investigation into the presidential nominee’s alleged contacts with foreign leaders during the campaign. FBI acting director Bruce Strauss, who is backing Mueller’s campaign, has said he is confident the FBI will conduct a very special investigation. “Director Mueller has not yet responded to requests from the FBI,” he said. “Whether such he could simply resign from his post remains to be seen.” Sen. John McCain (R-Tenn.) has been calling for a special attorney general to examine Mueller’s relations with classified email users, but has taken a skeptical position. “The Secretary is still being investigated, and I don’t think anyone should conduct any such investigation, unless he does,” McCain said. “How do you think the special investigation should be done? I don’t think it would be part of a very special investigation, which is another question.”Gramer, also close to Clinton, said she supports calling for the person in charge of the State Department to lead an investigation into classified emails. “I’m not sure if this is going to be investigated or not,”Gramer said. “I know what I’ve heard of from the FBI. There have been no encounters with the special attorney general himself, and that’s all I know,” she said. Meanwhile,Gramer sent a letter to then-Deputy Attorney General Elliot Schumer earlier this year calling for the FBI to conduct an investigation into classified emails. The letter contained a text fromGramer published by McCaffney & Schumer that called for the State Department’s top counsel — the special attorney general — to lead the investigation into the State Department’s presidential nominee’s alleged contacts with foreign leaders during the campaign. “I strongly believe that an investigation into Secretary Clinton’s classified email activities is antithetical to bringing forward an investigation into the Secretary’s ties with foreign leaders,” said the letter, which reportedly contained in paragraphs. “An investigation into Secretary Clinton’s alleged contacts with privileged email users... is the appropriate answer to this question.” After his disclosures to the FBI in June, Mueller received a formal inquiry from the Senate Intelligence Panel about contacts with foreign leaders during the campaign, which the panel did not consider. The report was released on June 23 at around the same time that Sen. Ted Cruz (R-Texas) announced his support for the investigation in a meeting with other Senate leaders. Sen. John McCain (R-Ky.) also called for the FBI to lead the investigation, but he did not. “It has a huge impact on American democracy,” Sen. Al Franken said in a statement. “It has a huge impact on America’s foreign policy.” Intelligence leaders on Tuesday released a brief statement from Jeff Sessions that alleged he soughtd advice from the FBI during his time as secretary of state. The groups also released a brief statement that alleged he met with various individuals associated with Russians during the 2016 election. Separately, Sen. Lindsey Graham (D-N.Y.) put out a similar statement on Tuesday, saying, “Choseman General Mueller should have told his appointees that he did nothing during his time as a secretary of state to seek advice from the FBI.” Sessions, who held annual meetings with General Mueller, told NBC’s World View Center on Tuesday, though he should still ask the special attorney general to look. “I don’t think I should look at the interaction between the secretary of state and the Intelligence community,” Sessions said. “Anything that happened to Mueller while he was at, I don’t know where that special attorney general was going to be at. What I am going to be concerned about is how the government is going to conduct an investigation into Mueller’s role of special attorney general: What are his positions? What are his views?” The head of Tahrir Democratic Forces, one of the most widely outlawed groups in Syria, has been handed to the US as part of a deal to try to overthrow Syrian President Bashar al-Assad regime."
