        "id": "s-b05dca292f2dd3b4bb18388b",
        "sourceId": 713,
        "text": "On Monday, Obama signed a warrant allowing his agency to eavesdrop on a slew of surveillance programs, in which up to 90 percent of the agency's employees are still eligible for legal action. \"They just don't want information to pass through our country,\" he said during a phone call with Sen. Richard S. Feinstein (D-Colo.), as reported by Fox News's \"Fox News Morning.\" The Environmental Protection Agency, which sits in the cabinet of President Trump, has played an active role in examining federal agents' past data restoration orders. EPA Director James Comey told Breitbart earlier this month that he had discovered federal agent hand-overs of information through the U.S. Department of Justice's Office of Management in Washington, the entity protected by the Affordable Care Act. That indicated that agents were eligible for federal data restoration warrants, according to an administration official, who spoke only on the condition of anonymity. He said it's unclear where the information came from, but the agency is doing a very careful evaluation of potentially restored information before releasing it to the public. He told Fox News that some of the information passing through the EPA's Office of Management appeared to be linked to past data restoration orders under federal law. \"When the EPA is doing a careful evaluation of this information, everybody knows when they're going to be hand over information about the Obama administration, as well as information about American citizens who think the information is inappropriate,\" he said. Monday's revelation is the latest in a string of attempts to compel federal agents into serving data restoration warrants. Critics say it violates the U.S. counterterrorism law, which prevents federal agents from being required to disclose how they're treating illegal immigrants when holding suspected criminal aliens. Under the law, U.S. attorney agents are required to perform a 15-hour custody warrant when detention illegal immigrants. Opponents have spoken out against the agency's counterterror and immigration policies. The president, Sen. Chuck Schumer (R-Va.), said the EPA's use of such policies is unconstitutional. The current law requires federal agents to hold suspected foreign terrorists — mostly foreign nationals, but not convicted of a crime — in the country's detention facilities. They're not required to disclose any information about terrorists. When EPA says we can't disclose any information about a terrorist, we ought to be enforcing law! — Donald Trump (@realDonaldTrump) June 21, 2017 Schumer is also just one of several presidents who have warned that EPA handing over data restoration orders will harm the U.S. economy. Top federal employees, including federal agents, account for more than two-thirds of the $3.6 billion in the agency's budget each year, according to a report released in January by market research firm Atris International, as well as the Environmental Protection Agency, the University of California, and the National Science Foundation. Luke Thomas is a vice-governatorial and former Washington reporter for Breitbart. You can contact him at [email protected]. Follow Tesser Bo on Twitter. A powerful constitutional court in Cuba decided Wednesday to temporarily amend Cuba's constitution, known as the dictatorship's constitution. To date, at least 100,000 cases have been registered in support of the amendment, which demands for a possible change to the country's constitution. Fidel Castro said in a statement on Monday that it would also be necessary to protect the beleaguered country's current constitutional structure. In a statement on Monday, Cuba Attorney General Angela Gucadala said the decision had been made only on a temporary basis, and that more than 100,000 legal cases had been registered. Aside from protecting the current constitution, she added that the court's decision mainly honors the Treaty of the Union, which came into effect last year after demanding a change to constitution. \"The Treaty of the Union preserves the constitutionality of the constitution and ensures the participation of states in all jurisdictions. However, as long as states are able to amend the treaty or participate, it remains a foundation for protecting the country's constitution,\" she said. Fidel Castro Ali Rodriguez, executive director of the Cuba Democratic Party, said in the statement that the court's decision was \"absolutely unacceptable,\" but suggested that states were not obliged to participate. \"It is an act of oppression. We have to preserve our constitution, as it has to be amended and replaced,\" he said, vaguely adding that Fidel Castro's dictatorship had entered \"a dark age.\""
