        "id": "s-8d0c7e9002ac9c3d47a16600",
        "sourceId": 829,
        "text": "GW: There’s some character aspects that I’m wanting to know more about this season. I’m a very big fan of the series and it’s basically the protagonist’s journey across Europe to Asia, so it will really appease me. There’s a lot of books to read, so I’m really hoping that each episode will tell us more about the protagonists. Also, there’s a nice ending to each of these episodes, which actually gets me a bit deeper into the story. There’s also a nice scene of life in Japan written by the Mother of the Hunters, following in the footsteps of the first episode of the series. There are a ton of books on the show that tell the story, and a lot of books that are really passionate about it. Japan Prison is a very nuanced kind of book, but that’s actually written by people more often than ever before. For me, Above the Grubes is a great episode, with the focus really going on the dangers of Japanese travel. As Michelle takes a walk through the interior of Japan Prison, Michelle reveals she’s one of the most important people in the game, and she teaches about the connection between love, work and life. Michelle reveals that life can be different than you could imagine, and she tries to persuase herself to the dangers of traveling hundreds of miles around the world. ACOM: What TV miniseries are you looking forward to for this course of the season? GW: I love the scene as she leaves Japan. Also, I love the way Michelle comes in and the way she learns about Japanese cults. The reason she loves to travel and the fact that she stays with her throughout her journey. ACOM: What would you like to watch HBO’s The Mother of the Hunters for this season? Have you read any? Can you tell us about other miniseries that you’d like to watch throughout the season? GW: I love all the books that I read. It’s great to watch some books from the previous most recent season of the show. These books really appease me in so many ways, they remind me of the characters on the show. When I screened The Mother of the Hunters with Carol Crosby as part of her documentary screening, I couldn’t help more than to expect it to appease me. I really wanted it to be one of my most beloved stories of the season. I also felt like it was my favorite story. There is a lot of anime, but there’s also TV, so each episode pulls me in a new direction. Whether it’s an anime or more of a TV series, there is a really wide variety of sitcoms and spin-offs, and a lot of them are inspired by the work of such people as Jim Thompson, Catherine Timberrow, Bill Coats, Ben Fieldhead and many others. There’s also a lot of miniseries, which means it will be fun to have a look at some of them. Injustice, which we discussed with the book during the screening, is also the favorite aspect of the season. For me, one of my favorite stories is Star Trek. It isn’t just Star Trek as I’ll tell you, but it is a multi-capscule adventure centered around the character of Scott Klax. It really is a love story, where Scott is in the role of a sailor and a spiritual leader. Some of the most favorite aspects of the season are the movies. As a side note, there are many movies that make me cry because they’re so beautifully animated and fun to watch. The best of these is Doombuster, which had me a freaking. It may sound like a terrible movie with terrible plot, but it is full of characters who fight for a legitimate cause and spend most of their lives in isolation on the landscape of the world. The animated comedy Teen Girl was a great throw-in for the season, and really did bring texture and humor to the action and also to the plot. myself and the show creators, Joe McCall, put together a book for each episode, then sent it to a fan, and decided that it was going to tell the story of the War on the Moon and share the story with some legendary guerrillas and political leaders in the dark parts of the world. So, to give the story a go for the viewer, I had to write a season that told the story of the Underworld’s military invasions and terrible genocide all around the world, to leverage emotions that still echo in my mind when it comes to the terrible genocide we are facing today."
