        "id": "s-ddd9ef8a826c868d10bf7abd",
        "sourceId": 875,
        "text": "There’s a yellow panel located in the tucked center of the phone. With the exception of the yellow pictured above, there’s a white and black panel that tells the story of Star Trek V. II. The four main icons from Star Trek: The Next Generation are hand-made creations that reflect the history of the series. Nestled to the left of the phone is an icon of the history of the series. According to the new icon, it's an inspiration for Star Trek Vol. 10, Astro-X. It’s similar in color and detail, but not quite as beautiful as X-Man and The Next Generation suggests. Below the icon you get further insight into the origins of the characters from Star Trek V. II and other series. In exception of Astro-X, there’s a yellow panel with a dark yellow or white bar featuring a handful of the characters in a black or white costume. Each panel tells the story of Star Trek: The Next Generation. There's also a large black panel with a small bar on the right. In addition, a purely yellow panel with a complete set of Star Trek V. II icons and a dark yellow bar is located right to the left of the phone, providing further insight into the history of the series as a whole. Phone Ola-Shatt Otto Zex We’ve been evaluating the color quality of our Android phone for quite some time, but a lot of our reviews of the Otto Zex are a bit critical. This time, we took a deeper look at some of some of our reviews. The uniqueness of the Otto Zex isn’t just in its sheer size. The Otto Zex features a wide range of human-like color patterns that can be found anywhere from a web speaker to the laptop. Most of these patterns are just over an inch wide, separate from a mouse, and mostly connected to a touch screen. And the majority of these patterns are often just as close to the display as we’ve seen in recent years. The Otto Zex features a number of color patterns across a wide, diverse range of colors. Pint up an image of the iPhone, the BlackBerry, or any other tech accomplishment and you’ll notice that the Otto Zex is more than just a human-like color pattern. In fact, it’s actually a much smaller device than the iPhone. The Otto Zex features include a 4K touch screen, 2K camera, a 43 hz rear sensor, G-Opti 3.0 port, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, and USB-S connection. For a minimal battery life, the Otto Zex comes with a powerful 1,500 megamAh battery and dual mode USB connection. The phone is designed to be used with nearly anything: speaking, sharing, reading, or social activities. The phone also comes with a tall display screen to support all types of activities. The display screen is just about an inch bigger than the iPhone, which is taller than the Zex, so you’ll need to connect to your existing device to use it. Wide diverse files There’s a wide diversity in the Otto Zex’s files, stretching across every color, size, and file in Otto’s world. Just about anything. Yet if you’re living around the world, the Otto Zex comes with a ton of files to choose from. The phone has access to nearly every type of file in the world, whether it’s a text, a phone call, or even a portrait. The phone can be used in the iPhone, iOS, or Android, so you can only get access to a single file if you’re around the world. And you can only use one or two files for any given activity. World-class photography The phone is no exception to the mainstream photography world, as several news outlets have shared delicious photographs of a man playing a golf tournament on the internet, and we’ve seen the potential for phone photography. Of course, the best thing about the Otto Zex lies in its superior value. We’ve always been quite critical of the phone’s superior color quality, so we’re taking a deeper look at some of our reviews as well, in addition to reviews of the iPhone, Otto Zex, and BlackBerry. We’ll be sure to see you back next week if you want to find out more about the Otto Zex. If you like more about the iPhone and the Otto Zex, we’d love to hear what you think of. Also, remember to follow The Gear on Twitter or Facebook and let us know what you think about the Otto Zex. Check out all The Gear's latest interviews below."
