        "id": "s-52f3e806d5c40b46e7450d2f",
        "sourceId": 879,
        "text": "Borandas said Biomes will add Super Mario Mario Bros’ latest game, Super Mario Mario Bros, to help develop the concept of creating fun race card games. Biomes will ultimately include smaller versions of the Super Mario Bros franchise, such as a MMO version. He said Biomes will introduce MMOs to allow gamers to control their electric vehicles and to improve driving experiences for the player. Borandas said there are many other race card games that Biomes — including Super Mario Bros 4 and Super Mario Bros Super Super — build on. “The most important factor, facing us now, is player identity,” he said. Biomes is making a remarkable leap by representing globally the Canadian entertainment market in a bid to make consumer cards included in race games, Borandas said. Biomes is making an remarkable leap by representing the race-card market. “It is a step in the right direction for our card business. Because we call it a next-generation cameras game, we’re doing that,” Borandas said. K.L.A. Joints With Entertainment Digital Group, Ltd. Biomes Entertainment and Biomes Entertainment are a joint venture of the Company of Digital. Biomes Entertainment distributes animated race-card games to gamers of all levels. Biomes spokesman Ken Deethe said Entertainment and Biomes were not involved in card creation or the development process. “We are a partnership and that’s what Biomes is all about. That’s what Biomes Entertainment is all about,” Borandas said. “We’re not involved in the development process.” A designer builds a tower at a property development site in Humphrey, northeast Burlington, Thursday, June 3, 2017.Buy Photo A designer builds a new tower at a property development site in Humphrey, northeast Burlington, Thursday, June 3, 2017. (Photo: David Burkesman / UNITED PRESS )Buy Photo A plan to build a luxurious apartment tower in the center of the city of downtown has received wide backing from residents and community leaders. The idea was proposed over the weekend by Christi P. Martin, the city's planning and expansion commissioner. The construction of the tower would attract visitors and businesses, according to city Mayor Stevin Geelow, who said the development would attract vacationers and tourists who live in the area. The plan calls for an expansion of the downtown space. Geelow said the city council should convert the development into a single building with a space for the hotels, nightclubs, cafes and restaurants, and adapt the center to fit its needs. \"It's like another new office building, it's new space, and we're interested in the simplicity and openness of downtown,\" Geelow said. \"There will be lots of places for people to set up in downtown.\" Asked whether he favors a plan like the tower convert, he said the idea has received widespread support and has not been given any media attention. \"It's a great idea,\" he said during an interview. \"And what I'm looking for is for the media and the public to congratulat me on what I did.\" Charles Pence, a city Democrat who proposed the plan, said the project has received a \"total of attention.\" \"But we're still trying to figure out what's going on on the ground and what's going to go forward,\" Pence said. \"It is a project that can help property owners and their citizens.\" Jon Hubbard, another Democrat in the Burlington Assembly, said he supported the plan. \"It has a lot of impact on all of our communities and our businesses, from the site to the hotel development to the restaurant development,\" Martin said during an interview. Other elements of the $50-million plan include an upgrade to the former downtown building of Six Members 6, eschewing the one-foot-tall wooden tower. Hubbard said he wants the tower, which's being used as a main elevator, to be preserved. He said he hopes there is a plan being developed to monetize the project. \"That's what it's about,\" he said. Alongside the tower, the project includes a public library, a zoo and dance facility for adults, a nightclub facility for adults and juniors, a swimming pool, a kids's library, an all-girl basketball court, a downtown hockey court and an outdoor dining and entertainment space for kids and teenagers. \"This is an absolutely ambitious development that will bring great benefits to the downtown community,\" Martin said. \"Everything related to this project is a great step forward.\""
